// Generated by content/build_cards.py — 1098 cards. Fields: cat(L/B), stat(w/d/W), author, book, insight, question, answer, action
const RAW_CARDS=[["L","d","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life","Life is not short; we waste much of it. We guard our money carefully but hand out our hours to anyone who asks. Treat time as your scarcest asset.","According to Seneca, why does life feel short?","Because we waste it, guarding money but giving time away freely.","Before saying yes to any request today, ask: is this worth an hour of my life?"],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Rehearse misfortune in advance. Spend a few minutes imagining loss or hardship, and you will find it less frightening and appreciate what you have more.","What is the purpose of 'premeditating adversity'?","To reduce fear of misfortune and increase gratitude for the present.","Spend 2 minutes imagining losing something you take for granted, then appreciate it."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","We suffer more in imagination than in reality. Most of what we fear never happens, and what does happen is rarely as bad as the dread before it.","What does Seneca say we suffer more from?","Imagination — anticipated troubles rather than real ones.","Write down one worry and label it: happening now, or only imagined?"],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Read deeply in a few great authors rather than skimming many. Scattered reading is like constant travel: you meet many hosts but make no friends.","Why does Seneca warn against reading too widely?","Scattered reading leaves nothing rooted; depth in a few authors builds real understanding.","Pick one book and reread a chapter instead of starting something new."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Each day, take one idea from your reading and digest it. A single thought lived with all day does more than a hundred skimmed.","What practice does Seneca recommend after each day's reading?","Select one idea to digest and carry through the day.","Choose one sentence from today's session and repeat it to yourself at lunch."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Practise poverty occasionally: live a few days on plain food and rough clothing. You will discover that what you feared losing is easily endured.","What does Seneca suggest to lose the fear of poverty?","Periodically live simply on purpose and see that it's bearable.","Have a deliberately simple, cheap meal today and notice that you are fine."],["L","d","Seneca","On Anger","Anger is a brief madness. The best cure is delay: let the first wave pass before you speak or act, because almost nothing done in anger is done well.","What is Seneca's best remedy for anger?","Delay — wait before acting on it.","When irritated today, wait ten breaths before responding."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Associate with people who will make you better, and welcome those you can make better. Character is contagious in both directions.","Whom does Seneca advise us to spend time with?","Those who improve us and those we can improve.","Message someone who brings out your best and set up a meeting."],["L","d","Seneca","On the Happy Life","Fortune gives and takes. Hold wealth, status and comfort as things on loan, ready to return them without bitterness.","How should we hold external goods, per Seneca?","As loans from fortune, ready to give them back.","Look at a possession you value and say: 'This is on loan.'"],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","'Luck is what happens when preparation meets opportunity.' Do the quiet work now so you can seize chances when they appear.","What turns opportunity into luck?","Preparation done in advance.","Spend 15 minutes preparing for an opportunity you hope will come."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Each morning, expect to meet difficult people: the ungrateful, the arrogant, the dishonest. Expecting them removes the shock, and remembering they act from ignorance removes the anger.","What does Marcus prepare himself for each morning?","Meeting difficult people — so he is neither shocked nor angered.","This morning, predict one difficult interaction and decide your calm response."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","'You have power over your mind — not outside events. Realize this, and you will find strength.' Your judgments, not events, disturb you.","According to Marcus, what actually disturbs us?","Our judgments about events, not the events themselves.","When upset today, ask: what story am I telling myself about this?"],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","'The impediment to action advances action. What stands in the way becomes the way.' Every obstacle is material to practise a virtue on.","What did Marcus say about obstacles?","The obstacle becomes the way — a chance to practise virtue.","Name today's biggest obstacle and the virtue it lets you practise."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Do every act as if it were the last of your life: without carelessness, pretence or complaint. This gives ordinary tasks dignity and focus.","How does Marcus advise performing each action?","As if it were your last — fully, without pretence or complaint.","Do one routine task today with full attention, as if it mattered most."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Picture the view from above: cities, empires, and lifetimes as tiny and brief. Your problems shrink to their true size.","What is 'the view from above'?","Imagining the vast scale of the world to put problems in perspective.","Close your eyes for a minute and zoom out from your room to the planet."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","'Waste no more time arguing what a good man should be. Be one.' Theory without practice is decoration.","What does Marcus tell himself instead of debating virtue?","Stop arguing about what a good person is — be one.","Replace one opinion you'd share today with one good action."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","The best revenge is not to be like the one who wronged you. Answer rudeness with steadiness, not imitation.","What is the best revenge, according to Marcus?","Not becoming like the person who wronged you.","If someone is rude today, deliberately respond with courtesy."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Everything is change. Fame, bodies and grievances are all flowing away like a river. Clinging to anything fixed is the root of distress.","Why should we not cling to things, says Marcus?","Everything is constantly changing and passing away.","Notice one thing you're clinging to and loosen your grip on it."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","When you want to cheer yourself up, think of the good qualities of the people around you: one's energy, another's modesty, another's generosity.","What does Marcus recommend for lifting your mood?","Reflect on the virtues of people around you.","List three people and one virtue each. Tell one of them."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Get up in the morning to do the work of a human being. You were not made to lie warm under the covers but to act and contribute.","What argument does Marcus use against staying in bed?","We were made to do human work, not for comfort.","Get up at the first alarm tomorrow — decide it tonight."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","If it is not right, do not do it; if it is not true, do not say it. Two simple rules cover most moral confusion.","What are Marcus's two quick rules?","If not right, don't do it; if not true, don't say it.","Catch and drop one small exaggeration in conversation today."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Retreat into yourself. No place is quieter than your own mind, available any moment for renewal.","Where does Marcus say the best retreat is?","Within your own mind.","Take a 3-minute mental retreat: eyes closed, slow breaths."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","Some things are up to us — our opinions, choices and efforts — and some are not: body, reputation, outcomes. Peace comes from focusing only on the first.","What is the dichotomy of control?","Distinguishing what's up to us (judgments, choices) from what isn't (outcomes, others).","Sort today's worries into two columns: in my control / not."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","People are disturbed not by things, but by their opinions about things. Change the opinion and the disturbance dissolves.","What disturbs people according to Epictetus?","Their opinions about things, not things themselves.","Reframe one frustration today in neutral words."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","If you want to improve, be content to appear foolish. Progress requires asking basic questions and being a beginner in public.","What must you accept to make progress, says Epictetus?","Looking foolish or uninformed.","Ask one 'stupid' question you've been avoiding."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","Wish things to happen as they actually happen, and your life will flow well. Fighting reality only adds a second wound.","What does Epictetus advise wishing for?","That things happen as they actually do.","When plans change today, say 'Good, this is what's happening' and adapt."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","When you lose something, say 'I have returned it' rather than 'I have lost it.' Everything is borrowed.","How should we speak of losses, according to Epictetus?","'I have returned it' — treating everything as borrowed.","Reframe one recent loss as something returned."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","We have two ears and one mouth so we can listen twice as much as we speak.","What does Epictetus infer from our ears and mouth?","Listen twice as much as you speak.","In your next conversation, ask questions and let others talk first."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","Don't explain your philosophy — embody it. At a banquet, don't lecture on how to eat; eat as one should.","What does Epictetus say to do instead of explaining principles?","Embody them through conduct.","Act on one principle today without mentioning it to anyone."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","No one is free who is not master of himself. Freedom is not having what you want but not being ruled by wants.","How does Epictetus define freedom?","Self-mastery — not being enslaved to desires.","Skip one small craving today on purpose."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","Any person capable of angering you becomes your master. Guard your reactions so others don't hold your controls.","What happens when someone can easily anger you?","They become your master.","Notice who 'pushes your buttons' and decide not to hand them control."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Between stimulus and response there is a space. In that space lies our freedom to choose our response, and in our response lies our growth.","What lies between stimulus and response?","A space containing our freedom to choose.","Pause one full breath before reacting to anything today."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Those with a 'why' to live can bear almost any 'how'. Meaning is the strongest source of resilience.","What allows people to endure almost any hardship?","Having a 'why' — a meaning or purpose.","Write one sentence: what is my 'why' this year?"],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Meaning is found in three ways: by creating work or doing deeds, by experiencing something or loving someone, and by the attitude we take to unavoidable suffering.","What are Frankl's three sources of meaning?","Work/deeds, love/experience, and attitude toward suffering.","Do one thing today in each category, however small."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Don't aim at success or happiness directly. They ensue as side effects of dedication to a cause greater than yourself.","How, per Frankl, do happiness and success come?","As side effects of devotion to something beyond ourselves.","Spend 20 minutes on something for someone else, not for yourself."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Everything can be taken from a person but one thing: the last of human freedoms — to choose one's attitude in any given circumstances.","What is the 'last of human freedoms'?","Choosing one's attitude in any circumstances.","In a frustrating moment today, consciously choose your attitude."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Live as if you were living for the second time and had acted as wrongly the first time as you are about to act now.","What is Frankl's 'second time' imperative?","Live as if you're living a second time and already made the mistake you're about to make.","Before one decision today, imagine you've already regretted the wrong choice."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","It is not what we expect from life, but what life expects from us. Life asks questions; we answer through responsible action.","How does Frankl reverse the question of meaning?","Ask what life expects from us, not what we expect from life.","Ask: what is life asking of me today? Do that thing."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Be proactive: focus on your circle of influence, not your circle of concern. Working on what you can affect makes that circle grow.","What's the difference between the circles of concern and influence?","Concern is all we care about; influence is what we can affect. Proactive people focus on influence.","Pick one concern and identify the one action within your influence."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Begin with the end in mind. Imagine your own funeral: what would you want people to say? Let that shape today's choices.","What exercise does Covey use for 'begin with the end in mind'?","Imagining your funeral and what you'd want said about you.","Write three words you'd want said about you, and act on one."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Put first things first. Important-but-not-urgent work (planning, health, relationships) is where effectiveness lives, yet it's always crowded out by urgency.","Which quadrant does Covey say matters most?","Important but not urgent (Quadrant II).","Block 30 minutes today for one important, non-urgent task."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Seek first to understand, then to be understood. Most people listen with the intent to reply; listen to grasp the other's view.","What is Covey's habit 5?","Seek first to understand, then to be understood.","In one conversation, summarise the other person's view before giving yours."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Think win-win. Treat life as cooperative, not a zero-sum contest. Look for solutions where both sides benefit, or make no deal.","What is the alternative Covey proposes if win-win isn't possible?","No deal.","In a negotiation or disagreement today, state what the other side needs too."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Make deposits in the emotional bank account: keep promises, show kindness, apologise sincerely. Trust is built in small deposits.","What builds the 'emotional bank account'?","Small acts: kept promises, kindness, sincere apologies.","Make one deliberate deposit with someone close today."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Sharpen the saw: regularly renew body, mind, heart and spirit. A woodcutter too busy to sharpen his saw works harder and achieves less.","What are the four dimensions of renewal?","Physical, mental, social/emotional, spiritual.","Do one small renewal act in each dimension today."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Synergise: the whole is greater than the sum of its parts. Value differences as sources of better ideas rather than threats.","What does Covey mean by synergy?","Valuing differences to create solutions better than either party alone.","Ask someone who thinks differently for their view on your problem."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","The Death of Ivan Ilyich","Ivan Ilyich lived a proper, comfortable, approved life and realised only at death it had been empty. A 'correct' life is not necessarily a true one.","What did Ivan Ilyich realise at the end?","His proper, conventional life had been empty of real meaning.","Ask: which of my habits do I keep only because it looks right?"],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Anna Karenina","'All happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.' Happiness rests on many basics done right; any single failure can undo it.","What does the 'Anna Karenina principle' suggest?","Success requires many factors done right; failure needs only one gone wrong.","Check one neglected basic in your relationships or health."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Three Questions","The most important time is now; the most important person is the one you are with; the most important task is to do them good.","What are the answers to Tolstoy's three questions?","Now; the person in front of you; doing them good.","Give the next person you speak with your complete attention."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","War and Peace","'The two most powerful warriors are patience and time.' Many battles are won by waiting rather than charging.","Which two 'warriors' does Kutuzov praise?","Patience and time.","Identify one situation where waiting is the better move, and wait."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","How Much Land Does a Man Need?","Pahom kept wanting more land until his greed killed him; in the end he needed only six feet. Endless wanting destroys contentment.","What is the lesson of 'How Much Land Does a Man Need?'","Greed for more is endless and destructive; we need far less than we think.","Name one thing you already have 'enough' of and stop seeking more."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","A Confession","Tolstoy found that wealth and fame did not answer 'why live?'. Simple people living by faith and work seemed to have the answer he lacked.","What did Tolstoy observe about simple working people?","They had a sense of meaning his wealth and fame couldn't provide.","Do some simple, physical, useful work today."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Writings","Everyone thinks of changing the world, but no one thinks of changing himself. Real reform starts at home.","What does Tolstoy say most people neglect?","Changing themselves rather than the world.","Pick one thing you criticise in others and fix it in yourself."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Know the enemy and know yourself, and you need not fear a hundred battles. Self-knowledge is half of every strategy.","What does Sun Tzu say ensures victory in a hundred battles?","Knowing both the enemy and yourself.","Write down your top strength and top weakness for a current challenge."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","The supreme art of war is to subdue the enemy without fighting. The best victories avoid conflict entirely.","What is the 'supreme art of war'?","Winning without fighting.","Resolve one tension today through a conversation instead of a confrontation."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Victorious warriors win first and then go to war; defeated warriors go to war first and then seek to win. Preparation decides outcomes.","How do victorious warriors differ from defeated ones?","They secure victory through preparation before engaging.","Prepare thoroughly for tomorrow's most important meeting or task."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","In the midst of chaos there is also opportunity. Disorder in your surroundings can be turned to your advantage if you stay calm.","What does Sun Tzu see in chaos?","Opportunity.","When something goes wrong today, look for the opening it creates."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Be like water: it shapes itself to the ground. Adapt your approach to circumstances rather than forcing a fixed plan.","Why does Sun Tzu compare strategy to water?","Water adapts to terrain; strategy must adapt to circumstances.","Change approach on one stuck problem instead of pushing harder."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Choose your battles. There are roads not to follow, armies not to attack, and positions not to contest.","What does Sun Tzu say about which battles to fight?","Not every battle is worth fighting; choose carefully.","Drop one argument or fight that isn't worth it."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","We become what we repeatedly do. Virtues are built by practice, like a craft: we become brave by doing brave acts.","How are virtues acquired, according to Aristotle?","Through habit — repeatedly performing virtuous acts.","Choose one virtue and do one small act of it today."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Virtue is a mean between two extremes: courage lies between cowardice and recklessness, generosity between stinginess and waste.","What is Aristotle's 'golden mean'?","Virtue as the midpoint between excess and deficiency.","Identify one trait where you lean to an extreme and nudge toward the middle."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","The highest good is eudaimonia — flourishing — a life of activity in accordance with virtue, over a complete lifetime, not momentary pleasure.","What is eudaimonia?","Flourishing: virtuous activity across a whole life.","Ask: what would a flourishing version of today look like?"],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","There are three kinds of friendship: of utility, of pleasure, and of virtue. Only the last, based on mutual admiration of character, endures.","Which kind of friendship endures, per Aristotle?","Friendship of virtue — based on character.","Reach out to a friend you admire for who they are."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Anyone can become angry. But to be angry with the right person, to the right degree, at the right time, for the right purpose — that is hard.","What makes anger virtuous, per Aristotle?","Right person, right degree, right time, right purpose.","If annoyed today, check all four 'rights' before expressing it."],["L","w","Aristotle","Rhetoric","Persuasion rests on three pillars: ethos (character), pathos (emotion) and logos (reason). Use all three.","What are Aristotle's three modes of persuasion?","Ethos, pathos, logos.","In your next request, include credibility, feeling and a reason."],["L","w","Aristotle","Metaphysics","'It is the mark of an educated mind to be able to entertain a thought without accepting it.' Curiosity before judgement.","What marks an educated mind?","Entertaining an idea without accepting it.","Steelman a view you disagree with for two minutes."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Franklin tracked 13 virtues, focusing on one per week and marking lapses daily. Small, systematic self-improvement compounds.","How did Franklin work on his virtues?","Focusing on one of 13 virtues weekly and tracking daily lapses.","Pick one virtue for this week and track it tonight."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Franklin began each day asking 'What good shall I do this day?' and ended asking 'What good have I done today?'","What were Franklin's morning and evening questions?","'What good shall I do this day?' / 'What good have I done today?'","Ask the morning question now; ask the evening one before bed."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","The Ben Franklin effect: asking someone for a small favour makes them like you more, because people justify helping by liking.","What is the Ben Franklin effect?","People who do you a favour come to like you more.","Ask someone for a small favour or recommendation today."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","'Early to bed and early to rise makes a man healthy, wealthy, and wise.' Rhythm and rest underpin all achievement.","What does Franklin link to early rising?","Health, wealth and wisdom.","Set a bedtime alarm tonight, 30 minutes earlier than usual."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","'Lost time is never found again.' Treat minutes as money and you'll spend them better.","What did Franklin say about lost time?","It is never found again.","Track where your time goes for one afternoon."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Franklin stopped contradicting people bluntly and began with 'I imagine' or 'it appears to me'. Modest phrasing won him far more agreement.","What phrasing change did Franklin make in arguments?","He replaced dogmatic assertions with modest ones like 'it appears to me'.","Replace 'You're wrong' with 'I see it differently because…' today."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","'An investment in knowledge pays the best interest.' Learning compounds over a lifetime.","According to Franklin, what investment pays best?","Knowledge.","Spend 15 minutes learning a skill that will pay off for years."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Don't criticise, condemn or complain. Criticism makes people defensive and rarely changes behaviour.","What is Carnegie's first principle?","Don't criticise, condemn or complain.","Go a full day without complaining. Restart the count if you slip."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Become genuinely interested in other people. You make more friends in two months by being interested in them than in two years trying to get them interested in you.","How does Carnegie say you make friends fastest?","By being genuinely interested in others.","Ask someone three follow-up questions about their life."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","A person's name is to them the sweetest sound in any language. Remember and use it.","What did Carnegie say about names?","A name is the sweetest sound to its owner — remember and use it.","Use the name of everyone you speak with today."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","The only way to win an argument is to avoid it. Even if you win, the other person resents you.","How do you get the best of an argument, per Carnegie?","Avoid it.","Let one argument go today, even if you're right."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","If you are wrong, admit it quickly and emphatically. It disarms the other person and earns respect.","What should you do when you're wrong, per Carnegie?","Admit it quickly and emphatically.","Own one recent mistake openly."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Talk in terms of the other person's interests. To influence someone, show how something gets them what they want.","How do you influence people, per Carnegie?","Talk in terms of their interests and wants.","Frame one request today around what the other person gains."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Live in 'day-tight compartments'. Shut out yesterday's regrets and tomorrow's fears; deal only with today.","What are 'day-tight compartments'?","Living only in today, closing off past and future.","When worry about tomorrow arises, say 'Not today's job.'"],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Ask: what is the worst that could happen? Accept it mentally, then calmly work to improve on it.","What is Carnegie's three-step worry formula?","Ask the worst case, accept it, then work to improve on it.","Apply the formula to one worry in writing."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Do nothing which is of no use. Strip away wasted motion in combat and in life.","What is one of Musashi's simplest rules?","Do nothing which is of no use.","Eliminate one useless task or habit from today."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Perceive what cannot be seen with the eye. Train both broad 'perception' and narrow 'sight' to read situations deeply.","What two kinds of seeing does Musashi distinguish?","Perception (broad, intuitive) and sight (narrow, literal).","In one meeting, notice the mood and what's unspoken."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Mastery is reached through daily practice over years: 'a thousand days of training to forge, ten thousand days to polish'.","What does Musashi say mastery requires?","Long, daily practice — thousands of days.","Do 10 minutes of deliberate practice on your core skill."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Do not regret what you have done. Learn the lesson, then put the act behind you.","What does Musashi say about regret?","Do not regret your past actions.","Write a lesson from a regret, then let the regret go."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Know the ways of all professions. Understanding other crafts sharpens your own.","Why should one study other crafts, per Musashi?","Understanding other ways deepens mastery of your own.","Learn how someone in a different field solves a problem."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Do not pursue pleasure for its own sake. Pleasure pursued becomes a master.","What is Musashi's view on pleasure-seeking?","Don't pursue pleasure for its own sake.","Postpone one pleasure today and notice your freedom."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","'A journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step.' Begin small; the beginning matters more than the plan.","Where does a long journey begin?","With a single step.","Take the smallest first step on a project you've been delaying."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Water is soft yet wears away stone. Gentleness and persistence overcome hardness and force.","What does water teach in the Tao Te Ching?","Softness and persistence overcome hardness.","Approach one conflict today softly instead of forcefully."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","He who knows others is wise; he who knows himself is enlightened. Mastering others is strength; mastering yourself is true power.","What does Lao Tzu call true power?","Mastering oneself.","Spend five minutes journaling about why you reacted as you did recently."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","When you are content to be simply yourself and don't compare or compete, everyone will respect you.","What does Lao Tzu say brings respect?","Being content as yourself without comparing or competing.","Notice one comparison you make today and drop it."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","When you see a worthy person, think of becoming equal to them. When you see an unworthy one, examine yourself inwardly.","What should we do on meeting unworthy people, per Confucius?","Examine ourselves for the same faults.","When someone annoys you today, check whether you share that flaw."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","'It does not matter how slowly you go as long as you do not stop.' Consistency beats speed.","What matters more than speed, per Confucius?","Not stopping — consistency.","Do your habit today even if only for two minutes."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","Do not impose on others what you would not wish for yourself. The silver rule as foundation of all ethics.","What is Confucius's version of the golden rule?","Don't do to others what you wouldn't want done to you.","Before one decision affecting others, picture yourself in their place."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","Learning without thinking is useless; thinking without learning is dangerous. Combine study and reflection.","What's wrong with learning without thought?","It's useless; and thought without learning is dangerous.","After this session, reflect for 2 minutes on one card."],["L","w","Plato","Apology","'The unexamined life is not worth living,' said Socrates. Regular self-examination is what makes life human.","What did Socrates say about the unexamined life?","It is not worth living.","Spend five minutes tonight reviewing your day honestly."],["L","w","Plato","Republic","In the allegory of the cave, prisoners mistake shadows for reality. Education is turning the soul toward the light, which is uncomfortable at first.","What does the cave allegory represent?","Mistaking appearances for reality; education as turning toward truth.","Question one belief you hold only because everyone around you does."],["L","w","Plato","Apology","Socrates was wise because he knew that he did not know. Intellectual humility opens the door to learning.","Why did the oracle call Socrates wise?","He knew that he knew nothing.","Say 'I don't know' honestly at least once today."],["L","w","Plato","Phaedrus","The soul is like a charioteer driving two horses — one noble, one unruly. Reason must guide both spirit and appetite.","What does the charioteer represent?","Reason guiding spirit and appetite.","Notice when appetite wants control today and let reason take the reins."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","'Our life is frittered away by detail. Simplify, simplify.' Fewer possessions and commitments leave room to live deliberately.","What did Thoreau urge in response to detail?","Simplify.","Remove or give away one thing you don't need."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","Thoreau went to the woods to live deliberately and front only the essential facts of life. Deliberate living means choosing, not drifting.","Why did Thoreau go to the woods?","To live deliberately and confront life's essentials.","Spend 20 minutes outdoors without your phone."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","The cost of a thing is the amount of life you exchange for it. Price everything in hours of your life.","How does Thoreau define cost?","The amount of life exchanged for it.","Convert your next purchase into hours of work before buying."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Self-Reliance","Trust yourself. Imitation is suicide; envy is ignorance. Your own perspective is worth expressing.","What does Emerson call imitation?","Suicide (of one's own originality).","Share one original idea you've kept to yourself."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Self-Reliance","'A foolish consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds.' Don't cling to past positions just to look consistent.","What is the hobgoblin of little minds?","A foolish consistency.","Change your mind openly about something when the evidence warrants."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Compensation","Every gain has a cost, and every loss brings something. The universe balances accounts over time.","What is Emerson's law of compensation?","Every gain has a cost and every loss a compensation.","Find the hidden benefit in a recent setback."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","'My life has been full of terrible misfortunes, most of which never happened.' Fear invents more than fate does.","What did Montaigne say about his misfortunes?","Most never actually happened.","Write down one fear and its realistic likelihood."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","To philosophise is to learn how to die. Familiarity with mortality removes its terror and sharpens life.","Why does Montaigne say to philosophise is to learn to die?","Contemplating death removes fear and makes life vivid.","Reflect for one minute: what would I do if this were my last year?"],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","Montaigne asked 'What do I know?' and wrote essays as experiments on himself. Self-study is a lifelong, honest inquiry.","What question was Montaigne's motto?","'What do I know?'","Write a short paragraph about a habit of yours, as if studying a stranger."],["L","w","Blaise Pascal","Pensées","All humanity's problems stem from our inability to sit quietly in a room alone. We flee boredom into distraction.","What did Pascal identify as the root of human trouble?","Inability to sit quietly alone in a room.","Sit alone, without input, for five minutes."],["L","w","Blaise Pascal","Provincial Letters","'I have made this letter longer than usual because I lacked the time to make it shorter.' Brevity takes effort.","What did Pascal apologise for in his letter?","Its length — he lacked time to make it shorter.","Edit your next message to half its length."],["B","m","Baltasar Gracián","The Art of Worldly Wisdom","Never compete with someone who has nothing to lose. They can risk everything; you can't.","Whom does Gracián advise never to compete with?","Someone who has nothing to lose.","Identify a fight where the other side risks little — and step back."],["B","m","Baltasar Gracián","The Art of Worldly Wisdom","Know how to say no. A gracious refusal is better than a grudging yes, and it protects your time.","What does Gracián say about refusing?","A gracious no beats a grudging yes.","Decline one request politely today."],["B","m","Baltasar Gracián","The Art of Worldly Wisdom","Leave others a little hungry. Don't exhaust your welcome or over-satisfy; scarcity preserves value.","Why should you leave people 'a little hungry'?","Scarcity preserves value and esteem.","End one conversation while it's still good."],["B","m","Niccolò Machiavelli","The Prince","Fortune governs half our actions; the other half is left to us. Build dikes in calm weather so the flood can't sweep you away.","What does Machiavelli advise doing in calm times?","Prepare defences ('dikes') against future misfortune.","Add to an emergency fund or backup plan today."],["B","m","Niccolò Machiavelli","The Prince","Men judge by appearance more than by touch, since everyone can see but few can feel. Manage impressions, but substance must back them.","How do people mainly judge, per Machiavelli?","By appearances — few experience the substance.","Make sure one piece of work today both looks and is excellent."],["L","w","Epicurus","Letter to Menoeceus","Pleasure is the absence of pain in the body and trouble in the soul — not luxury. Simple bread and water give real contentment.","How does Epicurus define pleasure?","Absence of bodily pain and mental disturbance.","Enjoy one very simple pleasure fully today."],["L","w","Epicurus","Principal Doctrines","Of all the things wisdom provides for a happy life, the greatest is friendship.","What did Epicurus call wisdom's greatest gift for happiness?","Friendship.","Share a meal or call with a friend this week — schedule it now."],["L","w","Epicurus","Fragments","Do not spoil what you have by desiring what you have not. What you now own was once among the things you hoped for.","What spoils what we have, per Epicurus?","Desiring what we lack.","Name three things you once wished for that you now have."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","We are what we think. With our thoughts we make the world. Guard your mind as you would your home.","What does the Dhammapada say we are?","What we think.","Notice one recurring negative thought and replace it."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Holding onto anger is like grasping a hot coal to throw at someone; you are the one who gets burned.","What is anger compared to?","Grasping a hot coal — you get burned first.","Release one grudge in writing."],["L","w","Thomas à Kempis","The Imitation of Christ","First keep peace within yourself; then you can bring peace to others.","What must come before bringing peace to others?","Peace within yourself.","Start the day with five quiet minutes before any screen."],["L","d","Boethius","The Consolation of Philosophy","Fortune is a turning wheel. Those at the top will fall, those at the bottom will rise. Happiness built on fortune is unstable.","What image does Boethius use for fortune?","A turning wheel.","Remind yourself that today's situation, good or bad, will turn."],["L","d","Boethius","The Consolation of Philosophy","Nothing is miserable unless you think it so; and every lot is happy to one who bears it calmly.","What makes a situation miserable, per Boethius?","Thinking it so.","Describe your current hardest situation in calm, neutral words."],["B","m","Samuel Smiles","Self-Help","Great results come from patient, persistent effort by ordinary people, not from genius. Character is built in daily drudgery.","What did Smiles see behind great achievements?","Patient perseverance by ordinary people.","Do the boring but important task first today."],["L","w","Arthur Schopenhauer","The Wisdom of Life","What a person is matters more than what he has or how he's regarded. Health and inner riches outweigh wealth and status.","Which of Schopenhauer's three categories matters most?","What a person is (character, health, mind).","Invest an hour in health or mind instead of possessions."],["L","w","William James","Talks to Teachers","Make habits your ally: launch new habits with strong initiative, never allow an exception until the habit is rooted, and act on resolutions immediately.","What is James's rule about exceptions to new habits?","Never allow an exception until the habit is firmly rooted.","Pick one habit and promise no exceptions for 7 days."],["L","w","William James","The Principles of Psychology","Act the way you want to feel. Behaviour changes emotion as much as emotion changes behaviour.","What does James say about action and feeling?","Acting as if changes how you feel.","Stand tall and smile for one minute before a hard task."],["B","m","Napoleon Hill","Think and Grow Rich","Definiteness of purpose is the starting point of achievement. Write a specific goal and read it daily.","What does Hill call the starting point of all achievement?","Definiteness of purpose.","Write one specific goal with a deadline and read it aloud."],["L","w","Samuel Johnson","The Rambler","People need to be reminded more often than they need to be instructed. Repetition of known truths is how wisdom sticks.","What do people need more than instruction, per Johnson?","To be reminded.","Review three old cards you already know."],["L","w","Cicero","On Duties","Gratitude is not only the greatest of virtues, but the parent of all the others.","What does Cicero call the parent of all virtues?","Gratitude.","Write a thank-you note or message to someone today."],["L","w","Cicero","On Old Age","Each stage of life has its own fitting qualities. Old age need not be mourned if a life has been well spent in learning and service.","What makes old age good, per Cicero?","A life well spent; each age has its own virtues.","Ask an older person what they enjoy about their current age."],["L","w","Ecclesiastes","Ecclesiastes","For everything there is a season. Timing matters: a time to plant and a time to reap, a time to speak and a time to be silent.","What is the central idea of 'a time for everything'?","Each action has its proper season.","Ask: is this a season to push or to wait? Act accordingly."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","The Brothers Karamazov","Above all, don't lie to yourself. One who lies to himself loses the ability to see truth in himself or others.","What does Zosima warn against above all?","Lying to yourself.","Admit one uncomfortable truth to yourself in writing."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","The Brothers Karamazov","Active love is harder than love in dreams. Loving humanity in general is easy; loving the person in front of you takes work.","What does Dostoevsky contrast with 'love in dreams'?","Active love — concrete, effortful care for real people.","Do one practical kindness for someone nearby."],["L","w","Victor Hugo","Les Misérables","Bishop Myriel answered theft with mercy, and it transformed Jean Valjean's life. One act of grace can change a person's trajectory.","What turned Jean Valjean's life around?","The bishop's mercy after Valjean stole from him.","Give someone the benefit of the doubt today."],["L","w","Charles Darwin","Autobiography","Darwin kept a note of every fact contradicting his theories, because he knew the mind forgets inconvenient evidence quickly.","What did Darwin write down immediately?","Facts contradicting his own views.","Note one piece of evidence against a belief you hold."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","'While we are postponing, life speeds by.' Begin at once to live, and count each day as a separate life.","What did Seneca say happens while we postpone?","Life speeds by.","Do today one thing you've been 'saving for later'."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","'The happiness of your life depends on the quality of your thoughts.' Tend your mind like a garden.","On what does happiness depend, per Marcus?","The quality of your thoughts.","Write three thoughts you want to cultivate today."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","First say to yourself what you would be; then do what you have to do. Identity precedes action.","What does Epictetus say to decide first?","Who you want to be — then act accordingly.","Write 'I am someone who…' and do one act that proves it."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Appear weak when you are strong and strong when you are weak. Don't reveal your full hand.","What does Sun Tzu say about appearances?","Conceal your true strength or weakness.","Hold back one card in your next negotiation."],["L","w","Aristotle","Politics","Man is by nature a social animal. Isolation diminishes us; community is part of a good life.","What did Aristotle call humans by nature?","Social (political) animals.","Join or contribute to a group activity this week."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Give honest, sincere appreciation. People crave appreciation more than almost anything, and flattery is a counterfeit of it.","How does Carnegie distinguish appreciation from flattery?","Appreciation is sincere; flattery is insincere.","Give one specific, sincere compliment today."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","Great companies are led by \"Level 5\" leaders who pair personal humility with fierce resolve for the company. They credit others for success and blame themselves for failure.","What two traits define a Level 5 leader?","Personal humility and intense professional will.","When a job goes well this week, credit the crew by name; when it goes badly, own it first."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","First get the right people on the bus, then decide where to drive it. The right people make strategy easier; the wrong people make any strategy fail.","What comes before deciding direction, per Collins?","Getting the right people on the bus (and wrong ones off).","Rate each person on your team: would you hire them again today? Note one action."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","Confront the brutal facts while never losing faith that you will prevail. Optimism that ignores reality breaks people; realism without hope breaks morale.","What is the Stockdale Paradox?","Face the brutal facts while keeping faith you will prevail.","Write down the one ugly number in your business you've been avoiding."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","The Hedgehog Concept sits where three circles overlap: what you can be best at, what drives your economics, and what you're deeply passionate about.","What are the three circles of the Hedgehog Concept?","Best in the world at, economic engine, passion.","List the job types where you're best AND most profitable. Do more of those."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","Breakthroughs come from the flywheel: many consistent pushes in one direction that build momentum. There is no single defining moment.","What does the flywheel image represent?","Momentum built by consistent effort in one direction, not a single big moment.","Identify the one repeatable action that grows your business and do it today."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","Good is the enemy of great. Settling for 'good enough' removes the pressure to become excellent.","Why is good the enemy of great?","Being 'good enough' removes the drive to become great.","Pick one routine part of your work and raise its standard a notch."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","Great companies keep a 'stop doing' list as carefully as a to-do list. Discipline means saying no to opportunities outside your core.","What list did good-to-great companies keep?","A 'stop doing' list.","Write a stop-doing list with three items and drop one this week."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Built to Last","Visionary companies preserve a core ideology while stimulating progress in everything else. Values stay; methods change.","What do visionary companies keep fixed and what do they change?","Fixed core values; everything else evolves.","Write the two values your company would never compromise on."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Built to Last","Big Hairy Audacious Goals unify people around a bold, clear target with a finish line. They stretch a company more than vague missions do.","What is a BHAG?","A bold, clear, long-term goal that unifies effort.","Set a 10-year goal for your business in one sentence."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Great by Choice","Fire bullets, then cannonballs: test ideas cheaply before betting big. Calibrate with small shots, then commit fully to what works.","What does 'bullets then cannonballs' mean?","Test small and cheap first; commit big resources only after evidence.","Test a new service with one small job before investing in new equipment."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Great by Choice","The 20-mile march: hit steady targets in good times and bad. Consistency beats bursts of heroic effort followed by collapse.","What is the 20-mile march?","Steady, consistent progress regardless of conditions.","Set a minimum weekly target you will hit whatever the weather."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Most small businesses are started by technicians who love the work, not entrepreneurs who build systems. Doing the work is different from running a business.","What is the 'entrepreneurial myth'?","That knowing the technical work means you know how to run a business doing it.","Block one hour this week to work ON the business, not in it."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Every business owner wears three hats: entrepreneur, manager and technician. Healthy businesses balance all three.","What are Gerber's three personalities?","Entrepreneur, manager, technician.","Note which hat you wore most this week and which you neglected."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Build your business as if it were a franchise prototype to be copied 5,000 times. That forces you to systematise everything.","Why imagine your business as a franchise prototype?","It forces documented, repeatable systems.","Write a one-page checklist for your most common job type."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Systems run the business; people run the systems. Good systems let ordinary people produce extraordinary results.","What runs the business, per Gerber?","Systems; people operate the systems.","Document how a site should look when the crew leaves for the day."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Create an organisation chart with positions, even if you fill them all. It reveals which jobs exist and which you must eventually hand off.","Why draw an org chart when you're alone?","To see every role and plan which to delegate.","Draw your org chart with roles, and put your name where you currently fill them."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Your business is not your life. It should serve your life goals, not consume them.","What should the business serve?","Your life and goals.","Write what you want your business to give your life in 5 years."],["B","m","Eric Ries","The Lean Startup","Build, measure, learn. Make the smallest version that tests your idea, measure real behaviour, and learn before scaling.","What is the core loop of the Lean Startup?","Build–Measure–Learn.","Test one new offer on three customers before printing anything."],["B","m","Eric Ries","The Lean Startup","A minimum viable product is the simplest thing that teaches you whether customers want it. Perfection before feedback is waste.","What is an MVP for?","Learning what customers really want with minimum effort.","Offer a new service verbally to customers before building a price list."],["B","m","Eric Ries","The Lean Startup","Pivot or persevere: at regular intervals, decide on evidence whether to change course or keep going. Don't drift.","What decision should be made regularly?","Whether to pivot or persevere.","Review one service that isn't working: pivot it, drop it, or push on."],["B","m","Eric Ries","The Lean Startup","Beware vanity metrics like total enquiries; track actionable ones like conversion and repeat rate.","What's the difference between vanity and actionable metrics?","Vanity metrics look good; actionable ones guide decisions.","Calculate what share of your quotes became jobs last month."],["B","m","Peter Thiel","Zero to One","Competition is for losers: the best businesses create a niche they dominate rather than fighting rivals on price.","Why does Thiel dislike competition?","It drives profits to zero; monopoly niches earn more.","Name one niche where you could be the obvious local choice."],["B","m","Peter Thiel","Zero to One","Ask: what important truth do very few people agree with you on? Secrets are the source of new value.","What is Thiel's contrarian question?","What important truth do few people agree with you on?","Write one thing you know about your industry that others miss."],["B","m","Peter Thiel","Zero to One","Start small and dominate a small market, then expand. Too big a market at first means you own none of it.","Where should a new business start?","By dominating a small market.","Pick one neighbourhood or customer type to dominate this season."],["B","m","Peter Thiel","Zero to One","Distribution matters as much as product. A great service nobody hears about fails.","What do engineers often underestimate, per Thiel?","Distribution and sales.","Spend 30 minutes on marketing today, not on operations."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Reciprocity: people feel obliged to return favours. Giving first, genuinely, opens doors.","What is the reciprocity principle?","People feel compelled to return what they receive.","Give a client a free small extra (like site tidying) and don't mention it."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Commitment and consistency: once people take a small public step, they tend to stay consistent with it.","How does commitment influence behaviour?","People act consistently with prior commitments.","Ask clients to agree a small step (site visit) before the big quote."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Social proof: when unsure, people follow what similar others do. Show that people like them chose you.","What is social proof?","People follow others' behaviour when uncertain.","Ask two happy customers for a review or a photo of their finished job."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Authority: people defer to credible experts. Signal expertise honestly through credentials, uniforms and clear knowledge.","How can you ethically use authority?","Show genuine expertise and credentials clearly.","Add your certifications and years of experience to your quote template."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Liking: we say yes to people we like — those similar to us, who compliment us, and cooperate with us.","What factors increase liking?","Similarity, compliments, cooperation, familiarity.","Find common ground with a client before discussing price."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Scarcity: things seem more valuable when they are rare or disappearing. Honest limits raise perceived value.","Why does scarcity persuade?","Rare or limited things seem more valuable.","Tell clients honestly how many slots you have left this season."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Pre-Suasion","What you focus someone's attention on just before a request shapes their answer. The moment before matters.","What is pre-suasion?","Shaping attention just before a message to influence response.","Show a finished-job photo before presenting the quote."],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Tactical empathy: show you understand the other side's feelings and view. Understanding is not agreement, but it unlocks cooperation.","What is tactical empathy?","Deliberately understanding and voicing the other's perspective.","In your next negotiation, name the other person's concern before your own."],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Mirroring: repeat the last few words someone said, with an upward tone. It makes them keep talking and reveal more.","What is mirroring?","Repeating the last few key words to encourage elaboration.","Mirror a supplier or client three times in one conversation."],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Labelling: name the emotion you sense — 'It seems like you're worried about the timeline.' Naming defuses it.","How does labelling help in negotiation?","Naming emotions defuses negative ones and builds rapport.","Use 'It sounds like…' once in a difficult conversation."],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Aim for 'that's right', not 'you're right'. 'That's right' means they feel truly understood.","Which phrase signals a breakthrough?","'That's right.'","Summarise a client's problem until they say 'that's right'."],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Ask calibrated 'how' and 'what' questions: 'How am I supposed to do that?' makes the other side solve your problem.","What are calibrated questions?","Open 'how/what' questions that make the other side think about your constraints.","When pushed on price, ask 'How am I supposed to do that for this amount?'"],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","'No' is not failure; it makes people feel safe. Ask no-oriented questions like 'Is it a bad idea to…?'","Why seek a 'no'?","It makes people feel safe and in control, opening dialogue.","Ask a slow-paying customer: 'Have you given up on settling this?'"],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Don't split the difference. Meeting halfway often produces a bad deal for both. Hold your value and find creative terms.","Why not split the difference?","Compromise often yields poor outcomes; creative terms are better.","Offer a different scope instead of a lower price next time."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","The Effective Executive","Know where your time goes. Record it, cut what's useless, and consolidate the rest into large blocks.","What is Drucker's first step to effectiveness?","Record and analyse where your time goes.","Log your time for one full workday."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","The Effective Executive","Effective executives ask 'What can I contribute?' rather than 'What am I owed?' Focus on results outside yourself.","What question focuses effort on contribution?","'What can I contribute?'","Ask what single contribution would most help your customers this month."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","The Effective Executive","Build on strengths — your own and others'. Staff for strengths, not to minimise weaknesses.","What does Drucker say to build on?","Strengths.","Give each crew member the task they're best at tomorrow."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","The Effective Executive","Do first things first, one thing at a time. Concentration is the secret of effectiveness.","What is Drucker's secret of effectiveness?","Concentration — one thing at a time.","Work 90 minutes on one task with your phone off."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","The Practice of Management","The purpose of a business is to create a customer. Marketing and innovation are the two basic functions.","What is the purpose of a business, per Drucker?","To create a customer.","Ask a recent customer why they chose you."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","Management","What gets measured gets managed — but measure the right things. Wrong measures distort behaviour.","What's the risk of measuring the wrong things?","They drive the wrong behaviour.","Choose one number that truly reflects job quality and track it."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","Innovation and Entrepreneurship","Innovation is systematic: look for unexpected successes, failures, and changes in demand. Most opportunities hide in surprises.","Where does Drucker say innovation opportunities often hide?","In the unexpected — surprises in success, failure and demand.","Write down the most unexpected request you got this year. Is it a market?"],["B","m","Andrew Grove","High Output Management","A manager's output is the output of their team plus the teams they influence. Your job is leverage.","How does Grove define a manager's output?","The output of their organisation and those they influence.","Spend 30 minutes teaching a crew member a skill that multiplies their output."],["B","m","Andrew Grove","High Output Management","One-on-ones are high-leverage: a short regular meeting where the report sets the agenda.","Who sets the agenda in a Grove one-on-one?","The subordinate.","Have a 15-minute one-on-one with an employee and let them lead."],["B","m","Andrew Grove","High Output Management","Find the limiting step in your production and plan around it. The bottleneck sets the pace.","What does Grove say determines production flow?","The limiting step (bottleneck).","Identify the bottleneck on your current job — machine, truck or permit."],["B","m","Andrew Grove","Only the Paranoid Survive","Watch for strategic inflection points — moments when the fundamentals of your industry shift. Spot them early.","What is a strategic inflection point?","A moment when industry fundamentals change dramatically.","List one change (rules, tech, prices) that could reshape your trade."],["B","m","Andrew Grove","High Output Management","Task-relevant maturity determines management style: new people need structure; experienced ones need autonomy.","What should determine how closely you manage someone?","Their task-relevant maturity.","Give one experienced worker more freedom on their next task."],["B","m","Gary Keller","The ONE Thing","Ask: what's the one thing I can do such that by doing it everything else becomes easier or unnecessary?","What is the Focusing Question?","What's the ONE thing I can do such that everything else becomes easier or unnecessary?","Ask the focusing question about your business this morning."],["B","m","Gary Keller","The ONE Thing","Time-block your most important work first thing, and protect it like an appointment.","How does Keller protect the ONE thing?","Time-blocking it and defending the block.","Block the first hour tomorrow for your most important task."],["B","m","Gary Keller","The ONE Thing","Success is sequential, not simultaneous. Like dominoes, small wins topple bigger ones.","What does the domino effect illustrate?","Success builds sequentially from small to large.","Pick today's first domino and knock it down before lunch."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","Eat That Frog!","Do your hardest, most important task first thing in the morning. Everything after feels easier.","What is 'eating the frog'?","Doing the hardest important task first.","Make the call you're dreading before 9 a.m."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","Eat That Frog!","Apply the 80/20 rule: 20% of your tasks produce 80% of results. Focus there.","What does the 80/20 rule say about tasks?","20% of tasks produce 80% of results.","Find the 20% of customers who give 80% of profit."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","Eat That Frog!","Plan every day in advance, the night before. Ten minutes of planning saves hours.","When should the day be planned?","The evening before.","Write tomorrow's plan tonight in 10 minutes."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Get 1% better every day; small improvements compound into remarkable results.","What compounds over time in Atomic Habits?","Small daily 1% improvements.","Improve one tiny thing in your daily routine."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","You don't rise to the level of your goals; you fall to the level of your systems.","What matters more than goals, per Clear?","Systems.","Turn one goal into a daily system."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Make good habits obvious, attractive, easy and satisfying; invert these for bad habits.","What are the four laws of behaviour change?","Make it obvious, attractive, easy, satisfying.","Make one habit easier by preparing what you need tonight."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Every action is a vote for the type of person you want to become. Identity-based habits last longer than outcome-based ones.","What are actions, per Clear's identity view?","Votes for the person you want to become.","Do one action today that proves 'I run a professional company'."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Habit stacking: attach a new habit to an existing one — 'After I pour coffee, I'll check today's jobs.'","What is habit stacking?","Linking a new habit to an existing one.","Stack one business habit on your morning coffee."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Environment beats motivation. Design your space so the right action is the easy one.","What beats motivation, per Clear?","Environment design.","Organise the truck so the most-used tools are within reach."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","The two-minute rule: scale a new habit down to something that takes two minutes. Show up first, then improve.","What is the two-minute rule?","Start a habit with a version that takes two minutes.","Do two minutes of bookkeeping today."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Never miss twice. Missing once is an accident; missing twice starts a new habit.","What's the rule after slipping on a habit?","Never miss twice.","If you skipped a habit yesterday, do it today no matter what."],["B","m","Cal Newport","Deep Work","Deep work — focused, distraction-free effort — produces the most value and is increasingly rare.","Why is deep work valuable?","It produces high value and is rare.","Do one 60-minute deep-work block on quoting or planning."],["B","m","Cal Newport","Deep Work","Schedule every minute of your workday in blocks. Planning reveals where time leaks.","What does Newport recommend doing with your workday?","Time-blocking every minute.","Block tomorrow's day in 30-minute chunks."],["B","m","Cal Newport","Deep Work","Shut down work at a fixed time with a ritual. Rest improves the next day's focus.","Why use a shutdown ritual?","It ends work cleanly so the mind can rest and recharge.","End today by reviewing tomorrow's list and saying 'shutdown complete'."],["B","m","Cal Newport","So Good They Can't Ignore You","Passion follows mastery. Build rare and valuable skills, and passion will grow from competence.","What does Newport say comes before passion?","Mastery of rare and valuable skills.","Pick one skill that would make you rare in your trade and practise it."],["B","m","Greg McKeown","Essentialism","Less but better. Pursue fewer things with full energy rather than many half-heartedly.","What is the essentialist motto?","Less but better.","Cut one low-value activity from your week."],["B","m","Greg McKeown","Essentialism","If it's not a clear yes, it's a clear no. Use strict criteria for commitments.","What is McKeown's 90% rule?","If something isn't a clear yes (90%+), it's a no.","Turn down one job that doesn't fit your best work."],["B","m","Greg McKeown","Essentialism","Protect the asset — you. Sleep and recovery are prerequisites for high contribution.","What is the asset to protect?","Yourself: sleep, health, energy.","Sleep 8 hours tonight."],["B","m","Greg McKeown","Essentialism","Build buffers: add 50% to time estimates. Things take longer than we expect.","How much buffer does McKeown suggest?","About 50% on time estimates.","Add buffer days to your next job schedule."],["B","m","Eliyahu Goldratt","The Goal","The goal of a business is to make money now and in the future. Every activity should be judged by that.","What is 'the goal'?","To make money now and in the future.","Check whether today's tasks contribute to profit."],["B","m","Eliyahu Goldratt","The Goal","An hour lost at a bottleneck is an hour lost for the whole system. An hour saved elsewhere is a mirage.","Why is bottleneck time so valuable?","Lost bottleneck time is lost system output.","Keep your bottleneck machine running during lunch by staggering breaks."],["B","m","Eliyahu Goldratt","The Goal","Theory of Constraints: identify the constraint, exploit it, subordinate everything else, elevate it, repeat.","What are the five focusing steps?","Identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, repeat.","Apply step one: name your constraint this month."],["B","m","Eliyahu Goldratt","The Goal","Local efficiency can hurt the whole. Keeping everyone busy can create piles of useless work.","Why can local efficiency be harmful?","It can create excess inventory and hide the real constraint.","Stop one task done just to look busy."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Traction","Every business has six key components: vision, people, data, issues, process and traction. Strengthen each.","What are the six EOS components?","Vision, people, data, issues, process, traction.","Score your business 1–10 on each of the six."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Traction","Rocks: set 3–7 priorities per quarter. Everything else waits.","What are 'Rocks'?","The 3–7 most important 90-day priorities.","Write your three Rocks for the next 90 days."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Traction","A weekly scorecard of 5–15 numbers tells you the health of the business at a glance.","What is a scorecard?","A weekly set of key numbers.","Make a 5-number weekly scorecard: quotes, jobs won, revenue, cash, hours."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Traction","Identify, Discuss, Solve: tackle issues one at a time by root cause, not by talking endlessly.","What is the IDS process?","Identify, Discuss, Solve.","Take one recurring problem through IDS today."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Traction","Right people, right seats: people must share core values (right person) and have the capacity for their role (right seat).","What defines 'right person, right seat'?","Shares values, and gets, wants and has capacity for the role.","Check one role: is the right person in the right seat?"],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Profit First","Take profit first: Sales − Profit = Expenses. Pay yourself before costs, and the business adapts.","What is the Profit First formula?","Sales − Profit = Expenses.","Open a separate profit account and move 1% of today's income there."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Profit First","Use separate bank accounts for profit, owner's pay, tax and operating expenses. Physical separation guides behaviour.","Why separate accounts?","Visible separation prevents spending money meant for profit and tax.","Set up a dedicated tax account this week."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Profit First","Parkinson's Law applies to money: expenses rise to meet available cash. Less available, more creative.","How does Parkinson's Law apply to money?","Spending expands to match available money.","Review subscriptions and cancel one you don't need."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Clockwork","Design the business to run without you. Identify the Queen Bee Role — the one thing that makes the business thrive — and protect it.","What is the Queen Bee Role?","The core function most critical to the business's success.","Name your Queen Bee Role and ensure it's covered every day."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Clockwork","Your time should shift from doing to deciding to delegating to designing.","What are the four Ds of time in Clockwork?","Doing, deciding, delegating, designing.","Move one task from 'doing' to 'delegating' this week."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Offers","Make an offer so good people feel stupid saying no. Value = dream outcome × likelihood ÷ (time delay × effort).","What is Hormozi's value equation?","Dream outcome × perceived likelihood ÷ (time delay × effort).","Rewrite one service offer to reduce the customer's effort."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Offers","Charge more. Higher prices allow better service, better staff and happier customers.","Why does Hormozi argue for higher prices?","They fund better delivery and attract better clients.","Raise prices on your most in-demand service by 5–10%."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Offers","Guarantees reduce perceived risk. A strong, specific guarantee can boost sales more than discounts.","What does a guarantee reduce?","The buyer's perceived risk.","Draft a clear guarantee, e.g. 'site left clean or we return free'."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Leads","To get leads, do more of the core four: warm outreach, cold outreach, content, and paid ads.","What are the 'core four' lead methods?","Warm outreach, cold outreach, content, paid ads.","Contact five past customers today to ask for referrals."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Offers","Niche down: a specific offer to a specific audience commands higher prices.","Why niche down?","Specific offers for specific audiences command premium prices.","Write a service offer aimed at one specific customer type."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Extreme Ownership","Leaders own everything in their world. No excuses, no blame — only ownership.","What is extreme ownership?","Taking full responsibility for all outcomes in your world.","Own one problem today that you'd normally blame on others."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Extreme Ownership","There are no bad teams, only bad leaders. Standards are set by what the leader tolerates.","What does Willink say about bad teams?","There are no bad teams, only bad leaders.","Address one sloppy standard you have been tolerating."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Extreme Ownership","Prioritise and execute: when overwhelmed, pick the highest-priority problem and attack it.","What's the method when facing multiple problems?","Prioritise and execute — one at a time.","When stressed today, write problems down and solve the top one."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Extreme Ownership","Simple: plans and orders must be simple and clear so everyone understands them.","Why must plans be simple?","Complexity causes confusion and failure.","Explain tomorrow's job plan to the crew in three sentences."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Extreme Ownership","Commander's intent: explain the why, so people can make good decisions without you.","What is commander's intent?","The purpose behind the plan, enabling independent decisions.","Tell the crew why a job matters, not just what to do."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Discipline Equals Freedom","Discipline creates freedom: routines and rigour give you control over your time and options.","How does discipline create freedom?","It gives control and options.","Get up at the same time tomorrow and start without delay."],["B","m","Ben Horowitz","The Hard Thing About Hard Things","There's no recipe for hard problems. Leaders must make decisions in the 'struggle' without clear answers.","What does Horowitz call the difficult period every founder faces?","The Struggle.","Make one decision you've been postponing."],["B","m","Ben Horowitz","The Hard Thing About Hard Things","Take care of the people, the products and the profits — in that order.","In what order should leaders take care of things?","People, products, profits.","Ask a worker what would make their job easier."],["B","m","Ben Horowitz","The Hard Thing About Hard Things","When delivering bad news to staff, be direct and honest. Sugar-coating erodes trust.","How should bad news be delivered?","Directly and honestly.","Deliver one overdue piece of feedback clearly and kindly."],["B","m","Jason Fried & DHH","Rework","Planning is guessing. Make decisions as you go, based on what you know now.","What does Rework say about long-term plans?","They're guesses; decide as you go.","Replace a long plan with next week's three priorities."],["B","m","Jason Fried & DHH","Rework","Embrace constraints: limited time, money and people force creativity and focus.","How are constraints useful?","They force creativity and focus.","Solve one problem today without spending money."],["B","m","Jason Fried & DHH","Rework","Say no by default. Every yes costs focus and future flexibility.","What's Rework's default answer?","No.","Decline one small request that distracts from core work."],["B","m","Jason Fried & DHH","Rework","Underdo the competition: do less, but better and simpler.","How should you compete, per Rework?","Do less but better and simpler.","Remove one service that complicates your business."],["B","m","Daniel Kahneman","Thinking, Fast and Slow","System 1 thinks fast and intuitively; System 2 thinks slow and deliberately. Big decisions deserve System 2.","What are System 1 and System 2?","Fast intuitive thinking and slow deliberate thinking.","Sleep on one big purchase before deciding."],["B","m","Daniel Kahneman","Thinking, Fast and Slow","The planning fallacy: we underestimate time and cost by focusing on the best case. Use past similar jobs as reference.","What is the planning fallacy?","Underestimating time and cost by assuming the best case.","Compare your next estimate to the actual time of three similar past jobs."],["B","m","Daniel Kahneman","Thinking, Fast and Slow","Loss aversion: losses hurt about twice as much as equal gains feel good. It can make us too cautious.","What is loss aversion?","Losses feel about twice as painful as gains feel good.","Frame a decision as both gain and loss to see it clearly."],["B","m","Daniel Kahneman","Thinking, Fast and Slow","Anchoring: the first number mentioned strongly influences negotiations. Set the anchor yourself.","What is anchoring?","The first number heavily influences judgement.","Lead with your price in your next negotiation."],["B","m","Daniel Kahneman","Thinking, Fast and Slow","Run a premortem: imagine the project failed and list why. It surfaces risks optimism hides.","What is a premortem?","Imagining a project has failed and explaining why, before it starts.","Do a 5-minute premortem on your next big job."],["B","m","Clayton Christensen","The Innovator's Dilemma","Successful companies fail by listening too well to current customers while cheaper, simpler disruptors grow from below.","Why do good companies get disrupted?","They serve existing customers while ignoring simpler, cheaper innovations.","Watch one low-cost competitor and note what they do well."],["B","m","Clayton Christensen","Competing Against Luck","Customers 'hire' products to do a job. Understand the job and you understand demand.","What is jobs-to-be-done?","Customers hire a product to accomplish a job in their lives.","Ask a client what problem they really wanted solved."],["B","m","W. Chan Kim & Renée Mauborgne","Blue Ocean Strategy","Instead of fighting in crowded 'red oceans', create uncontested 'blue oceans' with new value.","What is a blue ocean?","An uncontested market space with new demand.","Brainstorm one service nobody locally offers."],["B","m","W. Chan Kim & Renée Mauborgne","Blue Ocean Strategy","Eliminate, reduce, raise, create: redesign your offering to cut costs while raising value.","What is the ERRC grid?","Eliminate, Reduce, Raise, Create.","Fill out an ERRC grid for your main service."],["B","m","David Allen","Getting Things Done","Your mind is for having ideas, not holding them. Capture everything in a trusted system.","What is the purpose of capturing tasks?","To free your mind by storing tasks in a trusted system.","Write every open loop on paper today."],["B","m","David Allen","Getting Things Done","If an action takes less than two minutes, do it now.","What is GTD's two-minute rule?","If it takes under two minutes, do it immediately.","Clear all two-minute emails this morning."],["B","m","David Allen","Getting Things Done","Define the next physical action for every project. Vague projects stall; concrete actions move.","What keeps projects moving in GTD?","Defining the next physical action.","Write the next action for each active job."],["B","m","David Allen","Getting Things Done","Do a weekly review: clear inboxes, update lists, look ahead. It keeps the system trustworthy.","What is the weekly review?","A weekly reset of lists and plans.","Do a 30-minute weekly review on Friday."],["B","m","Morgan Housel","The Psychology of Money","Doing well with money is more about behaviour than intelligence. Patience and humility beat cleverness.","What matters most in managing money, per Housel?","Behaviour, not intelligence.","Automate one savings transfer today."],["B","m","Morgan Housel","The Psychology of Money","Wealth is what you don't see — the cars not bought. Spending to show wealth is the fastest way to lose it.","How does Housel define wealth?","Money not spent; unseen assets.","Delay one purchase meant to impress others."],["B","m","Morgan Housel","The Psychology of Money","Room for error is underrated. Leave a margin of safety so bad luck doesn't wipe you out.","Why is room for error important?","It lets you survive bad luck and stay in the game.","Calculate how many months of costs your cash reserve covers."],["B","m","Morgan Housel","The Psychology of Money","Compounding works best when uninterrupted. The key is staying in the game a long time.","What does compounding need most?","Time without interruption.","Set up a monthly automatic investment, however small."],["B","m","Morgan Housel","The Psychology of Money","Enough is a skill. Moving goalposts can lead to risking what you need for what you don't.","Why is knowing 'enough' important?","Endless wanting leads to reckless risk.","Write what 'enough' income means for you."],["B","m","George S. Clason","The Richest Man in Babylon","Pay yourself first: keep at least a tenth of all you earn.","What is the first cure for a lean purse?","Save at least one-tenth of everything you earn.","Move 10% of today's income to savings."],["B","m","George S. Clason","The Richest Man in Babylon","Make your money work for you: put savings into assets that earn returns.","What should saved gold do?","Work and earn more.","Research one investment option for 15 minutes."],["B","m","George S. Clason","The Richest Man in Babylon","Guard your treasure from loss: invest only where principal is safe, and seek advice from experts.","What does Clason say about risky investments?","Protect principal; take advice from the experienced.","Ask an experienced person before your next big investment."],["B","m","George S. Clason","The Richest Man in Babylon","Control expenses: distinguish necessary expenses from desires, which are endless.","Why control expenses?","Desires are endless; budgets keep necessities in check.","Write a monthly budget for the business."],["B","m","Robert Kiyosaki","Rich Dad Poor Dad","Assets put money in your pocket; liabilities take money out. Buy assets.","How does Kiyosaki define an asset?","Something that puts money in your pocket.","Classify your big purchases as assets or liabilities."],["B","m","Robert Kiyosaki","Rich Dad Poor Dad","Financial literacy is essential: learn to read numbers like a balance sheet and cash flow statement.","What skill does Kiyosaki emphasise?","Financial literacy.","Read your last month's profit and loss in detail."],["B","m","Robert Kiyosaki","Rich Dad Poor Dad","Mind your own business: build your asset column while working your job.","What does 'mind your own business' mean here?","Build your own assets, not just earn wages.","Spend an hour on an income-producing asset."],["B","m","Benjamin Graham","The Intelligent Investor","Mr. Market is moody: his offers swing between euphoria and despair. Use his prices, don't be guided by them.","Who is Mr. Market?","A metaphor for a moody market offering prices daily.","Ignore market news today; check your long-term plan instead."],["B","m","Benjamin Graham","The Intelligent Investor","Margin of safety: buy only when price is well below value, protecting against mistakes.","What is the margin of safety?","Buying well below intrinsic value to cushion errors.","Apply a safety margin to your next equipment purchase decision."],["B","m","Benjamin Graham","The Intelligent Investor","The investor's chief problem — and worst enemy — is likely to be himself.","What is the investor's worst enemy, per Graham?","Himself — his emotions.","Write investment rules and follow them without exceptions."],["B","m","John Warrillow","Built to Sell","A business that depends on its owner is hard to sell. Standardise services and reduce owner dependence.","What makes a business sellable, per Warrillow?","Standardised offerings that don't depend on the owner.","Document one process only you currently know."],["B","m","John Warrillow","Built to Sell","Specialise in one thing you do well and productise it with a fixed price.","What does productising a service mean?","Packaging a service with defined scope and fixed price.","Create a fixed-price package for a common job."],["B","m","John Warrillow","Built to Sell","Charge up front or get deposits to improve cash flow.","How can service businesses improve cash flow?","Charge deposits or upfront payments.","Add a deposit requirement to your next quote."],["B","m","Simon Sinek","Start with Why","People don't buy what you do; they buy why you do it. Lead with purpose, then how and what.","What is the Golden Circle?","Why, How, What — start with why.","Write one sentence about why your company exists."],["B","m","Simon Sinek","Leaders Eat Last","Great leaders create a circle of safety where people feel protected and trusted.","What is the 'circle of safety'?","An environment where people feel safe and trusted.","Back up a worker publicly today."],["B","m","Simon Sinek","The Infinite Game","Business is an infinite game: the aim is to keep playing, not to 'win'. Think long-term.","What's the goal in an infinite game?","To keep playing — long-term survival and growth.","Make one decision today with a 10-year view."],["B","m","Kerry Patterson et al.","Crucial Conversations","When stakes are high and emotions strong, make it safe to talk. Safety comes before content.","What must come first in crucial conversations?","Safety.","Start a difficult talk by stating your good intent."],["B","m","Kerry Patterson et al.","Crucial Conversations","Start with heart: ask what you really want for yourself, the other person and the relationship.","What does 'start with heart' mean?","Clarify what you truly want before speaking.","Before a hard talk, write what you want from it."],["B","m","Kerry Patterson et al.","Crucial Conversations","Separate facts from stories. We react to the stories we tell ourselves about facts.","Why separate facts from stories?","We often react to our interpretations, not the facts.","Write the facts of a conflict without any judgement words."],["B","m","Patrick Lencioni","The Five Dysfunctions of a Team","Trust is the foundation of teamwork; without vulnerability-based trust, teams avoid conflict.","What is the first dysfunction?","Absence of trust.","Admit a mistake to your team today."],["B","m","Patrick Lencioni","The Five Dysfunctions of a Team","Healthy teams have productive conflict over ideas. Artificial harmony hides problems.","What does fear of conflict produce?","Artificial harmony and poor decisions.","Ask the crew to challenge your plan for tomorrow."],["B","m","Patrick Lencioni","The Five Dysfunctions of a Team","Commitment requires clarity and buy-in; people commit when they've been heard.","What enables commitment?","Clarity and having been heard.","End your next meeting by confirming who does what by when."],["B","m","Patrick Lencioni","The Five Dysfunctions of a Team","Accountability means peers holding each other to standards, not just the boss.","Who holds people accountable in strong teams?","Peers, not only the leader.","Set one shared crew standard and ask everyone to uphold it."],["B","m","Patrick Lencioni","The Ideal Team Player","Ideal team players are humble, hungry and people-smart.","What three virtues define an ideal team player?","Humble, hungry, smart (people-smart).","Rate yourself on humble, hungry, smart."],["B","m","John Doerr","Measure What Matters","OKRs: set ambitious Objectives with measurable Key Results that show progress.","What are OKRs?","Objectives and Key Results.","Write one OKR for this quarter."],["B","m","John Doerr","Measure What Matters","Key results must be measurable and verifiable; 'it's not a key result unless it has a number'.","What defines a good key result?","It's measurable with a number.","Add a number to one vague goal."],["B","m","Charlie Munger","Poor Charlie's Almanack","Invert, always invert: ask what would guarantee failure, then avoid it.","What is inversion?","Thinking about what would cause failure and avoiding it.","List three things that would ruin your business. Avoid them."],["B","m","Charlie Munger","Poor Charlie's Almanack","Build a latticework of mental models from many disciplines to think better.","What is a latticework of mental models?","Key ideas from many disciplines used together.","Learn one new mental model this week."],["B","m","Charlie Munger","Poor Charlie's Almanack","Stay within your circle of competence: know what you know and what you don't.","What is the circle of competence?","The area where you truly have expertise.","Decline one job outside your competence or partner with an expert."],["B","m","Charlie Munger","Poor Charlie's Almanack","Show me the incentive and I'll show you the outcome. Incentives drive behaviour.","What did Munger say about incentives?","Incentives determine outcomes.","Check whether your pay system rewards the behaviour you want."],["B","m","Warren Buffett","Berkshire Hathaway Letters","It takes 20 years to build a reputation and five minutes to ruin it.","How long does it take to ruin a reputation?","Five minutes.","Fix one small thing that could hurt your reputation."],["B","m","Warren Buffett","Berkshire Hathaway Letters","Be fearful when others are greedy and greedy when others are fearful.","What is Buffett's contrarian rule?","Fearful when others are greedy; greedy when others are fearful.","Note where others are panicking in your market and look for opportunity."],["B","m","Warren Buffett","Berkshire Hathaway Letters","Look for businesses with a moat: a durable competitive advantage.","What is an economic moat?","A durable competitive advantage.","Name your company's moat and how to widen it."],["B","m","Dan Martell","Buy Back Your Time","Hire to buy back your time: delegate tasks below your hourly value.","What is the buyback principle?","Hire to free time for higher-value work.","Calculate your hourly value and delegate one task below it."],["B","m","Dan Martell","Buy Back Your Time","Use the camcorder method: record yourself doing a task to make it easy to delegate.","What is the camcorder method?","Recording a task to create training material.","Film yourself doing one admin task."],["B","m","Chet Holmes","The Ultimate Sales Machine","Pigheaded discipline: master a few key skills through repetition rather than doing 4,000 things.","What does Holmes call the key trait?","Pigheaded discipline and determination.","Practise your sales pitch five times."],["B","m","Chet Holmes","The Ultimate Sales Machine","Educate prospects: share useful market data that positions you as the expert.","What is education-based marketing?","Sharing valuable information to attract buyers.","Post a short tip about drainage or groundwork online."],["B","m","Neil Rackham","SPIN Selling","Ask Situation, Problem, Implication and Need-payoff questions to uncover real needs.","What does SPIN stand for?","Situation, Problem, Implication, Need-payoff.","Use SPIN questions at your next site visit."],["B","m","Neil Rackham","SPIN Selling","Implication questions reveal the cost of the problem, making solutions valuable.","What do implication questions do?","Reveal consequences, increasing urgency.","Ask a client 'What happens if the water keeps pooling?'"],["B","m","Geoff Smart & Randy Street","Who","Hiring mistakes are costly; use a scorecard of outcomes before interviewing.","What should you create before hiring?","A scorecard of outcomes and competencies.","Write a scorecard for your next hire."],["B","m","Geoff Smart & Randy Street","Who","Use a chronological interview: walk through every job, asking about successes, failures and why they left.","What is the topgrading interview?","A chronological walk through the candidate's career.","Prepare five chronological questions for your next interview."],["B","m","Josh Kaufman","The Personal MBA","Every business has five parts: value creation, marketing, sales, value delivery, finance.","What are the five parts of every business?","Value creation, marketing, sales, delivery, finance.","Rate each of the five in your company."],["B","m","Josh Kaufman","The Personal MBA","Pricing is based on value to the customer, not just your costs.","How should prices be set?","Based on value to the customer.","Estimate the value your work creates for a client."],["B","m","Phil Knight","Shoe Dog","Just keep going. Knight's story shows persistence through setbacks and cash crunches.","What lesson runs through Shoe Dog?","Persistence through constant setbacks.","Make the next move on a project that stalled."],["B","m","Phil Knight","Shoe Dog","Cash flow can kill a growing company. Growth uses cash before it returns it.","Why can growth be dangerous?","It consumes cash before returning it.","Check your cash flow forecast for the next three months."],["B","m","Sam Walton","Made in America","Exceed customer expectations. If you do, they'll come back again and again.","What did Walton say keeps customers coming back?","Exceeding their expectations.","Do one unexpected extra on a job this week."],["B","m","Sam Walton","Made in America","Control expenses better than competitors. Frugality is a competitive advantage.","How did Walton gain advantage?","By controlling costs better than competitors.","Review fuel and machine costs for waste."],["B","m","Ray Dalio","Principles","Pain + reflection = progress. Mistakes are opportunities to learn.","What is Dalio's formula for progress?","Pain + reflection = progress.","Write a lesson from one recent failure."],["B","m","Ray Dalio","Principles","Radical transparency: share honest feedback openly so the best ideas win.","What is radical transparency?","Open, honest sharing of information and feedback.","Ask the crew for brutally honest feedback on your leadership."],["B","m","Ray Dalio","Principles","Write your principles down so decisions become consistent and teachable.","Why write principles down?","For consistent, teachable decision-making.","Write three principles for running your business."],["B","m","Kim Scott","Radical Candor","Care personally and challenge directly. Kindness without honesty is ruinous empathy.","What is radical candor?","Caring personally while challenging directly.","Give specific, kind but direct feedback today."],["B","m","Kim Scott","Radical Candor","Praise in public, criticise in private.","Where should criticism be given?","In private.","Praise a crew member publicly today."],["B","m","Marshall Goldsmith","What Got You Here Won't Get You There","Habits that brought success may block the next level. Stop adding too much value to others' ideas.","What is the problem with past success?","Old habits may block future growth.","Let someone's idea stand without improving it."],["B","m","Marshall Goldsmith","Triggers","Ask daily active questions: 'Did I do my best to…?' to measure effort, not outcomes.","What are active questions?","Questions measuring effort: 'Did I do my best to…?'","Write three 'Did I do my best to…' questions for tonight."],["B","m","Stephen M. R. Covey","The Speed of Trust","Trust increases speed and lowers cost. Low trust adds a tax to every transaction.","How does trust affect business?","High trust increases speed and lowers costs.","Keep every small promise to clients this week."],["B","m","Michael Porter","Competitive Strategy","There are three generic strategies: cost leadership, differentiation and focus. Being stuck in the middle is dangerous.","What are Porter's three generic strategies?","Cost leadership, differentiation, focus.","Decide which of the three strategies your company follows."],["B","m","Michael Porter","Competitive Strategy","Five forces shape profitability: rivals, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power and supplier power.","Name Porter's five forces.","Rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power, supplier power.","Note which force squeezes your margins most."],["B","m","Michael Porter","Competitive Advantage","Strategy is choosing what not to do. Trade-offs make a position hard to copy.","What is the essence of strategy, per Porter?","Choosing what not to do.","Name one customer type you will stop serving."],["B","m","Al Ries & Jack Trout","Positioning","Positioning happens in the customer's mind. Own one simple word or idea in their head.","Where does positioning happen?","In the prospect's mind.","Pick one word you want customers to associate with you."],["B","m","Al Ries & Jack Trout","The 22 Immutable Laws of Marketing","It's better to be first than better. If you can't be first in a category, create a new one.","What is the Law of Leadership?","Being first beats being better.","Find a sub-category you could be first in locally."],["B","m","Al Ries & Jack Trout","The 22 Immutable Laws of Marketing","The Law of Focus: owning a single word, like 'safety', is the most powerful marketing concept.","What is the Law of Focus?","Own one word in the prospect's mind.","Use your chosen word in all your marketing this month."],["B","m","Seth Godin","Purple Cow","Be remarkable. Safe, ordinary products are invisible; only remarkable ones get talked about.","What is a 'purple cow'?","Something remarkable that stands out.","Add one remarkable touch to how you finish jobs."],["B","m","Seth Godin","This Is Marketing","Find the smallest viable audience and serve them deeply.","What is the smallest viable audience?","The minimum group you can serve exceptionally well.","Describe your ideal customer in one paragraph."],["B","m","Seth Godin","The Dip","Most worthwhile pursuits have a 'dip' — a hard slog. Quit the wrong things fast, and push through the dip on the right ones.","What is the Dip?","The difficult period between starting and mastering.","Decide whether one struggle is a dip worth pushing through."],["B","m","Seth Godin","Linchpin","Be indispensable: bring art, judgement and generosity to your work.","What makes someone a linchpin?","Being indispensable through creativity and judgement.","Do one task today with extra care no one asked for."],["B","m","Donald Miller","Building a StoryBrand","Make the customer the hero and your business the guide. Customers care about their story.","In StoryBrand, who is the hero?","The customer.","Rewrite your website headline around the customer's problem."],["B","m","Donald Miller","Building a StoryBrand","Give customers a simple plan: three clear steps to working with you.","What simple element reduces customer confusion?","A clear three-step plan.","Write your three steps: call, site visit, job done."],["B","m","Gary Vaynerchuk","Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook","Give value repeatedly (jabs) before asking for the sale (right hook).","What does the boxing metaphor mean?","Provide value repeatedly before asking for a sale.","Post a helpful tip before posting any promotion."],["B","m","Allan Dib","The 1-Page Marketing Plan","Marketing has three phases: before (prospect), during (lead) and after (customer). Plan each.","What are the three phases?","Before, during, after.","Plan one follow-up step for after each job."],["B","m","Allan Dib","The 1-Page Marketing Plan","Nurture leads with follow-up; most sales happen after multiple contacts.","Why follow up repeatedly?","Most sales require multiple touches.","Follow up on three unanswered quotes today."],["B","m","Frank Bettger","How I Raised Myself from Failure to Success in Selling","Enthusiasm is contagious. Act enthusiastic and you'll become enthusiastic.","What did Bettger say about enthusiasm?","Acting enthusiastic makes you enthusiastic, and it's contagious.","Bring visible enthusiasm to your next client call."],["B","m","Frank Bettger","How I Raised Myself from Failure to Success in Selling","Keep records of calls and results; numbers reveal what each call is worth.","Why track sales calls?","To know your conversion rates and value per call.","Track how many quotes you send this week and how many win."],["B","m","Zig Ziglar","Secrets of Closing the Sale","You can have everything you want if you help enough other people get what they want.","What is Ziglar's core principle?","Help others get what they want and you'll get what you want.","Focus a sales talk entirely on the client's goals."],["B","m","Jeffrey Gitomer","The Little Red Book of Selling","People love to buy but hate being sold. Create an atmosphere where they want to buy.","What do people hate?","Being sold to.","Ask questions instead of pitching at your next site visit."],["B","m","Jeffrey Gitomer","The Little Red Book of Selling","Your reputation precedes your sales call. Referrals are the easiest sales.","What are the easiest sales?","Referrals.","Ask a satisfied client for one referral."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","The Psychology of Selling","The top 20% of salespeople earn 80% of the income, mainly by prospecting more and better.","What separates top salespeople?","More and better prospecting.","Spend one hour prospecting new clients this week."],["B","m","Daniel Pink","To Sell Is Human","Everyone is in sales now: persuading, influencing, moving others.","What is Pink's main claim?","Most of us are in sales — moving others.","Notice how many times you 'sell' today."],["B","m","Daniel Pink","Drive","Motivation for complex work comes from autonomy, mastery and purpose, not carrots and sticks.","What three elements drive motivation?","Autonomy, mastery, purpose.","Give a worker more say in how they do a task."],["B","m","Daniel Pink","When","Timing matters: most people peak in the morning for analytic work. Schedule hard tasks accordingly.","When are most people sharpest analytically?","In the morning.","Do quoting and pricing in your peak hours."],["B","m","Ken Blanchard & Spencer Johnson","The One Minute Manager","Set one-minute goals, give one-minute praisings and one-minute redirects.","What are the three secrets of the One Minute Manager?","One-minute goals, praisings, redirects.","Give one specific one-minute praising today."],["B","m","Ken Blanchard & Spencer Johnson","The One Minute Manager","Catch people doing something right.","What should managers look for?","People doing something right.","Catch a worker doing something right and say it."],["B","m","Spencer Johnson","Who Moved My Cheese?","Change happens; anticipate it, adapt quickly, and enjoy the new.","What's the main lesson of 'Who Moved My Cheese'?","Anticipate and adapt to change quickly.","Name one market change and plan your adaptation."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Let the other person feel the idea is theirs. People support what they help create.","Why let others own ideas?","People support ideas they feel are their own.","Ask your crew how to solve a problem rather than telling them."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Call attention to mistakes indirectly, and talk about your own mistakes first.","How should mistakes be pointed out?","Indirectly, after admitting your own.","Share a past mistake before correcting someone."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Give people a fine reputation to live up to.","What does a fine reputation do?","Gives people a standard to live up to.","Tell a worker you know they take pride in quality."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Begin in a friendly way. A drop of honey catches more flies than a gallon of gall.","How should you begin when you want agreement?","In a friendly way.","Open a tough email with a friendly line."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Get the other person saying 'yes, yes' immediately. Early agreement builds momentum.","What is the 'yes, yes' technique?","Start with questions the other will agree with.","Begin a negotiation with points you both agree on."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Production vs production capability (P/PC balance): don't kill the goose that lays golden eggs.","What is the P/PC balance?","Balancing results with maintaining the capacity to produce them.","Schedule maintenance on your main machine."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 8th Habit","Find your voice and inspire others to find theirs.","What is the 8th habit?","Find your voice and help others find theirs.","Ask a worker what they're passionate about."],["B","m","Napoleon Hill","Think and Grow Rich","Form a mastermind group: two or more people working in harmony toward a goal.","What is a mastermind?","A group of people cooperating toward a goal.","Invite two other business owners for a monthly coffee."],["B","m","Napoleon Hill","Think and Grow Rich","Persistence is the sustained effort necessary to induce faith. Most people quit just before success.","What does Hill say most people lack?","Persistence.","Keep going on one project despite a setback."],["B","m","Napoleon Hill","Think and Grow Rich","Specialised knowledge, organised and applied, is power.","What kind of knowledge is power, per Hill?","Specialised knowledge applied with a plan.","Learn one regulation that affects your trade."],["B","m","Napoleon Hill","Think and Grow Rich","Decision: successful people decide quickly and change slowly; failures do the opposite.","How do successful people make decisions?","Quickly, and change them slowly.","Make a quick decision on something you've been dithering on."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","'Beware of little expenses; a small leak will sink a great ship.'","What did Franklin warn about little expenses?","Small leaks sink great ships.","Find one small recurring cost to cut."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","'Remember that time is money.' Idle hours carry real cost.","What did Franklin equate with money?","Time.","Calculate what a wasted crew hour costs you."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Advice to a Young Tradesman","Credit is money; pay debts punctually, and your reputation lets you borrow when needed.","Why pay debts punctually?","It builds credit and trust.","Pay one supplier invoice early."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","All warfare is based on deception — in business, keep plans private until ready.","What is the basis of warfare, per Sun Tzu?","Deception.","Keep a new plan private until it's ready."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Speed is the essence of war. Act quickly on opportunities before others react.","What is the essence of war?","Speed.","Reply to a new enquiry within one hour today."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Attack where the enemy is unprepared. Serve needs competitors neglect.","Where should you attack?","Where the enemy is unprepared.","Find a service competitors do badly and do it well."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Treat your soldiers as your children and they will follow you into the deepest valleys.","How should leaders treat their people?","With care, like their own children.","Do something to make the crew's work more comfortable."],["B","m","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Plan carefully: the general who wins makes many calculations before battle.","What precedes victory?","Many calculations.","Calculate costs carefully before your next quote."],["B","m","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Do not develop a favourite weapon. Over-attachment to one tool limits you.","Why avoid a favourite weapon?","It limits flexibility.","Learn to use a tool or method you avoid."],["B","m","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Become acquainted with every art. Breadth makes your craft wiser.","What should a warrior know?","Every art.","Spend 15 minutes learning about bookkeeping or law."],["B","m","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Know the rhythm of things: timing in business, like in combat, decides outcomes.","Why is rhythm important to Musashi?","Timing decides outcomes.","Time a price increase for the start of your busy season."],["B","m","Niccolò Machiavelli","The Prince","It is better to be feared than loved if you cannot be both — but never be hated.","What must a leader avoid above all?","Being hated.","Make sure discipline is fair and consistent."],["B","m","Niccolò Machiavelli","The Prince","Inflict harm all at once, but grant benefits gradually so they are savoured.","How should benefits be given, per Machiavelli?","Little by little.","Deliver bad news in one go; spread rewards over time."],["B","m","Niccolò Machiavelli","The Prince","Change creates enemies among those who did well under the old order, and only lukewarm support from those who might benefit.","Why is introducing change hard?","Old beneficiaries oppose it; new ones only weakly support it.","Plan how to win support before a change at work."],["B","m","Baltasar Gracián","The Art of Worldly Wisdom","Avoid outshining the master. Superiority provokes resentment.","Why avoid outshining superiors?","It breeds resentment.","Let a key client take credit for a good idea."],["B","m","Baltasar Gracián","The Art of Worldly Wisdom","Never promise more than you can deliver. Hopes betrayed are long remembered.","What does Gracián say about promises?","Don't promise more than you can deliver.","Set realistic completion dates in your next quote."],["B","m","Baltasar Gracián","The Art of Worldly Wisdom","Choose your associates well; you're judged by the company you keep.","How are we judged, per Gracián?","By our associates.","Review one subcontractor's reputation."],["B","m","Samuel Smiles","Self-Help","Business success depends on attention, application, accuracy, method, punctuality and dispatch.","Which qualities did Smiles say business needs?","Attention, application, accuracy, method, punctuality, dispatch.","Be five minutes early to every appointment today."],["B","m","Samuel Smiles","Self-Help","Small savings, steadily made, are the foundation of independence.","What is the basis of independence, per Smiles?","Steady small savings.","Save a small fixed amount today."],["B","m","Samuel Smiles","Thrift","Debt makes a man a slave; avoid borrowing for consumption.","What did Smiles say about debt?","Debt enslaves.","Pay down one small debt."],["B","m","Verne Harnish","Scaling Up","Every growing firm must master four decisions: people, strategy, execution and cash.","What are the four Scaling Up decisions?","People, strategy, execution, cash.","Score yourself 1–10 on each of the four."],["B","m","Verne Harnish","Scaling Up","Hold a short daily huddle: what's up, key numbers, where you're stuck.","What is a daily huddle?","A 5–15 minute team check-in on progress and blockers.","Hold a 10-minute morning huddle with the crew."],["B","m","Verne Harnish","Scaling Up","Cash is oxygen. Shorten the cash conversion cycle: invoice faster, collect sooner.","What does Harnish call cash?","Oxygen.","Send every outstanding invoice today."],["B","m","Keith Cunningham","The Road Less Stupid","Schedule thinking time: sit with one important question and a notepad, undisturbed.","What practice does Cunningham recommend?","Regular dedicated thinking time.","Sit for 30 minutes with one question about your business."],["B","m","Keith Cunningham","The Road Less Stupid","Avoiding dumb mistakes beats chasing brilliant moves. Stupid tax is expensive.","What is the 'dumb tax'?","The cost of avoidable mistakes.","List the costliest avoidable mistake from last year and a rule to prevent it."],["B","m","Keith Cunningham","The Road Less Stupid","Know your numbers: revenue, gross margin, overhead and cash. Ignorance of numbers is the most common killer.","What must every owner know?","Their key financial numbers.","Calculate your gross margin on last month's jobs."],["B","m","Ramit Sethi","I Will Teach You to Be Rich","Spend extravagantly on what you love and cut costs mercilessly on what you don't.","What is conscious spending?","Spend big on what you love; cut what you don't care about.","Cut one expense you don't value."],["B","m","Ramit Sethi","I Will Teach You to Be Rich","Automate your finances so saving and investing happen without willpower.","Why automate finances?","It removes willpower from saving and investing.","Set up an automatic savings transfer on payday."],["B","m","Thomas Stanley","The Millionaire Next Door","Most millionaires live below their means, drive used cars and budget carefully.","How do most millionaires live?","Below their means.","Compare your lifestyle spending with your income."],["B","m","Thomas Stanley","The Millionaire Next Door","Wealth is built by allocating time, energy and money efficiently toward building wealth.","What three resources do wealthy people allocate efficiently?","Time, energy, money.","Spend 30 minutes planning your finances."],["B","m","Nassim Taleb","Antifragile","Some things gain from disorder. Build a business that benefits from shocks: diverse clients, low debt, options.","What does antifragile mean?","Gaining from volatility and stress.","Reduce dependence on your single biggest client."],["B","m","Nassim Taleb","The Black Swan","Rare, high-impact events shape history. Prepare for what you can't predict.","What is a Black Swan?","A rare, unpredictable, high-impact event.","Ensure you have insurance and reserves for a bad surprise."],["B","m","Nassim Taleb","Skin in the Game","Don't trust advice from people who don't bear the consequences.","What does skin in the game mean?","Bearing the consequences of your decisions.","Ask advisers whether they have done what they recommend."],["B","m","Annie Duke","Thinking in Bets","Judge decisions by process, not outcome. Good decisions can have bad results.","What is 'resulting'?","Judging decision quality solely by outcome.","Review a decision based on what you knew then."],["B","m","Annie Duke","Quit","Quitting at the right time is a skill. Set kill criteria in advance.","What are kill criteria?","Pre-set conditions for when to quit.","Set a kill criterion for one struggling service."],["B","m","Shane Parrish","Clear Thinking","Most bad decisions happen in ordinary moments when emotion, ego or social pressure take over. Create space before reacting.","When do most bad decisions happen?","In ordinary moments when defaults take over.","Pause before replying to an annoying message."],["B","m","Robert Greene","Mastery","Mastery requires an apprenticeship phase of deep, patient practice under good mentors.","What is the first phase of mastery?","The apprenticeship.","Ask an experienced tradesperson to teach you a technique."],["B","m","Robert Greene","The 48 Laws of Power","Guard your reputation with your life; it is the cornerstone of power.","What should be guarded at all costs?","Reputation.","Address one complaint before it spreads."],["B","m","Robert Greene","The 33 Strategies of War","Create a sense of urgency: work as if you're down to your last resources.","What is the 'death-ground' strategy?","Creating urgency as if no retreat is possible.","Set a hard deadline on a task you've delayed."],["B","m","Ryan Holiday","Ego Is the Enemy","Ego sabotages learning. Stay a student, especially after success.","What is the enemy, per Holiday?","Ego.","Ask for feedback after your next successful job."],["B","m","Ryan Holiday","The Obstacle Is the Way","Perception, action, will: see clearly, act decisively, endure what you can't change.","What are the three disciplines?","Perception, action, will.","Apply the three to today's biggest problem."],["B","m","Ryan Holiday","Stillness Is the Key","Stillness — calm focus — is the key to great performance in business and life.","What does Holiday call the key?","Stillness.","Take a 10-minute walk without your phone."],["B","m","Ryan Holiday","Discipline Is Destiny","Self-discipline in small things — sleep, food, words — builds the capacity for big ones.","What does Holiday say discipline determines?","Destiny.","Keep one small promise to yourself today."],["B","m","Tim Ferriss","The 4-Hour Workweek","Eliminate before you automate, automate before you delegate.","What is the order of operations?","Eliminate, automate, delegate.","Eliminate one recurring task this week."],["B","m","Tim Ferriss","The 4-Hour Workweek","Batch similar tasks: check email and do admin at set times.","What is batching?","Grouping similar tasks to do at once.","Answer emails only twice today."],["B","m","Tim Ferriss","Tools of Titans","Fear-setting: define your worst case, how to prevent and repair it, and the cost of inaction.","What is fear-setting?","Defining fears, prevention, repair, and cost of inaction.","Do a fear-setting exercise on one decision."],["B","m","Darren Hardy","The Compound Effect","Small, smart choices plus consistency plus time equal radical difference.","What is the compound effect formula?","Small choices + consistency + time = big results.","Track one small habit daily for a week."],["B","m","Darren Hardy","The Compound Effect","Track everything you want to improve — money, time, habits. Awareness changes behaviour.","Why track behaviour?","Awareness drives change.","Track every expense for one day."],["B","m","Grant Cardone","The 10X Rule","Set targets ten times higher than you think and take ten times the action. Most people underestimate effort.","What is the 10X Rule?","Set 10x goals and take 10x action.","Double your outreach today."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","Goals!","Write goals down, set deadlines and make a plan. Written goals are far more likely to be achieved.","What makes goals more effective?","Writing them down with deadlines and plans.","Write three business goals with dates."],["B","m","Og Mandino","The Greatest Salesman in the World","Form good habits and become their slave. Persist until you succeed.","What did the scroll say to persist until?","Until you succeed.","Make one more sales call than usual."],["B","m","Og Mandino","The Greatest Salesman in the World","Greet each day with love in your heart; it disarms resistance.","How should you greet each day?","With love in your heart.","Greet every client today warmly."],["B","m","Les McKeown","Predictable Success","Growing firms move through stages: early struggle, fun, whitewater, then predictable success. Systems are needed in whitewater.","What stage requires systems?","Whitewater.","Write one system to reduce chaos in your busiest week."],["B","m","Michael Gerber","The E-Myth Contractor","Contractors must price for overhead, profit and their own wage; most underprice.","What do most contractors forget in pricing?","Overhead, owner's wage and profit.","Recalculate your hourly rate including overhead and profit."],["B","m","Michael Gerber","The E-Myth Contractor","A good job file includes scope, schedule, materials, risks and photos.","What makes a good job file?","Scope, schedule, materials, risks, photos.","Create a job file template."],["B","m","Jack Stack","The Great Game of Business","Open-book management: teach employees the numbers and let them share in success.","What is open-book management?","Sharing financial data and rewarding results with employees.","Show your crew the cost of one job."],["B","m","Jack Welch","Winning","Candour is crucial. Telling the truth fast speeds everything up.","What did Welch say is most crucial?","Candour.","Give one straight answer you've been softening."],["B","m","Jack Welch","Winning","Differentiate: reward top performers, develop the middle and deal with low performers.","What is differentiation?","Treating performers differently by results.","Reward your best worker visibly."],["B","m","Ed Catmull","Creativity, Inc.","Early ideas are ugly babies; protect them while they mature.","What are 'ugly babies'?","Early fragile ideas.","Give a new idea a chance before judging it."],["B","m","Ed Catmull","Creativity, Inc.","Candid feedback groups ('braintrusts') improve work without taking ownership.","What is a braintrust?","A group giving candid, non-binding feedback.","Ask peers to critique a job plan."],["B","m","Howard Schultz","Pour Your Heart Into It","Treat employees as partners; their engagement shapes the customer experience.","How did Schultz treat employees?","As partners.","Thank a worker for their contribution today."],["B","m","Ray Kroc","Grinding It Out","Press on: nothing takes the place of persistence.","What did Kroc champion?","Persistence.","Finish an unfinished task."],["B","m","Elon Musk biography (Isaacson)","Elon Musk","Question every requirement, delete parts, simplify, accelerate, then automate.","What is 'the algorithm'?","Question, delete, simplify, accelerate, automate.","Question one requirement in a current process."],["B","m","Bob Iger","The Ride of a Lifetime","Optimism, courage, focus, decisiveness, curiosity, fairness, thoughtfulness, authenticity, perfection and integrity guide leadership.","Name three of Iger's leadership principles.","e.g. optimism, courage, decisiveness.","Act on one principle today."],["B","m","Satya Nadella","Hit Refresh","Growth mindset culture: be a learn-it-all, not a know-it-all.","What does Nadella prefer to a know-it-all?","A learn-it-all.","Learn one new thing from a crew member."],["B","m","Carol Dweck","Mindset","A growth mindset believes abilities can be developed. It welcomes challenge.","What is a growth mindset?","Belief that abilities can be developed.","Try a challenging task you've avoided."],["B","m","Angela Duckworth","Grit","Grit — passion and perseverance for long-term goals — predicts success more than talent.","What is grit?","Passion plus perseverance.","Commit to one long-term goal."],["B","m","Charles Duhigg","The Power of Habit","Habits follow a loop: cue, routine, reward. Change the routine while keeping cue and reward.","What is the habit loop?","Cue, routine, reward.","Identify the cue of one bad habit."],["B","m","Charles Duhigg","The Power of Habit","Keystone habits, like exercise or planning, trigger widespread positive change.","What is a keystone habit?","A habit that sparks other positive changes.","Start one keystone habit."],["B","m","Nir Eyal","Indistractable","Distraction is an escape from discomfort. Master internal triggers to stay focused.","What causes distraction, per Eyal?","Avoiding internal discomfort.","Note what feeling precedes your phone checking."],["B","m","Brené Brown","Dare to Lead","Clear is kind; unclear is unkind. Vague expectations hurt people.","What does 'clear is kind' mean?","Clear expectations are kinder than vague ones.","Clarify expectations with one worker."],["B","m","Simon Sinek","Start with Why","People inspired by why stay loyal through hard times.","Why do purpose-driven firms have loyal customers?","Customers believe what they believe.","Tell a client why you care about quality."],["B","m","Patrick Bet-David","Your Next Five Moves","Know yourself, then think five moves ahead, like a chess master.","What does Bet-David urge?","Thinking five moves ahead.","Plan your next five business moves."],["B","m","Robert Kiyosaki","Cashflow Quadrant","Income comes from four quadrants: employee, self-employed, business owner, investor.","What are the four quadrants?","Employee, self-employed, business owner, investor.","Identify your quadrant and your next one."],["B","m","MJ DeMarco","The Millionaire Fastlane","Wealth comes from control, entry barriers, need, time and scale — not from slow saving alone.","What are DeMarco's CENTS?","Control, Entry, Need, Time, Scale.","Rate your business on CENTS."],["B","m","Paul Jarvis","Company of One","Growth isn't always better; staying small and profitable can be the goal.","What does Jarvis question?","The assumption that growth is always good.","Decide your ideal company size."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","The Pumpkin Plan","Grow your best customers like prize pumpkins; cut the worst.","What is the Pumpkin Plan?","Focus on top clients and cut poor ones.","Rank your clients and drop the worst one."],["B","m","Jim Collins","Good to Great","Technology accelerates momentum but never creates it. Adopt tools that fit your core, not fads.","How do great companies treat technology?","As an accelerator, not a creator, of momentum.","Pick one tool that would speed up your best service."],["B","m","Jim Collins","How the Mighty Fall","Decline begins with hubris born of success, then undisciplined pursuit of more.","What is the first stage of decline?","Hubris born of success.","List one assumption you've stopped questioning since things went well."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","The client sees your business, not you. Every touchpoint — phone, truck, site — tells a story.","What do customers experience, per Gerber?","The whole business's touchpoints.","Clean the truck and tidy the site sign today."],["B","m","Michael E. Gerber","The E-Myth Revisited","Quantify everything: how many calls, quotes, wins. Numbers make the invisible visible.","Why quantify, per Gerber?","To see what's really happening.","Count this week's calls, quotes and wins."],["B","m","Eric Ries","The Lean Startup","Ask 'five whys' to find the root cause of a problem, then fix the system.","What is the five whys technique?","Asking why five times to reach a root cause.","Ask five whys about a recent delay on site."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","The Effective Executive","Effective people make few, fundamental decisions rather than many small ones.","What kind of decisions do effective executives make?","Few, fundamental ones.","Turn a recurring decision into a standing rule."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","Managing Oneself","Use feedback analysis: write expected results of decisions, then compare 9–12 months later.","What is feedback analysis?","Comparing actual results to written expectations.","Write expected results for one decision today."],["B","m","Peter Drucker","Managing Oneself","Know how you learn and work best — reader or listener, alone or in teams.","What should you know about yourself?","How you perform and learn best.","Note when and how you work best."],["B","m","Andrew Grove","High Output Management","Detect problems at the lowest-value stage. Inspect early when fixing is cheap.","Where should problems be caught?","At the lowest-value stage.","Check measurements before the concrete truck arrives."],["B","m","Andrew Grove","High Output Management","Use leading indicators to see the future: quotes sent today predict revenue next month.","What are leading indicators?","Measures predicting future results.","Track quotes sent this week as a leading indicator."],["B","m","Robert Cialdini","Influence","Unity: people say yes to those they see as part of 'us'. Shared identity is powerful.","What is the unity principle?","Shared identity increases influence.","Mention your local roots to a local client."],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","Use an accusation audit: name every negative thing they might think about you before they say it.","What is an accusation audit?","Listing the other side's possible negative feelings upfront.","Start a price talk: 'You may think this is expensive…'"],["B","m","Chris Voss","Never Split the Difference","The late-night FM DJ voice — calm, slow, downward — calms tension.","What voice does Voss recommend?","A calm, slow 'late-night FM DJ' voice.","Use a slower, calmer voice on a tense call."],["B","m","Cal Newport","Slow Productivity","Do fewer things, work at a natural pace and obsess over quality.","What are the three principles of slow productivity?","Fewer things, natural pace, obsess over quality.","Limit active jobs to what you can do excellently."],["B","m","Cal Newport","A World Without Email","Replace ad-hoc messaging with clear processes; inbox chaos kills focus.","What does Newport propose instead of constant messaging?","Structured processes.","Create a standard form for job enquiries."],["B","m","Greg McKeown","Effortless","Make the essential easy: ask 'what if this could be easy?'","What question does Effortless pose?","What if this could be easy?","Simplify one hard recurring task."],["B","m","Eliyahu Goldratt","The Goal","Reduce batch sizes to speed flow; huge batches create waiting.","What do large batches cause?","Waiting and slow flow.","Split a big order into smaller deliveries."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Traction","Every role should have one clear measurable that shows if the job is done well.","What does each seat need?","A measurable.","Give each crew role one clear measure."],["B","m","Gino Wickman","Rocket Fuel","Every company needs a Visionary and an Integrator who turns vision into execution.","What two roles does Rocket Fuel describe?","Visionary and Integrator.","Decide who in your company is the integrator."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Profit First","Pay yourself a fair salary; an owner working for free is subsidising an unprofitable business.","Why must the owner be paid?","Otherwise the business is hiding losses.","Set a regular owner's pay transfer."],["B","m","Mike Michalowicz","Fix This Next","Fix needs in order: sales, profit, order, impact, legacy — like a business pyramid.","What is the Business Hierarchy of Needs?","Sales, profit, order, impact, legacy.","Find the lowest unmet level and fix it."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Offers","Stack bonuses that solve the next problems the customer will face.","How should bonuses be chosen?","To solve the customer's next problems.","Add a free drainage check to your landscaping offer."],["B","m","Alex Hormozi","$100M Leads","Volume negates luck: do more outreach than seems reasonable.","What negates luck in lead generation?","Volume.","Make 20 contacts this week."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","The Dichotomy of Leadership","Leaders must balance opposites: confident not cocky, disciplined not rigid.","What is the dichotomy of leadership?","Balancing opposing qualities.","Find one area where you overcorrect."],["B","m","Jocko Willink","Extreme Ownership","Decentralised command: give small teams clear intent and let them lead.","What is decentralised command?","Empowering junior leaders with clear intent.","Let a foreman run tomorrow's job."],["B","m","Ben Horowitz","What You Do Is Who You Are","Culture is what people do when the leader isn't there.","What is culture, per Horowitz?","What people do without supervision.","Ask what happens on site when you're absent."],["B","m","Jason Fried & DHH","It Doesn't Have to Be Crazy at Work","Calm companies set reasonable hours and deadlines; chaos isn't a badge of honour.","What do calm companies avoid?","Constant crisis and overwork.","Set a firm end time for workdays this week."],["B","m","Daniel Kahneman","Noise","Different people judge the same case differently; checklists and guidelines reduce noise.","What is noise in judgement?","Unwanted variability in decisions.","Create a quote checklist so prices are consistent."],["B","m","Morgan Housel","Same as Ever","Things that never change: risk, greed, fear, incentives. Plan around timeless behaviour.","What does Housel focus on in Same as Ever?","Timeless human behaviour.","Plan for a downturn, since cycles always return."],["B","m","Warren Buffett","Berkshire Hathaway Letters","Rule No. 1: never lose money. Rule No. 2: never forget rule No. 1.","What are Buffett's two rules?","Never lose money; never forget rule 1.","Check that every job is priced to profit."],["B","m","Warren Buffett","Berkshire Hathaway Letters","Price is what you pay; value is what you get.","What is the difference between price and value?","Price is paid; value is received.","Explain value, not price, in your next quote."],["B","m","Charlie Munger","Poor Charlie's Almanack","Avoid envy, resentment and self-pity — they are wealth killers.","What did Munger say to avoid?","Envy, resentment, self-pity.","Drop one envious thought about a competitor."],["B","m","Ray Dalio","Principles","Believability-weight decisions: give more weight to people with track records.","What is believability weighting?","Weighting opinions by track record.","Ask the most experienced person before deciding."],["B","m","Kim Scott","Radical Candor","Get feedback before giving it: ask 'What could I do that would make it easier to work with me?'","How should leaders start with feedback?","By asking for it first.","Ask a worker what you could do better."],["B","m","Stephen M. R. Covey","The Speed of Trust","Talk straight, show respect, create transparency and keep commitments.","Name two trust behaviours.","e.g. talk straight, keep commitments.","Be transparent about a job delay."],["B","m","Simon Sinek","Leaders Eat Last","In great teams, leaders sacrifice their comfort for their people.","Why do leaders 'eat last'?","They put their people's needs first.","Let the crew go home first today."],["B","m","Josh Kaufman","The Personal MBA","Every offer carries perceived risk; reduce it with guarantees and proof.","How do you reduce perceived risk?","Guarantees and proof.","Add before/after photos to quotes."],["B","m","Josh Kaufman","The Personal MBA","Track your sales funnel: prospects → leads → quotes → customers.","What is a sales funnel?","The stages from prospect to customer.","Draw your funnel with last month's numbers."],["B","m","Seth Godin","The Dip","Winners quit fast and often — just not the right things.","What do winners do, per Godin?","Quit the wrong things quickly.","Quit one dead-end effort."],["B","m","Donald Miller","Business Made Simple","Value-driven professionals solve problems, take ownership and get things done.","What characterises a value-driven professional?","Problem-solving, ownership, action.","Solve one problem without being asked."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","No Excuses!","Self-discipline is the key to personal greatness; do what you should when you should.","What is the key to greatness, per Tracy?","Self-discipline.","Do one task the moment it's due."],["B","m","Napoleon Hill","Think and Grow Rich","Desire backed by a definite plan and burning commitment turns into riches.","What starts riches, per Hill?","Burning desire with a plan.","Write a burning desire and plan."],["B","m","Tony Robbins","Money: Master the Game","Know your numbers: how much you need for security, independence and freedom.","What financial dreams does Robbins define?","Security, vitality, independence, freedom.","Calculate the income you need for security."],["B","m","Dave Ramsey","The Total Money Makeover","Build an emergency fund, then pay debts smallest to largest — the debt snowball.","What is the debt snowball?","Paying debts smallest to largest for momentum.","List your debts smallest to largest."],["B","m","Dave Ramsey","EntreLeadership","Profit is the scorecard. Communicate clearly and treat team members as people.","What does Ramsey call profit?","The scorecard.","Share one profit number with the team."],["B","m","Jack Stack","The Great Game of Business","Make business a game with clear scores and rewards; people play harder.","What is the great game?","Business run with clear scores and shared rewards.","Set a team bonus for a job finished early."],["B","m","Marshall Goldsmith","What Got You Here Won't Get You There","Say thank you. Simply thanking people for feedback avoids defensiveness.","How should you respond to feedback?","Say thank you.","Thank someone for feedback without arguing."],["B","m","Brian Tracy","Eat That Frog!","Use the ABCDE method: rank tasks A (must) to E (eliminate).","What is ABCDE?","Ranking tasks by importance.","Rank today's tasks A–E."],["B","m","David Allen","Getting Things Done","Use contexts: group tasks by where they can be done — @phone, @truck, @office.","What are contexts?","Groupings by place or tool.","Make an @phone list and clear it."],["B","m","James Clear","Atomic Habits","Measure the habit, not just the outcome; seeing progress keeps you going.","Why use a habit tracker?","Visible progress motivates.","Track one habit with a check mark."],["B","m","Ryan Holiday","The Daily Stoic","The first rule of business: don't make the problem worse.","What is a rule for problems?","Don't make them worse.","Pause before reacting to a site problem."],["B","m","Nassim Taleb","Antifragile","Use the barbell: keep most resources very safe and a small part in high-upside bets.","What is the barbell strategy?","Extreme safety plus small high-risk bets.","Keep reserves safe and try one small new venture."],["B","m","Annie Duke","Thinking in Bets","Ask 'How sure am I?' and express beliefs as percentages.","How should beliefs be expressed?","As probabilities.","Rate your confidence in a forecast."],["B","m","Shane Parrish","The Great Mental Models","The map is not the territory: plans simplify reality.","What does 'map is not the territory' mean?","Models simplify reality.","Visit a site before relying on the drawing."],["B","m","Shane Parrish","The Great Mental Models","Second-order thinking: ask 'and then what?'","What is second-order thinking?","Considering consequences of consequences.","Ask 'and then what?' about a decision."],["B","m","Shane Parrish","The Great Mental Models","First principles: break problems down to basic truths and rebuild.","What is first-principles thinking?","Reasoning from fundamentals.","Rebuild a price from first principles."],["B","m","Rory Sutherland","Alchemy","Perceived value matters; small psychological touches can matter more than big changes.","What does Sutherland emphasise?","Psychology over pure logic.","Send a short progress photo to a client during a job."],["B","m","Rory Sutherland","Alchemy","The opposite of a good idea can also be a good idea.","What can the opposite of a good idea be?","Another good idea.","Try the opposite approach to a problem."],["B","m","Byron Sharp","How Brands Grow","Brands grow by being easy to notice and easy to buy. Mental and physical availability matter.","How do brands grow, per Sharp?","Mental and physical availability.","Make your phone number clear on every vehicle."],["B","m","Jim Rohn","The Art of Exceptional Living","Work harder on yourself than on your job.","What should you work hardest on?","Yourself.","Read for 30 minutes."],["B","m","Jim Rohn","The Seasons of Life","You are the average of the five people you spend most time with.","Who are you the average of?","The five people you spend most time with.","List your five and assess."],["B","m","Jim Rohn","The Five Major Pieces to the Life Puzzle","Discipline weighs ounces, regret weighs tons.","What weighs more: discipline or regret?","Regret.","Choose discipline in one moment."],["B","m","Zig Ziglar","See You at the Top","Motivation doesn't last; neither does bathing — that's why we recommend it daily.","Why need daily motivation?","It wears off like bathing.","Start with an inspiring read."],["B","m","Earl Nightingale","The Strangest Secret","We become what we think about.","What is the strangest secret?","We become what we think about.","Think about your goal for 5 minutes."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Cooperate with the inevitable; don't fight what can't be changed.","What should we do with the inevitable?","Cooperate with it.","Accept an unavoidable delay."],["B","m","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Keep busy: worry thrives in idleness.","What is a cure for worry?","Keeping busy.","Do practical work when you worry."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","First Things First","Put the big rocks in first: schedule priorities before filling time with small things.","What is the big rocks lesson?","Schedule priorities first.","Place three big rocks in tomorrow's calendar."],["B","m","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Paradigm shifts change how we see everything; change the lens before the behaviour.","What is a paradigm shift?","A change in perspective.","View a problem from the customer's lens."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Franklin formed the Junto, a club of tradesmen who met weekly to improve themselves and their businesses.","What was the Junto?","A self-improvement club of tradesmen.","Start or join a group of local business owners."],["B","m","Benjamin Franklin","The Way to Wealth","Industry pays debts; despair increases them.","What pays debts, per Franklin?","Industry.","Work an extra hour on income today."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Travel cannot cure a troubled mind; you take yourself with you. Change the mind, not the scenery.","Why doesn't travel cure unhappiness, per Seneca?","You bring yourself along.","Find calm where you are today instead of wishing to be elsewhere."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Count each day as a separate life. Close it at night with a review, as if it were complete.","How should each day be treated?","As a separate life.","Review today tonight as if it were your whole life."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","A friend is someone you can trust like yourself. Judge carefully before friendship, then trust fully.","When should judgement happen in friendship?","Before, not after.","Tell a trusted friend something real."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Philosophy is not for display but for shaping the soul and ordering life.","What is philosophy for, per Seneca?","Shaping the soul and guiding action.","Apply one idea instead of quoting it."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","No wind is favourable to the sailor who doesn't know his port.","What did Seneca say about the sailor without a port?","No wind helps him.","Write where you're heading this year."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Avoid the crowd; it sweeps you along into its vices. Spend time with those who improve you.","Why avoid the crowd?","It pulls you into its habits.","Skip one gathering that drains you."],["L","d","Seneca","On Tranquillity of Mind","Restlessness comes from dissatisfaction with oneself. Settle into useful work.","What causes restlessness?","Dissatisfaction with oneself.","Commit an hour to useful work without switching."],["L","d","Seneca","On Providence","Fire tests gold; misfortune tests brave men. Hardship is training.","What does misfortune test?","Brave people, like fire tests gold.","Treat a hardship today as a training exercise."],["L","d","Seneca","On Anger","Look at yourself in a mirror when angry; the ugliness may shame you into calm.","What odd remedy for anger did Seneca mention?","Looking in a mirror.","Notice your face when irritated."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Hold every day as if it were your last, and you will never fear tomorrow.","How should we treat each day?","As if it were the last.","Do today what matters most."],["L","d","Seneca","On the Happy Life","A happy life is one in harmony with its own nature, guided by reason.","What makes a life happy, per Seneca?","Living in harmony with nature and reason.","Act according to your values once today."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","It is not the man who has too little who is poor, but the one who craves more.","Who is truly poor?","The one who craves more.","Name what you already have enough of."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Look within. Within is the fountain of good, which will bubble up if you keep digging.","Where is the fountain of good?","Within.","Spend five minutes in quiet reflection."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Soon you will have forgotten everything; soon everything will have forgotten you. Act well now.","What perspective does Marcus give on fame?","Everything is soon forgotten.","Do one good deed anonymously."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Accept the things to which fate binds you, and love the people with whom fate brings you together.","What should we love?","The people fate brings us together with.","Show appreciation to someone you see daily."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","What is not good for the hive is not good for the bee. Individual good aligns with common good.","What did Marcus say about the hive and the bee?","What harms the hive harms the bee.","Do one thing for your community."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Confine yourself to the present. The past and future have no power over you now.","Where should attention be?","In the present.","Do one task with full presence."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Never value anything that makes you break promises or lose self-respect.","What should never be valued?","Anything that costs self-respect or integrity.","Refuse one compromise of your integrity."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Loss is nothing else but change, and change is nature's delight.","How does Marcus define loss?","Simply change.","Reframe a loss as change."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Do not act as if you had ten thousand years to live. Death hangs over you; be good while you can.","Why act now?","Life is limited.","Do the good thing you've postponed."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","How much time he gains who does not look at what his neighbour says or does.","What wastes time, per Marcus?","Watching what others do.","Avoid checking others' social media today."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Kindness is invincible, if it is sincere. It disarms the most violent person.","What is invincible?","Sincere kindness.","Respond to hostility with kindness."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Begin each day by telling yourself what you will meet — and that none can harm your character.","What can't others harm?","Your character.","Remind yourself this morning that no one can harm your character."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","The art of living is more like wrestling than dancing; be ready to stand firm.","What is the art of living like?","Wrestling.","Stand firm on one decision today."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","If someone can show me I'm wrong, I'll gladly change. I seek truth, which never harmed anyone.","How did Marcus view correction?","He welcomed it.","Thank someone who corrects you."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","If you want to be good, first believe that you are bad. Awareness of fault begins improvement.","What starts improvement?","Recognising your faults.","Name one fault honestly."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","At a banquet, take what's offered modestly; wait for what hasn't reached you. Do the same with life.","What is the banquet metaphor?","Accept what comes modestly; don't grab.","Practise patience with something you want."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","You are an actor in a play chosen by another. Play your role well.","What role analogy does Epictetus use?","Actor in a play.","Play your role well today, whatever it is."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","If someone criticises you, say: 'He doesn't know my other faults, or he'd mention those too.'","How should we respond to criticism?","With humour and humility.","Respond to criticism with good humour."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Difficulties show what men are. Life sends a rough opponent so you can train.","What do difficulties reveal?","Character.","Welcome a hard task as training."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Freedom is secured not by fulfilling desires but by removing them.","How is freedom secured?","By eliminating desires.","Give up one want today."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Make the best of what is in your power, and take the rest as it happens.","What should we do with what's outside our power?","Accept it as it happens.","Let go of one outcome."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Don't seek for things to happen as you wish; wish them as they happen.","What should we wish?","For things as they happen.","Accept one disappointment calmly."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","Before any action, consider what comes before and after it. Then begin.","What should we consider before acting?","What precedes and follows.","Think through one decision fully."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Practise yourself in little things, and thence proceed to greater.","Where should practice start?","With little things.","Practise patience in small annoyances."],["L","d","Musonius Rufus","Lectures","Philosophy must be practised more than studied; virtue is a matter of training.","What is virtue, per Musonius?","A matter of training.","Practise one virtue deliberately."],["L","d","Musonius Rufus","Lectures","Eat simple food, sleep on a hard bed and endure cold sometimes: it strengthens body and soul.","Why choose hardship?","It strengthens body and soul.","Take a cold shower."],["L","d","Musonius Rufus","Lectures","If you do good with toil, the toil passes but the good remains; if you do wrong with pleasure, the pleasure passes but the wrong remains.","What remains after good done with toil?","The good.","Do a good but tiring task."],["L","d","Cato (via Plutarch)","Plutarch's Lives","Cato practised being laughed at by wearing unusual clothes, so he'd be ashamed only of what was truly shameful.","Why did Cato wear odd clothes?","To learn to ignore unjust shame.","Do something slightly unusual without caring what others think."],["L","w","Plutarch","Moralia","The mind is not a vessel to be filled but a fire to be kindled.","What did Plutarch call the mind?","A fire to be kindled.","Explore a topic out of pure curiosity."],["L","w","Plutarch","Moralia","Know how to listen and you will profit even from those who talk badly.","How can you profit from poor speakers?","By listening well.","Listen fully to someone you usually tune out."],["L","w","Plutarch","Parallel Lives","Character is revealed more in small actions and words than in great battles.","Where is character revealed?","In small actions.","Notice your small actions today."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Suffering ceases to be suffering the moment it finds meaning.","When does suffering change?","When it finds meaning.","Find a meaning in a current difficulty."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Humour is a weapon of the soul in the fight for self-preservation.","What role did humour play in the camps?","A weapon of the soul.","Find humour in a stressful situation."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Love is the ultimate and highest goal to which humans can aspire.","What is the highest goal, per Frankl?","Love.","Express love to someone today."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Self-transcendence — reaching beyond oneself — is the essence of human existence.","What is self-transcendence?","Reaching beyond oneself to people or causes.","Help someone without expecting anything."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","The Will to Meaning","The existential vacuum — boredom and emptiness — appears when meaning is missing.","What is the existential vacuum?","Emptiness from lack of meaning.","Replace idle scrolling with meaningful activity."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Even in the worst conditions, people chose to comfort others and give away their last bread.","What did some prisoners do?","Comforted others and gave away bread.","Give something today when it's inconvenient."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","War and Peace","Pierre found that happiness lies in simple needs met and in loving life itself.","What did Pierre learn in captivity?","Happiness lies in simple things.","Enjoy a simple meal mindfully."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","War and Peace","Prince Andrei, wounded, saw the sky's infinity and his ambitions seemed small.","What did Andrei realise at Austerlitz?","The vastness of the sky made ambitions petty.","Look at the sky for a minute."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Anna Karenina","Levin found meaning not in theories but in living for goodness and caring for others.","Where did Levin find meaning?","Living for goodness.","Do one good act for family."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Anna Karenina","Levin's scythe-mowing with peasants showed the joy of absorbed physical work.","What did Levin experience while mowing?","Absorbed joy in physical work.","Do some physical work with full attention."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","The Kingdom of God Is Within You","Real change comes from within each person, not from force.","Where is the kingdom of God, per Tolstoy?","Within you.","Change one inner attitude."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","What Men Live By","People live by love — not by worry for themselves.","What do men live by, per Tolstoy?","Love.","Act from care rather than worry."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Master and Man","A selfish master, lost in a blizzard, finds joy by warming his servant with his body.","What transforms the master?","Self-sacrifice for his servant.","Put someone else's comfort first today."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","A Calendar of Wisdom","Read a little wisdom each day; daily reflection shapes the soul.","What did Tolstoy's Calendar of Wisdom encourage?","Daily reflection on wise thoughts.","Reread one card tonight."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Pleasure in doing good acts signals virtue has become habit.","What signals true virtue?","Taking pleasure in virtuous acts.","Notice whether you enjoy doing the right thing."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Practical wisdom (phronesis) is knowing how to act well in particular situations.","What is phronesis?","Practical wisdom.","Choose the wise action in one situation today."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","One swallow does not make a spring, nor one day a happy life.","What does 'one swallow' mean?","A single good day isn't a good life.","Build a habit, not a one-off."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","We are what we repeatedly do; excellence is a habit, not an act (popular paraphrase).","What is excellence, per the Aristotelian idea?","A habit.","Repeat one excellent action."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Courage is acting rightly despite fear, not the absence of fear.","What is courage, per Aristotle?","Acting rightly despite fear.","Do one thing you fear but know is right."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","A friend is a second self; we see ourselves more clearly through friends.","What did Aristotle call a friend?","A second self.","Ask a friend for honest feedback."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Temperance means enjoying pleasures in the right amount, neither denying nor overindulging.","What is temperance?","Moderation in pleasures.","Enjoy a treat in moderation."],["L","w","Aristotle","Politics","Leisure is for learning, reflection and higher pursuits, not mere amusement.","What is leisure for?","Higher pursuits and reflection.","Spend leisure time learning."],["L","w","Aristotle","Rhetoric","Good speakers adapt to the audience's character and emotions.","What must speakers adapt to?","The audience.","Tailor your message to your listener."],["L","w","Aristotle","Poetics","Stories need a beginning, middle and end; a plot unifies events.","What does a plot need?","Beginning, middle, end.","Tell a story with a clear structure."],["L","w","Plato","Republic","Justice in the soul is when reason, spirit and appetite each do their own job.","What is justice in the soul?","Each part doing its proper role.","Let reason lead one decision."],["L","w","Plato","Republic","The Ring of Gyges asks: would you be just if you could never be caught?","What does the Ring of Gyges test?","Whether we'd be just if invisible.","Do the right thing when no one watches."],["L","w","Plato","Symposium","Love ascends from one beautiful body to beauty itself — from the particular to the ideal.","What is the ladder of love?","Ascent from particular beauty to beauty itself.","Appreciate beauty in nature today."],["L","w","Plato","Crito","Socrates refused to escape prison, keeping his commitment to the laws.","Why did Socrates refuse escape?","To keep his commitment to the laws.","Keep a commitment even when inconvenient."],["L","w","Plato","Gorgias","It is better to suffer injustice than to commit it.","What did Socrates say about injustice?","Better to suffer than commit it.","Refuse to retaliate today."],["L","w","Plato","Meno","Learning is like recollection; questions draw out knowledge we already have.","What is learning, in Meno?","Recollection.","Teach someone by asking questions."],["L","w","Plato","Phaedo","Philosophy is practice for dying — freeing the soul from bodily attachments.","What is philosophy practice for, per Phaedo?","Dying.","Let go of one attachment."],["L","w","Socrates (via Xenophon)","Memorabilia","Socrates said the less we need, the closer we are to the gods.","What brings us closer to the gods?","Needing less.","Go without something today."],["L","w","Xenophon","Memorabilia","Socrates trained his body as well as his mind; fitness serves clear thinking.","Why did Socrates train his body?","Fitness supports clear thinking.","Exercise for 20 minutes."],["L","w","Xenophon","Oeconomicus","Order and organisation make a household beautiful and efficient.","What makes a household efficient?","Order.","Organise one drawer or cupboard."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","To know you have enough is to be rich.","Who is rich, per Lao Tzu?","One who knows they have enough.","Say 'I have enough' about one thing."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","The sage does not hoard; the more he gives, the more he has.","What happens when the sage gives?","He has more.","Give generously today."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Act without striving; let things happen naturally (wu wei).","What is wu wei?","Effortless action.","Let one thing unfold without forcing."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Great acts are made of small deeds; difficult things begin as easy.","What are great acts made of?","Small deeds.","Do one small step toward a big goal."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Those who know don't speak; those who speak don't know.","What does Lao Tzu say about speakers?","Those who know don't speak.","Speak less in one meeting."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","The usefulness of a bowl is its emptiness. Space creates usefulness.","Why is a bowl useful?","Its emptiness.","Leave empty space in your schedule."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Mastering others requires force; mastering yourself requires strength.","What requires true strength?","Mastering yourself.","Control one impulse."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","When I let go of what I am, I become what I might be.","What happens when we let go of what we are?","We become what we might be.","Let go of one fixed self-image."],["L","w","Chuang Tzu","Zhuangzi","The useless tree survives because no carpenter wants it. Usefulness can bring harm.","What did the useless tree teach?","Usefulness can lead to destruction.","Rest without justifying it."],["L","w","Chuang Tzu","Zhuangzi","The butcher's knife stayed sharp for years because he cut along natural joints.","What kept the butcher's knife sharp?","Cutting along natural joints.","Work with, not against, the grain of a problem."],["L","w","Chuang Tzu","Zhuangzi","Did I dream I was a butterfly, or am I a butterfly dreaming I'm a man? Hold identities lightly.","What is the butterfly dream about?","The uncertainty of identity and reality.","Hold one belief more lightly."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","The superior man is modest in speech but exceeds in actions.","How does the superior man behave?","Modest in speech, exceeding in action.","Do more than you say today."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","Real knowledge is knowing the extent of one's ignorance.","What is real knowledge?","Knowing the extent of one's ignorance.","Admit one thing you don't know."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","By three methods we learn wisdom: reflection, imitation and experience.","What are the three methods of wisdom?","Reflection, imitation, experience.","Use reflection tonight."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","The man who moves a mountain begins by carrying small stones.","How is a mountain moved?","By carrying small stones.","Carry one small stone toward a big goal."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","Respect yourself and others will respect you.","What earns respect?","Self-respect.","Act with self-respect today."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","Before you embark on revenge, dig two graves.","What does revenge cost?","It harms you too.","Let go of a grudge."],["L","w","Mencius","Mencius","Human nature is good, like water flowing downhill. Cultivate the sprouts of virtue.","What did Mencius say about human nature?","It is good.","Nurture one good impulse."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Better than a thousand hollow words is one word that brings peace.","What is better than a thousand hollow words?","One word that brings peace.","Say one calming word to someone."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Conquer anger with love, evil with good, meanness with generosity, lies with truth.","How is anger conquered?","With love.","Respond to meanness with generosity."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","One who conquers himself is greater than one who conquers a thousand men in battle.","Who is the greatest conqueror?","One who conquers himself.","Master one impulse today."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","As rain breaks through an ill-thatched house, passion breaks through an unreflecting mind.","What protects the mind?","Reflection.","Reflect before indulging."],["L","w","Buddha","Sutta Nipata","Craving is the root of suffering; release from craving brings peace.","What is the root of suffering?","Craving.","Notice one craving and let it pass."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Health is the greatest gift, contentment the greatest wealth.","What is the greatest wealth?","Contentment.","List what you're content with."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Drop by drop the water pot is filled. Good is accumulated little by little.","How is the pot filled?","Drop by drop.","Do one small good deed."],["L","w","Shantideva","The Way of the Bodhisattva","You can't cover the earth in leather, but you can wear sandals. Change your mind instead of the world.","What does the leather analogy teach?","Change your mind rather than the world.","Adjust your attitude to an annoyance."],["L","w","Thich Nhat Hanh","The Miracle of Mindfulness","Wash the dishes to wash the dishes. Full presence turns chores into meditation.","What does it mean to wash dishes to wash dishes?","Doing chores with full presence.","Do one chore mindfully."],["L","w","Thich Nhat Hanh","Peace Is Every Step","Breathing in, I calm my body; breathing out, I smile.","What is a simple mindfulness practice?","Conscious breathing with a smile.","Take three mindful breaths."],["L","w","Bhagavad Gita","Bhagavad Gita","You have a right to your actions, but not to their fruits. Act without attachment to results.","What do we have a right to, per the Gita?","Our actions, not their fruits.","Do your best work without worrying about praise."],["L","w","Bhagavad Gita","Bhagavad Gita","Better to do your own duty imperfectly than another's perfectly.","Whose duty should you do?","Your own.","Focus on your own responsibilities."],["L","w","Bhagavad Gita","Bhagavad Gita","The mind is restless, but it can be controlled by practice and detachment.","How is the mind controlled?","Practice and detachment.","Meditate for five minutes."],["L","w","Bhagavad Gita","Bhagavad Gita","Be even-minded in success and failure; that evenness is yoga.","What is yoga, per the Gita?","Evenness of mind.","Respond equally calmly to good and bad news."],["L","w","Rumi","Masnavi","The wound is the place where the light enters you.","What does Rumi say about wounds?","Light enters through them.","Find a lesson in a past hurt."],["L","w","Rumi","Masnavi","Yesterday I was clever and wanted to change the world; today I am wise and change myself.","What did Rumi say about changing the world?","Wise people change themselves.","Change one personal habit."],["L","w","Kahlil Gibran","The Prophet","Work is love made visible.","What did Gibran call work?","Love made visible.","Do today's work with love."],["L","w","Kahlil Gibran","The Prophet","Your children are not your children; they come through you but not from you.","What did Gibran say of children?","They come through you, not from you.","Encourage someone's independence."],["L","w","Kahlil Gibran","The Prophet","Let there be spaces in your togetherness.","What should be in togetherness?","Spaces.","Give a loved one room."],["L","w","Kahlil Gibran","The Prophet","The deeper sorrow carves into your being, the more joy you can contain.","How does sorrow relate to joy?","Sorrow deepens our capacity for joy.","Remember a sorrow that deepened you."],["L","w","Thomas à Kempis","The Imitation of Christ","Be not angry that you cannot make others as you wish, since you cannot make yourself as you wish.","Why not be angry at others?","We cannot even make ourselves as we wish.","Forgive someone's flaw."],["L","w","Thomas à Kempis","The Imitation of Christ","Occasions of adversity best show us how much virtue we have.","What shows our virtue?","Adversity.","Treat today's challenge as a test."],["L","w","Augustine","Confessions","Our hearts are restless until they find rest in something greater.","What did Augustine say about restless hearts?","They seek rest in something greater.","Reflect on what gives you rest."],["L","w","Augustine","Confessions","Augustine prayed 'give me chastity, but not yet' — we postpone virtue.","What did Augustine's 'not yet' show?","Our tendency to postpone virtue.","Start a good habit today, not tomorrow."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","The greatest thing in the world is to know how to belong to oneself.","What is the greatest thing, per Montaigne?","Knowing how to belong to oneself.","Spend an hour on your own terms."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","When I play with my cat, who knows whether she is amusing herself with me more than I with her?","What does the cat remark teach?","Humility about our perspective.","Consider a situation from another's view."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","We must reserve a back room all our own, entirely free, for our true liberty.","What is Montaigne's 'back room'?","An inner space of freedom.","Create a quiet corner or time for yourself."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","No man is a hero to his valet; intimacy reveals ordinary humanity.","What does intimacy reveal?","Ordinary humanity.","Be kind about a loved one's flaws."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","Habit is a violent schoolmistress; it gradually establishes authority over us.","What did Montaigne say about habit?","It gains authority gradually.","Notice a habit that controls you."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","A wise man sees as much as he ought, not as much as he can.","What does the wise man see?","As much as he ought.","Ignore one unnecessary piece of news."],["L","w","Blaise Pascal","Pensées","The heart has its reasons which reason knows nothing of.","What does the heart have?","Reasons reason doesn't know.","Honour a feeling you can't explain."],["L","w","Blaise Pascal","Pensées","Man is a thinking reed — fragile, yet noble because he thinks.","Why is man noble, per Pascal?","Because he thinks.","Spend 10 minutes thinking deeply."],["L","w","Blaise Pascal","Pensées","We never live in the present; we anticipate the future or recall the past.","Where do we rarely live?","The present.","Notice when you drift from the present."],["L","w","La Rochefoucauld","Maxims","We all have strength enough to endure the misfortunes of others.","What did La Rochefoucauld say we can easily endure?","Others' misfortunes.","Show real sympathy to someone struggling."],["L","w","La Rochefoucauld","Maxims","Self-interest speaks all languages and plays all roles, even disinterest.","What does self-interest disguise itself as?","Disinterest.","Check your motives in one generous act."],["L","w","La Rochefoucauld","Maxims","We are never so easily deceived as when we imagine we are deceiving others.","When are we easily deceived?","When we think we deceive others.","Be honest in one small thing."],["L","w","La Bruyère","Characters","Most men use the first part of life to make the last part miserable.","What did La Bruyère say about youth?","Many waste it making later life miserable.","Make one choice your future self will thank you for."],["L","w","Voltaire","Candide","We must cultivate our garden: practical work beats idle speculation.","What is Candide's conclusion?","Cultivate our garden.","Do practical work at home."],["L","w","Voltaire","Philosophical Dictionary","The best is the enemy of the good. Perfectionism blocks progress.","What is the enemy of the good?","The best.","Finish something good enough."],["L","w","Jean-Jacques Rousseau","Emile","Patience is bitter but its fruit sweet; children learn by experience, not lectures.","How should children learn, per Rousseau?","By experience.","Let someone learn by doing."],["L","w","Adam Smith","The Theory of Moral Sentiments","Imagine an impartial spectator watching you; behave as they'd approve.","What is the impartial spectator?","An imagined fair observer judging our conduct.","Ask what an impartial observer would think of your actions."],["L","w","Adam Smith","The Theory of Moral Sentiments","Happiness consists in tranquillity and enjoyment, not in endless ambition.","What brings happiness, per Smith?","Tranquillity and enjoyment.","Enjoy a quiet moment without ambition."],["L","w","David Hume","Essays","Be a philosopher, but amid all your philosophy, be still a man.","What did Hume advise philosophers?","Remain human.","Balance study with friendship today."],["L","w","Immanuel Kant","Groundwork","Act only by rules you could will to be universal laws.","What is the categorical imperative?","Act only on maxims you'd make universal laws.","Ask: what if everyone did this?"],["L","w","Immanuel Kant","Groundwork","Treat people always as ends, never merely as means.","How should people be treated?","As ends, never merely as means.","Treat a service worker as a person, not a function."],["L","w","Immanuel Kant","What Is Enlightenment?","Dare to know: have courage to use your own understanding.","What is the motto of enlightenment?","Dare to know.","Form your own view on one issue."],["L","w","Arthur Schopenhauer","The Wisdom of Life","We seldom think of what we have but always of what we lack.","What do we seldom think of?","What we have.","List five things you have."],["L","w","Arthur Schopenhauer","Essays","Reading is thinking with someone else's head; think for yourself too.","What is reading, per Schopenhauer?","Thinking with someone else's head.","Reflect on a card in your own words."],["L","w","Arthur Schopenhauer","The Wisdom of Life","A cheerful disposition is the greatest asset for happiness.","What is the greatest asset for happiness?","Cheerfulness.","Choose cheerfulness in one task."],["L","w","Friedrich Nietzsche","Twilight of the Idols","What does not kill me makes me stronger.","What makes us stronger, per Nietzsche?","What doesn't kill us.","Face one discomfort."],["L","w","Friedrich Nietzsche","Thus Spoke Zarathustra","Become who you are: develop your unique potential.","What is Nietzsche's call?","Become who you are.","Do one thing true to yourself."],["L","w","Friedrich Nietzsche","The Gay Science","Amor fati: love your fate, including its hardships.","What is amor fati?","Love of one's fate.","Embrace a setback as necessary."],["L","w","Friedrich Nietzsche","The Gay Science","Imagine living your life over and over eternally. Would you rejoice?","What is the eternal recurrence test?","Would you embrace living this life eternally?","Make today one you'd happily repeat."],["L","w","Friedrich Nietzsche","Twilight of the Idols","He who has a why to live can bear almost any how.","What can bear almost any how?","One with a why.","Write your why."],["L","w","Søren Kierkegaard","Journals","Life can only be understood backwards, but must be lived forwards.","How is life understood and lived?","Understood backwards, lived forwards.","Make a decision without needing total certainty."],["L","w","Søren Kierkegaard","Either/Or","Anxiety is the dizziness of freedom.","What is anxiety, per Kierkegaard?","The dizziness of freedom.","Treat anxiety as a sign of choice."],["L","w","Søren Kierkegaard","Works of Love","Love believes all things yet is never deceived.","What does love do?","Believes all things.","Assume good intent once today."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","Crime and Punishment","Raskolnikov's guilt shows we cannot reason ourselves above conscience.","What does Raskolnikov's story show?","Conscience can't be reasoned away.","Listen to your conscience."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","The Brothers Karamazov","Each of us is responsible for everyone; small kindnesses matter.","What did Zosima teach about responsibility?","We're responsible for all.","Do a kindness for a stranger."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","Notes from Underground","Humans value free will so much they'll act irrationally to prove it.","What does the Underground Man insist on?","Free will even against reason.","Notice where you resist good advice out of pride."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","The Idiot","Beauty will save the world — compassion and goodness have power.","What will save the world?","Beauty.","Create or notice beauty today."],["L","w","Victor Hugo","Les Misérables","To love another person is to see the face of God.","What did Hugo say about love?","To love another is to see God's face.","Show love to someone."],["L","w","Victor Hugo","Les Misérables","Even the darkest night will end and the sun will rise.","What did Hugo say about the darkest night?","It ends and the sun rises.","Remember a past hard time that ended."],["L","w","Victor Hugo","Les Misérables","Javert's rigid sense of law, without mercy, destroyed him.","What destroyed Javert?","Rigid justice without mercy.","Show mercy in one judgement."],["L","w","Charles Dickens","A Christmas Carol","Scrooge transformed by facing past, present and future consequences of his life.","What changed Scrooge?","Seeing the consequences of his life.","Imagine your future if habits continue."],["L","w","Charles Dickens","David Copperfield","Annual income twenty pounds, expenditure nineteen — happiness; expenditure twenty-one — misery.","What is Micawber's rule?","Spend less than you earn.","Check that spending is below income."],["L","w","Charles Dickens","Great Expectations","Pip learns that loyalty and humility matter more than social status.","What does Pip learn?","Loyalty and humility outrank status.","Thank someone who helped you early."],["L","w","Jane Austen","Pride and Prejudice","First impressions mislead; pride and prejudice blind us.","What blinds Elizabeth and Darcy?","Pride and prejudice.","Revisit a first impression."],["L","w","Jane Austen","Sense and Sensibility","Balance feeling with judgement; neither alone suffices.","What must be balanced?","Sense and sensibility.","Weigh both feelings and facts in a decision."],["L","w","George Eliot","Middlemarch","The growing good of the world depends partly on unhistoric acts of quiet lives.","What does the world's good depend on?","Unhistoric acts.","Do a quiet good deed."],["L","w","Herman Melville","Moby-Dick","Ahab's obsession destroyed him and his crew. Fixation can sink you.","What destroyed Ahab?","Obsession.","Drop an unhealthy fixation."],["L","w","Mark Twain","Following the Equator","Twenty years from now you'll regret what you didn't do more than what you did.","What will you regret most?","What you didn't do.","Do one thing you've wanted to try."],["L","w","Mark Twain","Notebooks","The secret of getting ahead is getting started.","What is the secret of getting ahead?","Getting started.","Start a task you've delayed."],["L","w","Mark Twain","Pudd'nhead Wilson","Courage is resistance to fear, mastery of fear — not absence of fear.","What is courage, per Twain?","Mastery of fear.","Face one fear."],["L","w","Ernest Hemingway","The Old Man and the Sea","A man can be destroyed but not defeated.","What did Santiago say?","A man can be destroyed but not defeated.","Keep going after a setback."],["L","w","Ernest Hemingway","A Farewell to Arms","The world breaks everyone; afterwards many are strong at the broken places.","What happens at broken places?","Many become strong there.","Recall a strength from a past break."],["L","w","Hermann Hesse","Siddhartha","Siddhartha said he could think, wait and fast — patience and self-control are powers.","What three skills did Siddhartha have?","Think, wait, fast.","Practise waiting patiently today."],["L","w","Hermann Hesse","Siddhartha","Wisdom cannot be taught; it must be lived and experienced.","How is wisdom gained, per Siddhartha?","Through experience.","Learn by doing today."],["L","w","Hermann Hesse","Siddhartha","The river teaches that everything returns and flows; listen to it.","What did the river teach?","Everything flows and returns.","Sit by water or listen to nature."],["L","w","Rainer Maria Rilke","Letters to a Young Poet","Be patient toward all that is unsolved in your heart; live the questions.","What did Rilke advise about questions?","Live them.","Write a question you'll live with."],["L","w","Rainer Maria Rilke","Letters to a Young Poet","Go into yourself; ask if you must create. Inner necessity matters.","What should a young poet ask?","Whether they must write.","Ask what you must do in life."],["L","w","Rainer Maria Rilke","Letters to a Young Poet","Solitude is not to be feared; it is where we grow.","What is solitude for?","Growth.","Spend 20 minutes alone."],["L","w","Bertrand Russell","The Conquest of Happiness","Zest — wide interests and friendly curiosity — is a key to happiness.","What is a key to happiness, per Russell?","Zest and wide interests.","Explore one new interest."],["L","w","Bertrand Russell","The Conquest of Happiness","Fatigue and worry come from thinking about problems at the wrong time. Think hard at the right time, then stop.","When should problems be thought about?","At the proper time, not continually.","Set a 'worry time' and stick to it."],["L","w","Bertrand Russell","In Praise of Idleness","Leisure is essential for civilisation; too much work crowds out living.","What did Russell praise?","Idleness and leisure.","Take an hour off guilt-free."],["L","w","Bertrand Russell","The Conquest of Happiness","Envy is one of the most potent causes of unhappiness. Compare less.","What is a potent cause of unhappiness?","Envy.","Stop one comparison today."],["L","w","Albert Camus","The Myth of Sisyphus","One must imagine Sisyphus happy: meaning comes from embracing the struggle.","How should we imagine Sisyphus?","Happy.","Find satisfaction in a repetitive task."],["L","w","Albert Camus","Return to Tipasa","In the midst of winter, I found there was within me an invincible summer.","What did Camus find in winter?","An invincible summer within.","Recall your inner strength."],["L","w","Albert Camus","The Plague","Decency is doing your job well in a crisis.","What is decency, per Dr Rieux?","Doing your job.","Do your duty well today."],["L","w","Jean-Paul Sartre","Existentialism Is a Humanism","Existence precedes essence: we create ourselves by our choices.","What does 'existence precedes essence' mean?","We define ourselves through choices.","Make one choice that defines you."],["L","w","Simone de Beauvoir","The Ethics of Ambiguity","Freedom is realised by willing freedom for others too.","How is freedom realised?","By willing others' freedom.","Support someone else's choice."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","The mass of men lead lives of quiet desperation. Live deliberately instead.","How do most men live, per Thoreau?","In quiet desperation.","Do one deliberate thing."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","If one advances confidently toward his dreams, he will meet with unexpected success.","What happens when you advance toward your dreams?","Unexpected success.","Take a step toward a dream."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","Rather than love, money or fame, give me truth.","What did Thoreau value above love, money and fame?","Truth.","Speak a truth gently."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walking","In wildness is the preservation of the world. Nature restores the mind.","What preserves the world?","Wildness.","Walk in nature."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Civil Disobedience","Follow conscience over unjust law.","What should guide us over unjust law?","Conscience.","Stand up for a principle."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Self-Reliance","Insist on yourself; never imitate.","What did Emerson insist on?","Being yourself.","Express your genuine opinion."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Self-Reliance","To be great is to be misunderstood.","What did Emerson say greatness involves?","Being misunderstood.","Accept being misunderstood once."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Friendship","The only way to have a friend is to be one.","How do you have a friend?","Be one.","Be a friend to someone today."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Nature","In the woods, we return to reason and faith. Nature heals.","What happens in the woods?","We return to reason and faith.","Spend time among trees."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Essays","Nothing great was ever achieved without enthusiasm.","What is required for great achievement?","Enthusiasm.","Bring enthusiasm to one task."],["L","w","Walt Whitman","Leaves of Grass","I contain multitudes. It's fine to be complex and changing.","What did Whitman contain?","Multitudes.","Accept one contradiction in yourself."],["L","w","Walt Whitman","Leaves of Grass","Keep your face always toward the sunshine — and shadows will fall behind you.","What happens if you face the sunshine?","Shadows fall behind you.","Focus on one positive today."],["L","w","William James","The Principles of Psychology","The greatest discovery is that a person can alter their life by altering their attitudes.","What is the greatest discovery, per James?","Changing attitudes changes life.","Change one attitude."],["L","w","William James","The Will to Believe","Believe life is worth living, and your belief will help create the fact.","What can belief do?","Help create the fact.","Act as if your effort matters."],["L","w","William James","Varieties of Religious Experience","The art of being wise is knowing what to overlook.","What is the art of being wise?","Knowing what to overlook.","Overlook a minor annoyance."],["L","w","John Stuart Mill","Autobiography","Ask yourself whether you are happy and you cease to be. Aim at something else.","How is happiness found, per Mill?","By aiming at something else.","Focus on a purpose rather than happiness."],["L","w","John Stuart Mill","On Liberty","He who knows only his own side of the case knows little of that.","What does one-sided knowledge yield?","Little understanding.","Read a view you disagree with."],["L","w","John Stuart Mill","Utilitarianism","Better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied.","What did Mill prefer?","Socrates dissatisfied over a fool satisfied.","Choose a higher pleasure."],["L","w","James Allen","As a Man Thinketh","Man is the master of his thoughts, moulder of character, maker of circumstances.","What does Allen say we are masters of?","Our thoughts.","Choose one empowering thought."],["L","w","James Allen","As a Man Thinketh","Circumstances do not make the man; they reveal him.","What do circumstances do?","Reveal the man.","Show your character in a difficulty."],["L","w","James Allen","As a Man Thinketh","The mind is like a garden: it will grow weeds if not cultivated.","What is the mind compared to?","A garden.","Weed out one negative thought."],["L","w","James Allen","As a Man Thinketh","Calmness of mind is one of the beautiful jewels of wisdom.","What is a jewel of wisdom?","Calmness.","Stay calm in one tense moment."],["L","w","Booker T. Washington","Up from Slavery","Success is measured by obstacles overcome, not position reached.","How is success measured, per Washington?","By obstacles overcome.","Note one obstacle you've overcome."],["L","w","Booker T. Washington","Up from Slavery","Dignity of labour: no work is beneath us when done well.","What did Washington teach about labour?","All work done well has dignity.","Do a humble task well."],["L","w","Frederick Douglass","Narrative","Knowledge is the pathway from slavery to freedom.","What is the pathway to freedom?","Knowledge.","Learn something valuable today."],["L","w","Helen Keller","The Story of My Life","Life is either a daring adventure or nothing.","What is life, per Keller?","A daring adventure or nothing.","Do something adventurous."],["L","w","Helen Keller","Optimism","Optimism is the faith that leads to achievement.","What leads to achievement?","Optimism.","Expect a good outcome."],["L","w","Anne Frank","The Diary of a Young Girl","How wonderful it is that nobody need wait a single moment before starting to improve the world.","When can we start improving the world?","Immediately.","Improve something now."],["L","d","Theodore Roosevelt","Citizenship in a Republic","Credit belongs to the man in the arena who strives, not the critic.","Who deserves credit?","The one in the arena.","Try something despite critics."],["L","d","Theodore Roosevelt","The Strenuous Life","A life of effort and challenge is better than ease.","What life did Roosevelt praise?","The strenuous life.","Do a hard physical activity."],["L","d","Theodore Roosevelt","Attributed sayings","Comparison is the thief of joy.","What steals joy?","Comparison.","Stop comparing today."],["L","w","Abraham Lincoln","Attributed sayings","Give me six hours to chop a tree and I'll spend four sharpening the axe.","What did Lincoln say about the axe?","Spend most time sharpening.","Prepare well before a task."],["L","w","Abraham Lincoln","Letters","Write angry letters but don't send them. Lincoln wrote 'hot letters' and filed them.","What did Lincoln do with angry letters?","Wrote and didn't send them.","Write an angry message and delete it."],["L","w","Abraham Lincoln","Speeches","Folks are usually about as happy as they make up their minds to be.","How happy are people, per Lincoln?","As happy as they decide.","Decide to be happy today."],["L","w","Winston Churchill","Speeches","Success is not final, failure is not fatal; the courage to continue counts.","What counts, per Churchill?","The courage to continue.","Continue after a failure."],["L","w","Winston Churchill","Attributed sayings","If you're going through hell, keep going.","What to do if going through hell?","Keep going.","Keep moving through a hard patch."],["L","w","Winston Churchill","Painting as a Pastime","Change is the master key; rest the mind by using a different part of it.","How does Churchill rest the mind?","By switching activities.","Take up a hobby for an hour."],["L","w","Nelson Mandela","Long Walk to Freedom","Resentment is like drinking poison and hoping it kills your enemies.","What is resentment like?","Drinking poison hoping it kills your enemy.","Release one resentment."],["L","w","Nelson Mandela","Long Walk to Freedom","It always seems impossible until it's done.","What seems impossible?","Everything, until it's done.","Attempt something that seems impossible."],["L","w","Mahatma Gandhi","Autobiography","Be the change you wish to see (popular paraphrase of his teaching).","What does Gandhi's teaching urge?","Embody the change you want.","Model one behaviour you want from others."],["L","w","Mahatma Gandhi","Autobiography","Happiness is when what you think, say and do are in harmony.","What is happiness, per Gandhi?","Harmony of thought, word and deed.","Align one action with your words."],["L","w","Martin Luther King Jr.","Strength to Love","Darkness cannot drive out darkness; only light can.","What drives out darkness?","Light.","Answer negativity with positivity."],["L","w","Martin Luther King Jr.","Speeches","Take the first step in faith; you don't have to see the whole staircase.","What don't you need to see?","The whole staircase.","Take a first step without a full plan."],["L","w","Mother Teresa","Speeches","Not all of us can do great things, but we can do small things with great love.","What can we all do?","Small things with great love.","Do a small thing with great love."],["L","w","Albert Schweitzer","Out of My Life and Thought","Reverence for life: kindness to all living things.","What is Schweitzer's ethic?","Reverence for life.","Be kind to an animal or plant."],["L","w","Albert Einstein","The World As I See It","Life is like riding a bicycle: to keep balance, keep moving.","How is life like a bicycle?","You must keep moving.","Keep moving on a project."],["L","w","Albert Einstein","Letters","Strive not to be a success but to be of value.","What should we strive for?","To be of value.","Do something valuable for someone."],["L","w","Marie Curie","Pierre Curie","Nothing in life is to be feared, only understood.","What should we do instead of fear?","Understand.","Learn about one fear."],["L","w","Leonardo da Vinci","Notebooks","Simplicity is the ultimate sophistication.","What is the ultimate sophistication?","Simplicity.","Simplify one thing."],["L","w","Leonardo da Vinci","Notebooks","Learning never exhausts the mind.","What never exhausts the mind?","Learning.","Learn for 15 minutes."],["L","w","Leonardo da Vinci","Notebooks","Iron rusts from disuse; the mind withers without exercise.","What happens to an unused mind?","It withers.","Do a mental challenge."],["L","w","Isaac Newton","Letters","If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders of giants.","How did Newton see further?","By standing on giants' shoulders.","Thank a mentor."],["L","d","M. Scott Peck","The Road Less Traveled","Life is difficult. Once we accept this truly, it no longer matters as much.","What is Peck's opening truth?","Life is difficult.","Accept one difficulty without complaint."],["L","d","M. Scott Peck","The Road Less Traveled","Discipline means delaying gratification, accepting responsibility, dedication to truth and balancing.","What are Peck's four tools of discipline?","Delay gratification, responsibility, truth, balance.","Delay one gratification today."],["L","d","M. Scott Peck","The Road Less Traveled","Love is the will to extend oneself for another's spiritual growth.","How does Peck define love?","Will to extend oneself for another's growth.","Support someone's growth."],["L","w","Erich Fromm","The Art of Loving","Love is an art requiring knowledge, effort, discipline and patience, not just a feeling.","What is love, per Fromm?","An art requiring practice.","Practise one act of attentive love."],["L","w","Erich Fromm","To Have or To Be?","Being-mode — living, sharing, growing — brings more fulfilment than having-mode.","What are Fromm's two modes?","Having and being.","Choose an experience over a purchase."],["L","w","Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi","Flow","Flow arises when challenge matches skill and attention is fully absorbed.","When does flow occur?","When challenge matches skill.","Find a task that fully absorbs you."],["L","w","Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi","Flow","The best moments usually occur when body or mind is stretched to its limits voluntarily.","When do the best moments occur?","When stretched voluntarily.","Push yourself in a chosen activity."],["L","w","Martin Seligman","Learned Optimism","Pessimists see setbacks as permanent, pervasive and personal; optimists as temporary and specific.","What are the 3 Ps of pessimism?","Permanent, pervasive, personal.","Reframe a setback as temporary."],["L","w","Martin Seligman","Authentic Happiness","Use your signature strengths daily for lasting satisfaction.","What brings lasting satisfaction?","Using signature strengths.","Use your top strength today."],["L","w","Martin Seligman","Flourish","Write three good things each evening and why they happened.","What is the 'three blessings' exercise?","Writing three good things nightly.","Write three good things tonight."],["L","w","Jonathan Haidt","The Happiness Hypothesis","The mind is a rider on an elephant: reason guides, but emotion has the power.","What is the elephant and rider?","Emotion (elephant) and reason (rider).","Motivate your 'elephant' with a small reward."],["L","w","Jonathan Haidt","The Happiness Hypothesis","Happiness comes from between: relationships, work and something larger.","Where does happiness come from, per Haidt?","From between — connections.","Strengthen one connection."],["L","w","Brené Brown","The Gifts of Imperfection","Vulnerability is the birthplace of courage, connection and creativity.","What is vulnerability the birthplace of?","Courage, connection, creativity.","Share something honest."],["L","w","Brené Brown","Daring Greatly","Shame thrives on secrecy; speaking it shrinks it.","How is shame reduced?","By speaking about it.","Talk about a struggle with someone trusted."],["L","w","Don Miguel Ruiz","The Four Agreements","Be impeccable with your word.","What is the first agreement?","Be impeccable with your word.","Say only what you mean today."],["L","w","Don Miguel Ruiz","The Four Agreements","Don't take anything personally; others' actions reflect them.","What is the second agreement?","Don't take anything personally.","Let a rude remark pass."],["L","w","Don Miguel Ruiz","The Four Agreements","Don't make assumptions; ask questions.","What is the third agreement?","Don't make assumptions.","Ask instead of assuming once."],["L","w","Don Miguel Ruiz","The Four Agreements","Always do your best — which varies by day.","What is the fourth agreement?","Always do your best.","Do your best without self-judgement."],["L","w","Eckhart Tolle","The Power of Now","The present moment is all you ever have; most suffering comes from mind-time.","What is all you ever have?","The present moment.","Notice your breath for a minute."],["L","w","Eckhart Tolle","The Power of Now","Watch the thinker: you are not your thoughts.","What does 'watch the thinker' mean?","Observe thoughts without identifying.","Observe your thoughts for two minutes."],["L","w","Paulo Coelho","The Alchemist","When you want something, the universe conspires to help you — persistence opens doors.","What happens when you truly want something?","Things align to help you.","Take action on a dream."],["L","w","Paulo Coelho","The Alchemist","The treasure is often found where you started, after the journey teaches you.","Where was Santiago's treasure?","Where he started.","Appreciate what's at home."],["L","w","Robert Pirsig","Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintenance","Quality is found in caring about what you do, even fixing a bolt.","Where is Quality found?","In caring about what you do.","Do a repair with care."],["L","w","Robert Pirsig","Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintenance","Gumption — enthusiasm — is the fuel; watch for gumption traps like impatience.","What are gumption traps?","Things that drain enthusiasm.","Spot a gumption trap and step around it."],["L","w","Antoine de Saint-Exupéry","The Little Prince","It is only with the heart that one can see rightly; what is essential is invisible to the eye.","What is invisible to the eye?","What is essential.","Notice an invisible value in someone."],["L","w","Antoine de Saint-Exupéry","The Little Prince","You become responsible forever for what you have tamed.","What are we responsible for?","What we have tamed.","Care for a relationship you've built."],["L","w","Antoine de Saint-Exupéry","Wind, Sand and Stars","Perfection is achieved when there is nothing left to take away.","When is perfection achieved?","When nothing is left to take away.","Remove one unnecessary thing."],["L","d","Jordan Peterson","12 Rules for Life","Compare yourself to who you were yesterday, not to others.","Whom should you compare yourself to?","Who you were yesterday.","Improve on yesterday in one way."],["L","d","Jordan Peterson","12 Rules for Life","Set your house in order before criticising the world.","What should come before criticising the world?","Setting your house in order.","Tidy one area of your life."],["L","d","Jordan Peterson","12 Rules for Life","Stand up straight with your shoulders back; posture affects confidence.","What is Rule 1?","Stand up straight.","Check your posture now."],["L","w","Mark Manson","The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F*ck","Choose what to care about; you can't care about everything.","What is Manson's core idea?","Choose what to care about.","Stop caring about one trivial thing."],["L","w","Mark Manson","The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F*ck","Happiness comes from solving problems, not avoiding them.","Where does happiness come from, per Manson?","Solving problems.","Solve one nagging problem."],["L","d","David Goggins","Can't Hurt Me","When you think you're done, you're only at 40% of your capacity.","What is the 40% rule?","When you think you're done, you're at 40%.","Push a bit further in your workout."],["L","d","David Goggins","Can't Hurt Me","Keep a 'cookie jar' of past victories to draw strength from.","What is the cookie jar?","A mental list of past victories.","Write three past victories."],["L","d","David Goggins","Can't Hurt Me","The accountability mirror: face yourself honestly and set goals on sticky notes.","What is the accountability mirror?","Honest self-confrontation with written goals.","Write a goal on your mirror."],["L","d","Peter Attia","Outlive","Train for the 'centenarian decathlon': fitness now determines what you can do at 90.","What is the centenarian decathlon?","Training for the tasks you want to do in old age.","Do strength training today."],["L","d","Peter Attia","Outlive","Zone 2 cardio builds metabolic health; a few hours a week pays off for life.","What does zone 2 cardio build?","Metabolic health.","Do 30 minutes of easy cardio."],["L","d","Matthew Walker","Why We Sleep","Sleep is the single most effective thing for brain and body health each day.","What is the most effective daily health practice?","Sleep.","Go to bed at a fixed time tonight."],["L","d","Matthew Walker","Why We Sleep","Keep a regular sleep schedule, and a cool, dark bedroom.","What helps sleep?","Regularity, cool and dark room.","Make your bedroom darker."],["L","d","Yamamoto Tsunetomo","Hagakure","Matters of great concern should be treated lightly; small matters seriously.","How should great and small matters be treated?","Great lightly, small seriously.","Take a small task seriously."],["L","d","Yamamoto Tsunetomo","Hagakure","Live each day as if already dead — free of fear, fully committed.","What does Hagakure advise about death?","Live as if already dead.","Act without fear today."],["L","d","Yamamoto Tsunetomo","Hagakure","Decide within seven breaths. Hesitation is weakness.","How quickly should a samurai decide?","Within seven breaths.","Make a quick decision."],["L","d","Inazo Nitobe","Bushido","Rectitude, courage, benevolence, politeness, honesty, honour and loyalty form the samurai code.","Name three Bushido virtues.","e.g. rectitude, courage, benevolence.","Practise politeness today."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Accept everything just the way it is.","What is Musashi's first precept?","Accept everything as it is.","Accept a situation without complaining."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Think lightly of yourself and deeply of the world.","How should we think of ourselves and the world?","Lightly of self, deeply of world.","Focus on others today."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Do not be jealous; jealousy wastes energy.","What does Musashi advise against?","Jealousy.","Congratulate someone sincerely."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Do not fear death; fear of death clouds judgement.","Why not fear death?","Fear clouds judgement.","Reflect calmly on mortality."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","Dokkodo","Never stray from the Way.","What should never be strayed from?","The Way.","Stay true to your path today."],["L","d","Takuan Sōhō","The Unfettered Mind","Don't let the mind stop on anything; a mind that stops is caught.","What happens when the mind stops?","It gets caught.","Let a thought pass without dwelling."],["L","w","Shunryu Suzuki","Zen Mind, Beginner's Mind","In the beginner's mind there are many possibilities; in the expert's few.","What does beginner's mind offer?","Many possibilities.","Approach a familiar task freshly."],["L","w","Shunryu Suzuki","Zen Mind, Beginner's Mind","When you do something, burn yourself completely, like a good bonfire.","How should we act?","Completely.","Give one task everything."],["L","w","Alan Watts","The Wisdom of Insecurity","Trying to secure ourselves against life makes us anxious. Accept uncertainty.","What does seeking total security create?","Anxiety.","Accept one uncertainty."],["L","w","Alan Watts","Lectures","Life is like music: the point is the playing, not reaching the end.","What is life like, per Watts?","Music — the point is the playing.","Enjoy the process of a task."],["L","w","Joseph Campbell","The Power of Myth","Follow your bliss: doors open where there were none.","What did Campbell advise?","Follow your bliss.","Spend time on what lights you up."],["L","w","Joseph Campbell","The Hero with a Thousand Faces","The cave you fear to enter holds the treasure you seek.","What holds the treasure?","The cave you fear.","Enter one 'cave' you've avoided."],["L","w","C.S. Lewis","Mere Christianity","Humility is not thinking less of yourself but thinking of yourself less.","What is humility, per Lewis?","Thinking of yourself less.","Focus on others in a conversation."],["L","w","C.S. Lewis","The Four Loves","Friendship is born when one person says to another: 'What! You too?'","When is friendship born?","At 'What! You too?'","Share an interest with someone."],["L","w","J.R.R. Tolkien","The Lord of the Rings","All we have to decide is what to do with the time given to us.","What must we decide, per Gandalf?","What to do with our time.","Decide how to use one free hour."],["L","w","J.R.R. Tolkien","The Lord of the Rings","Not all who wander are lost.","What does Tolkien say about wanderers?","Not all are lost.","Explore without a fixed goal."],["L","w","John Gottman","The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work","Couples who thrive turn toward each other's small bids for attention.","What are 'bids' in relationships?","Small attempts to connect.","Respond warmly to every bid from your partner today."],["L","w","John Gottman","The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work","Criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling predict breakups.","What are the Four Horsemen?","Criticism, contempt, defensiveness, stonewalling.","Replace criticism with a gentle request."],["L","w","John Gottman","The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work","Stable couples have about five positive interactions for each negative one.","What is the magic ratio?","5 positive to 1 negative.","Add five positive moments today."],["L","w","John Gottman","The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work","Build love maps: know your partner's world, worries and hopes.","What is a love map?","Knowledge of your partner's inner world.","Ask your partner about their current worries."],["L","w","Gary Chapman","The 5 Love Languages","People feel loved through words, time, gifts, service or touch.","Name the five love languages.","Words of affirmation, quality time, gifts, acts of service, touch.","Show love in your partner's language."],["L","w","Cicero","On Friendship","Friendship makes prosperity brighter and adversity lighter by sharing it.","What does friendship do to prosperity and adversity?","Brightens one, lightens the other.","Share good news with a friend."],["L","w","Cicero","On Friendship","Friendship can only exist between good people.","Between whom can true friendship exist?","Good people.","Be the friend you want."],["L","w","Cicero","On Duties","What is morally wrong can never be truly useful.","What can never be useful, per Cicero?","What is morally wrong.","Reject a shortcut that's wrong."],["L","w","Cicero","Tusculan Disputations","A room without books is like a body without a soul.","What is a room without books like?","A body without a soul.","Put a book where you'll see it."],["L","w","Cicero","On Old Age","Old men keep their minds if they keep their interest and industry.","How do the old keep their minds?","By staying engaged.","Keep learning something new."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","A soft answer turns away wrath.","What turns away wrath?","A soft answer.","Answer harshness softly."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","Go to the ant, sluggard; consider her ways and be wise — work in summer, store for winter.","What does the ant teach?","Diligence and preparation.","Prepare now for a slow season."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","Pride goes before destruction.","What goes before destruction?","Pride.","Check your pride in a dispute."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","As iron sharpens iron, so one person sharpens another.","What sharpens a person?","Another person.","Spend time with someone who challenges you."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","Where there is no vision, the people perish.","What happens without vision?","People perish.","Write your vision."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","Plans fail for lack of counsel; with many advisers they succeed.","Why do plans fail?","Lack of counsel.","Ask two people for advice."],["L","w","Proverbs","Book of Proverbs","Better a dry crust with peace than a house full of feasting with strife.","What is better than feasting with strife?","A dry crust with peace.","Choose peace over winning."],["L","w","Ecclesiastes","Ecclesiastes","Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with all your might.","How should we work, per Ecclesiastes?","With all our might.","Give full effort to one task."],["L","w","Ecclesiastes","Ecclesiastes","Two are better than one; if one falls, the other lifts him up.","Why are two better than one?","One lifts the other.","Help a friend up."],["L","w","Ecclesiastes","Ecclesiastes","Vanity of vanities — much striving is chasing the wind. Enjoy simple gifts.","What is much striving called?","Chasing the wind.","Enjoy food and work simply today."],["L","w","Job","Book of Job","Job lost everything yet kept integrity; suffering is not always punishment.","What does Job show?","Integrity in suffering.","Hold to integrity in a trial."],["L","w","Psalms","Psalms","Teach us to number our days, that we may gain a heart of wisdom.","Why number our days?","To gain wisdom.","Count how many weekends remain this year."],["L","w","Matthew","Sermon on the Mount","Do not worry about tomorrow; tomorrow will worry about itself.","What does the Sermon say about tomorrow?","Don't worry; tomorrow handles itself.","Focus only on today's tasks."],["L","w","Matthew","Sermon on the Mount","Build your house on rock, not sand — on lasting principles.","What should a house be built on?","Rock.","Strengthen one foundation in life."],["L","w","Luke","Gospel of Luke","To whom much is given, much is required.","What is required of those given much?","Much.","Use a gift to help others."],["L","w","Corinthians","1 Corinthians","Love is patient and kind; it keeps no record of wrongs.","What does love not keep?","A record of wrongs.","Forget one old wrong."],["L","w","Talmud","Pirkei Avot","Who is wise? One who learns from everyone.","Who is wise, per Pirkei Avot?","One who learns from everyone.","Learn from someone unexpected."],["L","w","Talmud","Pirkei Avot","Who is rich? One who rejoices in his portion.","Who is rich?","One content with their portion.","Rejoice in what you have."],["L","w","Talmud","Pirkei Avot","Who is strong? One who conquers his impulses.","Who is strong?","One who masters impulses.","Master one impulse."],["L","w","Talmud","Pirkei Avot","If I am not for myself, who will be? If I am only for myself, what am I? If not now, when?","What are Hillel's three questions?","Self, others, now.","Act on something now."],["L","w","Talmud","Pirkei Avot","Say little and do much.","What did Shammai advise?","Say little, do much.","Speak less, act more today."],["L","w","Maimonides","Guide for the Perplexed","Give a man a fish and you feed him for a day; teach him and you feed him for life (traditional saying).","Why teach rather than give?","It creates lasting independence.","Teach a skill instead of doing it for someone."],["L","w","Ibn Khaldun","Muqaddimah","Group solidarity (asabiyyah) builds dynasties; luxury erodes it.","What erodes group solidarity?","Luxury.","Strengthen team bonds over comfort."],["L","w","Omar Khayyam","Rubaiyat","The moving finger writes and moves on; nothing can cancel a line.","What does the moving finger teach?","The past cannot be changed.","Let go of a past mistake."],["L","w","Hafez","Divan","Even after all this time, the sun never says to the earth 'You owe me.'","What does the sun never say?","'You owe me.'","Give without keeping score."],["L","w","Saadi","Gulistan","Human beings are members of a whole; when one suffers, all feel it.","What did Saadi say of humanity?","We are members of a whole.","Help someone suffering."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Opportunities multiply as they are seized.","What happens when opportunities are seized?","They multiply.","Seize one small opportunity."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","He will win who knows when to fight and when not to fight.","Who wins?","One who knows when to fight.","Skip a fight not worth it."],["L","w","Epicurus","Letter to Menoeceus","Death is nothing to us: when we are, death is not; when death is, we are not.","Why is death nothing to us?","We never experience it.","Reflect calmly on mortality for a minute."],["L","w","Epicurus","Principal Doctrines","It's impossible to live pleasantly without living wisely, honourably and justly.","What is necessary for a pleasant life?","Living wisely, honourably, justly.","Act justly today."],["L","w","Epicurus","Vatican Sayings","It is not so much our friends' help that helps us, as the confidence of their help.","What helps us most about friends?","Confidence in their help.","Tell a friend you're there for them."],["L","w","Lucretius","On the Nature of Things","Nothing comes from nothing; everything follows natural laws.","What comes from nothing?","Nothing.","Study a natural process."],["L","d","Boethius","The Consolation of Philosophy","True happiness is not in fortune's gifts but in the highest good within.","Where is true happiness?","In the highest good, within.","Find joy independent of possessions."],["L","d","Boethius","The Consolation of Philosophy","Adversity reveals true friends; prosperity hides them.","What does adversity reveal?","True friends.","Thank a friend who stood by you."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Don't be ashamed to need help. Like a soldier storming a wall, you may need a hand.","What should we not be ashamed of?","Needing help.","Ask for help with one task."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Think of yourself as dead; now take what's left and live it properly.","What exercise does Marcus suggest?","Imagining yourself already dead.","Live the rest of today 'properly'."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Our life is what our thoughts make it.","What makes our life, per Marcus?","Our thoughts.","Notice your thoughts' effect on your mood."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Be like the rocky headland on which waves constantly break; it stands firm.","What image does Marcus use for steadiness?","A headland against waves.","Stand firm under pressure."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","From my grandfather: good morals and control of temper. Gratitude for our teachers.","How did Marcus begin Meditations?","Listing what he learned from others.","List what you learned from three people."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","It is ridiculous not to escape your own faults, which is possible, but to try to escape others', which isn't.","What is ridiculous, per Marcus?","Fleeing others' faults rather than our own.","Work on one of your own faults."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","The whole future lies in uncertainty: live immediately.","Why live immediately?","The future is uncertain.","Do something meaningful now."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Wherever there's a human being, there's an opportunity for kindness.","Where is there an opportunity for kindness?","Wherever there's a human.","Be kind to every person you meet."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","True happiness is to enjoy the present without anxious dependence on the future.","What is true happiness?","Enjoying the present.","Enjoy one moment fully."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Ask yourself daily: what weakness have I overcome today? What vice resisted?","What daily questions did Seneca ask?","What weakness overcome, what vice resisted.","Ask these tonight."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Withdraw into yourself as much as you can; associate with those who make you better.","Who should we associate with?","Those who make us better.","Plan time with an uplifting person."],["L","d","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life","Busy people are least able to live; true leisure is spent with philosophy.","Who is least able to live?","The busy.","Cancel one unnecessary commitment."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Who then is invincible? The one who is disturbed by nothing outside his will.","Who is invincible?","One undisturbed by externals.","Stay calm about something outside your control."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Be careful whom you associate with; it is human to imitate.","Why choose company carefully?","We imitate those around us.","Notice whom you imitate."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Set aside days to live on the scantiest food, and ask: 'Is this what I feared?'","What question follows voluntary poverty?","'Is this what I feared?'","Skip one comfort and notice you're fine."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Nothing is ours except time. Nature entrusted us with this fleeting thing.","What alone is ours?","Time.","Guard one hour from distractions."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","You must live for another if you wish to live for yourself.","How do we live for ourselves?","By living for others.","Do a favour for someone."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","A good mind possesses a kingdom.","What does a good mind possess?","A kingdom.","Invest in your mind today."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Every new beginning comes from some other beginning's end.","Where do beginnings come from?","Other endings.","Treat an ending as a beginning."],["L","d","Seneca","On Leisure","We have two commonwealths: our local community and the whole world. Serve both.","What are the two commonwealths?","Local and universal.","Help both a neighbour and a global cause."],["L","d","Seneca","Natural Questions","Our ancestors' discoveries were not final; much remains for future generations.","What did Seneca say of knowledge?","Much remains to discover.","Learn something new about the world."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","It is a rough road that leads to the heights of greatness.","What leads to greatness?","A rough road.","Take the harder right path today."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Look back over the past with its changing empires; you can foresee the future too.","What does history let us foresee?","The future's patterns.","Read history for 15 minutes."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","When you arise in the morning, think of what a privilege it is to be alive.","What should we think of upon waking?","The privilege of being alive.","Say thanks on waking."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Reject your sense of injury and the injury itself disappears.","How does injury disappear?","By rejecting the sense of injury.","Drop the feeling of being wronged."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","A cucumber is bitter? Throw it away. Briars? Step aside. Don't ask why they exist.","What does Marcus say about bitter cucumbers?","Throw them away; don't complain.","Step around an annoyance without complaint."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Whatever happens was prepared for you from eternity; accept it.","How should we view events?","As woven by fate.","Accept an event as part of the whole."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Pass through this brief life as nature demands, and end it gracefully, like an olive falling ripe.","How should life end, per Marcus?","Like a ripe olive falling.","Reflect on living well to the end."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Don't go on discussing what a good person is like — be one.","What should we do instead of discussing goodness?","Be good.","Do something good rather than discussing it."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Only the educated are free.","Who is free, per Epictetus?","The educated.","Study for 20 minutes."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Circumstances don't make the man; they reveal him to himself.","What do circumstances do, per Epictetus?","Reveal the man.","Notice what today's situation reveals about you."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Seek not that things happen as you wish but wish them as they happen.","What is Epictetus's maxim on events?","Wish things as they happen.","Embrace an unplanned event."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","If you wish to be a writer, write. Become by doing.","How do you become a writer?","By writing.","Do the thing you want to become."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Don't explain philosophy; eat like a philosopher, walk like one.","What should a philosopher do instead of lecturing?","Live it.","Live a principle silently."],["L","d","Hierocles","Fragments","Draw the circles of concern inward: treat strangers like cousins, cousins like siblings.","What did Hierocles propose?","Drawing outer circles inward.","Treat a stranger like family."],["L","d","Zeno of Citium","Fragments","Well-being is attained little by little, yet it is no little thing.","How is well-being attained?","Little by little.","Take one small step toward well-being."],["L","d","Cleanthes","Hymn to Zeus","Lead me, Fate, where I must go; I'll follow willingly — or be dragged.","What is the choice in Cleanthes' hymn?","Follow willingly or be dragged.","Accept a necessary task willingly."],["L","d","Diogenes","Diogenes Laertius, Lives","Diogenes threw away his cup on seeing a child drink from his hands — owning less is freedom.","Why did Diogenes discard his cup?","He realised he didn't need it.","Give away an unneeded item."],["L","d","Diogenes","Diogenes Laertius, Lives","Asked by Alexander what he wanted, Diogenes said: 'Stand out of my sunlight.'","What did Diogenes ask of Alexander?","To stand out of his sunlight.","Value simple pleasures over status."],["L","w","Heraclitus","Fragments","No man steps in the same river twice; everything flows.","What did Heraclitus say about rivers?","You can't step in the same river twice.","Notice how you've changed this year."],["L","w","Heraclitus","Fragments","Character is destiny.","What is destiny, per Heraclitus?","Character.","Strengthen one trait."],["L","w","Pythagoras","Golden Verses","Each night, review the day: what did I do wrong, what right, what left undone?","What three questions are in the Golden Verses?","What wrong, what right, what undone.","Do the nightly review tonight."],["L","w","Democritus","Fragments","Happiness resides not in possessions but in the soul.","Where does happiness reside?","In the soul.","Find joy in a non-material thing."],["L","w","Solon","Herodotus, Histories","Call no man happy until he is dead; fortune can change.","What did Solon tell Croesus?","Call no man happy until he is dead.","Stay humble in good times."],["L","w","Herodotus","Histories","Haste in every business brings failure.","What brings failure, per Herodotus?","Haste.","Slow down on one important task."],["L","w","Thucydides","History of the Peloponnesian War","The strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must. Build strength in calm.","What did the Athenians tell the Melians?","The strong do what they can.","Build your reserves."],["L","w","Thucydides","History of the Peloponnesian War","The secret of happiness is freedom, and the secret of freedom is courage.","What is the secret of freedom?","Courage.","Act courageously once today."],["L","d","Homer","The Odyssey","Odysseus had himself tied to the mast to resist the Sirens. Pre-commit against temptation.","What did Odysseus do to resist the Sirens?","Tied himself to the mast.","Remove a temptation in advance."],["L","d","Homer","The Iliad","Achilles chose a short glorious life over a long obscure one; know what you value.","What choice did Achilles make?","Glory over long life.","Clarify what you value most."],["L","d","Homer","The Odyssey","Penelope's patient weaving shows loyalty and cunning in waiting.","What does Penelope's weaving show?","Patient loyalty and cleverness.","Be patient in a waiting period."],["L","d","Virgil","Aeneid","Perhaps one day it will please us to remember even these things.","What did Aeneas say of hardships?","One day we may enjoy remembering them.","View today's struggle as a future story."],["L","d","Virgil","Georgics","Relentless labour conquers all.","What conquers all, per Virgil?","Relentless work.","Work steadily on a hard job."],["L","w","Horace","Odes","Carpe diem: seize the day, trusting little in tomorrow.","What does carpe diem mean?","Seize the day.","Do something today you'd postpone."],["L","w","Horace","Epistles","He who has begun is half done; dare to be wise.","What did Horace say about beginning?","Begun is half done.","Begin something."],["L","w","Horace","Satires","The golden mean: avoid extremes of luxury and poverty.","What did Horace praise?","The golden mean.","Moderate one excess."],["L","d","Ovid","Metamorphoses","Dripping water hollows stone, not through force but persistence.","How does water hollow stone?","Through persistence.","Persist on one task."],["L","d","Ovid","Ars Amatoria","Be patient and tough; one day this pain will be useful to you.","What will pain become?","Useful.","Endure a discomfort with purpose."],["L","w","Publilius Syrus","Sentences","Anyone can hold the helm when the sea is calm.","When can anyone steer?","When the sea is calm.","Lead calmly in a crisis."],["L","w","Publilius Syrus","Sentences","A good reputation is more valuable than money.","What is worth more than money?","A good reputation.","Protect your reputation today."],["L","w","Publilius Syrus","Sentences","We are interested in others when they are interested in us.","When are we interested in others?","When they're interested in us.","Show interest in someone."],["L","d","Juvenal","Satires","A sound mind in a sound body.","What did Juvenal pray for?","A sound mind in a sound body.","Exercise and read today."],["L","w","Quintilian","Institutes of Oratory","Write quickly and you'll never write well; write well and you'll soon write quickly.","What did Quintilian say about writing?","Write well first; speed follows.","Do one task slowly and well."],["L","w","Tacitus","Agricola","They make a desert and call it peace. Beware false victories.","What did Calgacus say of Rome?","They make a desert and call it peace.","Check if a 'win' damaged a relationship."],["L","w","Pliny the Younger","Letters","An object in possession seldom retains the charm it had in pursuit.","What happens to objects once possessed?","They lose their charm.","Wait a week before buying something."],["L","w","Plutarch","On Tranquillity of Mind","Contentment comes from recognising what we have, not dwelling on others' good fortune.","What brings contentment, per Plutarch?","Recognising what we have.","Write what you're grateful for."],["L","w","Plutarch","How to Profit by One's Enemies","Enemies watch you closely, so they make you careful and better.","How can enemies help us?","They make us more careful and better.","Learn from a critic."],["L","w","Plutarch","On Listening","Listening well is the beginning of living well.","What is the beginning of living well?","Listening well.","Listen without interrupting."],["L","w","Plutarch","Parallel Lives","Alexander said he'd rather excel in knowledge than in power.","What did Alexander prefer to power?","Knowledge.","Spend time learning."],["L","w","Arrian","Anabasis of Alexander","Alexander shared his soldiers' hardships — he poured out water rather than drink alone.","What did Alexander do with the water?","Poured it out to share hardship.","Share a hardship with your team."],["L","w","Sallust","The Jugurthine War","Concord makes small states great; discord ruins great ones.","What makes small states great?","Concord.","Resolve a family or team conflict."],["L","w","Livy","History of Rome","Fortune favours the brave.","What does fortune favour?","The brave.","Take a brave step."],["L","w","Cicero","On Duties","The first duty is to see to it that one's actions harm no one.","What is the first duty?","Harm no one.","Check whether a plan harms anyone."],["L","w","Cicero","Letters","If you have a garden and a library, you have everything you need.","What do you need, per Cicero?","A garden and a library.","Spend time in a garden or with a book."],["L","w","Cicero","Philippics","Any man can make mistakes, but only a fool persists in error.","What does a fool do?","Persists in error.","Correct one ongoing mistake."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Temperance: eat not to dullness; drink not to elevation.","What is Franklin's first virtue?","Temperance.","Stop eating before you're full."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Silence: speak not but what may benefit others or yourself; avoid trifling talk.","What is Franklin's virtue of silence?","Speak only what benefits.","Avoid gossip today."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Order: let all your things have their places; let each part of business have its time.","What is the virtue of order?","Everything in its place and time.","Give one item a fixed place."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Resolution: resolve to perform what you ought; perform without fail what you resolve.","What is resolution?","Doing what you resolve.","Keep one resolution today."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Sincerity: use no hurtful deceit; think innocently and justly.","What is sincerity, per Franklin?","No hurtful deceit.","Be sincere in one compliment."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Tranquillity: be not disturbed at trifles or common accidents.","What is tranquillity?","Not being disturbed by trifles.","Stay calm about a minor accident."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Humility: imitate Jesus and Socrates.","How did Franklin define humility?","Imitate Jesus and Socrates.","Ask a question instead of asserting."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Autobiography","Franklin admitted he never fully acquired humility, but the attempt improved him.","What did Franklin say about humility?","He never mastered it but improved by trying.","Pursue a virtue without needing perfection."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","Well done is better than well said.","What is better than well said?","Well done.","Finish a task instead of talking about it."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","He that lies down with dogs shall rise up with fleas.","What does the dogs and fleas proverb mean?","Bad company affects you.","Reduce time with a negative influence."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","Hunger never saw bad bread. Need sharpens appreciation.","What does hunger do to appreciation?","It sharpens it.","Eat a plain meal with appreciation."],["L","d","Benjamin Franklin","Poor Richard's Almanack","By failing to prepare, you are preparing to fail (popular attribution).","What happens if you fail to prepare?","You prepare to fail.","Prepare tomorrow's clothes and bag tonight."],["L","w","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Private victories precede public victories: master yourself before leading others.","What precedes public victory?","Private victory.","Win one private battle today."],["L","w","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Write a personal mission statement as your constitution for decisions.","What is a personal mission statement?","A personal constitution for life.","Draft a three-sentence mission statement."],["L","w","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Identify your roles — parent, partner, friend — and set weekly goals for each.","How does Covey plan weekly?","By roles and goals.","Set one goal per role this week."],["L","w","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Love is a verb. Love the feeling is a fruit of love the verb.","What did Covey say love is?","A verb.","Do a loving act regardless of feeling."],["L","w","Stephen Covey","The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People","Reactive language — 'I have to', 'They make me' — gives away power. Use proactive language.","What does reactive language do?","Gives away power.","Replace 'I have to' with 'I choose to'."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Smile. A smile says 'I like you, you make me happy, I'm glad to see you.'","What does a smile communicate?","Liking and gladness.","Smile at everyone you meet."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Be a good listener; encourage others to talk about themselves.","How can you be a good conversationalist?","Be a good listener.","Ask someone about their passion."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Make the other person feel important — and do it sincerely.","What do people crave?","Feeling important.","Make someone feel important sincerely."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Win Friends and Influence People","Try honestly to see things from the other person's point of view.","What should you try to see?","Others' point of view.","Imagine a conflict from the other side."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Count your blessings, not your troubles.","What should you count?","Blessings.","Count ten blessings."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Don't try to get even with enemies; it hurts you more than them.","Why not get even?","It hurts you more.","Let go of a desire for revenge."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Expect ingratitude; give for the joy of giving.","What should we expect from people?","Ingratitude.","Give without expecting thanks."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","If you have a lemon, make lemonade.","What is the lemonade principle?","Turn misfortune into advantage.","Find an advantage in a setback."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Don't saw sawdust: what's past is past.","What is 'sawing sawdust'?","Dwelling on the past.","Stop replaying a past event."],["L","w","Dale Carnegie","How to Stop Worrying and Start Living","Rest before you get tired; fatigue breeds worry.","When should you rest?","Before getting tired.","Take a short break before fatigue."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Prisoners who lost faith in the future were doomed; hope kept others alive.","What doomed prisoners?","Losing faith in the future.","Write one thing you look forward to."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Frankl survived partly by imagining lecturing about the camps after the war.","What mental technique did Frankl use?","Imagining a meaningful future.","Visualise a meaningful future moment."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Paradoxical intention: wishing for what you fear can dissolve anxiety.","What is paradoxical intention?","Intending the feared thing to reduce anxiety.","Laugh at a small fear."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Dereflection: stop overthinking yourself; turn attention outward.","What is dereflection?","Redirecting attention away from oneself.","Focus on someone else when anxious."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","The Death of Ivan Ilyich","Gerasim's simple, honest care comforted Ivan more than lies from family.","What comforted Ivan Ilyich?","Gerasim's honest care.","Offer someone honest, simple care."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Family Happiness","Romantic passion fades; a deeper, quieter love grows from shared life.","What replaced passion in Family Happiness?","A quieter, deeper love.","Appreciate the quiet side of a relationship."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Resurrection","Nekhlyudov sought redemption by righting a past wrong.","What did Nekhlyudov try to do?","Right a past wrong.","Make amends for a past wrong."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Hadji Murat","A crushed thistle that still stands inspired the story — a symbol of stubborn vitality.","What image opens Hadji Murat?","A crushed thistle still standing.","Persist despite being knocked down."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","The Kreutzer Sonata","Unexamined passions can destroy; know your inner drives.","What does the Kreutzer Sonata warn of?","Destructive unexamined passions.","Reflect on a strong urge."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Writings on Civil Disobedience","Violence breeds violence; meet evil with nonresistance and good.","What did Tolstoy recommend against evil?","Nonresistance and good.","Respond to hostility without hostility."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","A Calendar of Wisdom","The best way to live is not to be idle; idleness breeds vice.","What did Tolstoy say about idleness?","It breeds vice.","Fill idle time with useful work."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Choices reveal character more than words.","What reveals character?","Choices.","Make one choice that shows who you are."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Generosity means giving the right amount, to the right person, at the right time.","What is true generosity?","Right amount, person, time.","Give thoughtfully today."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Magnanimity: the great-souled person claims much and deserves much, without arrogance.","What is magnanimity?","Greatness of soul with deserved self-respect.","Hold yourself to high standards."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Contemplation is the highest human activity — the life of the mind.","What is the highest activity?","Contemplation.","Spend 10 minutes thinking deeply."],["L","w","Aristotle","Eudemian Ethics","Truthfulness lies between boastfulness and false modesty.","What is truthfulness the mean between?","Boastfulness and false modesty.","Describe your work honestly."],["L","w","Aristotle","Politics","He who cannot be a good follower cannot be a good leader.","What must a leader first be?","A good follower.","Follow someone else's lead today."],["L","w","Aristotle","Rhetoric","We trust people of good character more readily on almost every subject.","Whom do we trust more readily?","People of good character.","Build credibility by keeping a promise."],["L","w","Aristotle","Physics","Nature does nothing in vain; everything has its purpose.","What does nature never do?","Anything in vain.","Find purpose in an ordinary task."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","If you are far from the enemy, make him believe you are near. Perception shapes reality.","What does Sun Tzu say about perception?","Shape the enemy's perception.","Present yourself confidently."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","The good fighter puts himself beyond defeat, then waits for the enemy's mistake.","What does the good fighter do first?","Makes himself invincible.","Secure your defences before taking risks."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","A leader leads by example, not by force.","How does a leader lead?","By example.","Model the behaviour you want."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Do not repeat the tactics that gained you one victory; vary your methods.","Why vary tactics?","Repeated tactics become predictable.","Try a new approach to an old problem."],["L","w","Sun Tzu","The Art of War","Anger may turn to gladness, but a kingdom destroyed can never be restored. Act coolly.","Why avoid acting in anger?","Its damage can't be undone.","Delay a decision made in anger."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","The Way is in training. Daily practice is the path.","Where is the Way?","In training.","Practise your craft daily."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","You must understand there is more than one path to the top of the mountain.","What did Musashi say about paths?","There are many paths.","Consider an alternative route."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Today is victory over yourself of yesterday; tomorrow victory over lesser men.","What is today's victory?","Over yesterday's self.","Beat yesterday's performance."],["L","d","Miyamoto Musashi","The Book of Five Rings","Fixed gaze sees little; soften your eyes to see widely.","What does a fixed gaze do?","Sees little.","Step back to see the big picture."],["L","d","Musashi","Dokkodo","Do not seek pleasure for its own sake; respect gods and Buddha without relying on them.","What should one not rely on, per Dokkodo?","Gods and Buddha.","Take responsibility rather than hoping for luck."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","What is the first business of philosophy? To part with self-conceit.","What is philosophy's first business?","Parting with self-conceit.","Drop one pretension."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Begin at once to live: count each day a separate life.","When should we begin to live?","At once.","Start one thing today."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Very little is needed to make a happy life; it's all within you.","How much is needed for happiness?","Very little.","Enjoy a simple pleasure."],["L","w","Epicurus","Vatican Sayings","Nothing is enough for the man to whom enough is too little.","What is never enough?","Anything for one who finds enough too little.","Define 'enough' for one desire."],["L","w","Samuel Johnson","The Rambler","Great works are performed not by strength but by perseverance.","How are great works performed?","By perseverance.","Persist on a long project."],["L","w","Samuel Johnson","Rasselas","The happiness of life is made up of minute fractions — little kindnesses.","What makes up life's happiness?","Small fractions, little kindnesses.","Do three small kindnesses."],["L","w","Samuel Johnson","Boswell's Life of Johnson","Integrity without knowledge is weak; knowledge without integrity is dangerous.","What is knowledge without integrity?","Dangerous.","Pair learning with honesty."],["L","w","Samuel Johnson","The Idler","The chains of habit are too weak to be felt until they are too strong to be broken.","What did Johnson say of habit's chains?","Weak until unbreakable.","Break a minor bad habit now."],["L","w","Samuel Johnson","Boswell's Life of Johnson","When a man is tired of London, he is tired of life — stay curious about the world.","What did Johnson link to tiredness of life?","Being tired of London.","Explore a new place nearby."],["L","w","Alexander Pope","An Essay on Criticism","To err is human; to forgive, divine.","What is divine, per Pope?","To forgive.","Forgive one error."],["L","w","Alexander Pope","An Essay on Criticism","A little learning is a dangerous thing; drink deep.","Why is a little learning dangerous?","It breeds false confidence.","Go deeper on one subject."],["L","w","John Milton","Paradise Lost","The mind is its own place, and can make a heaven of hell or a hell of heaven.","What can the mind make?","Heaven of hell, hell of heaven.","Reframe a bad situation."],["L","w","John Donne","Meditation XVII","No man is an island; we are all part of the main.","What did Donne say no man is?","An island.","Reach out to someone isolated."],["L","w","Francis Bacon","Essays","Reading makes a full man; conference a ready man; writing an exact man.","What does writing make, per Bacon?","An exact man.","Write a short summary of a card."],["L","w","Francis Bacon","Essays","Knowledge is power.","What did Bacon say knowledge is?","Power.","Learn one useful fact."],["L","w","Francis Bacon","Essays","Some books are to be tasted, others swallowed, and some few chewed and digested.","How should some books be treated?","Chewed and digested.","Reread a key passage slowly."],["L","w","William Shakespeare","Hamlet","This above all: to thine own self be true.","What does Polonius advise above all?","Be true to yourself.","Make one choice true to yourself."],["L","w","William Shakespeare","Hamlet","There is nothing either good or bad, but thinking makes it so.","What makes things good or bad, per Hamlet?","Thinking.","Reinterpret one event."],["L","w","William Shakespeare","Julius Caesar","Cowards die many times before their deaths; the valiant taste death but once.","How often do cowards die?","Many times.","Act despite fear."],["L","w","William Shakespeare","Measure for Measure","Our doubts are traitors and make us lose the good we might win by fearing to attempt.","What are our doubts?","Traitors.","Attempt something despite doubts."],["L","w","William Shakespeare","As You Like It","All the world's a stage; each plays many parts.","What is the world, per Shakespeare?","A stage.","Play your part well today."],["L","w","William Shakespeare","King Lear","Lear learned too late to value honest love over flattery.","What did Lear fail to value?","Honest love over flattery.","Appreciate honest people."],["L","w","Cervantes","Don Quixote","Too much sanity may be madness — and to see life as it is, not as it should be.","What did Quixote say may be madness?","Too much sanity.","Hold onto an ideal."],["L","w","Cervantes","Don Quixote","The road is always better than the inn.","What is better than the inn?","The road.","Enjoy the journey of a project."],["L","w","Dante","Divine Comedy","In the middle of life's journey, I found myself in a dark wood. Every life has dark woods.","Where did Dante find himself?","A dark wood.","Seek guidance in a confused moment."],["L","w","Dante","Divine Comedy","The hottest places in hell are reserved for those who stay neutral in moral crisis (popular paraphrase).","What does the Dante paraphrase warn against?","Neutrality in moral crises.","Take a stand on something right."],["L","w","Goethe","Faust","Whatever you can do or dream you can, begin it (attributed).","What does the Goethe-attributed line urge?","Begin.","Begin a dream project."],["L","w","Goethe","Maxims and Reflections","Knowing is not enough; we must apply. Willing is not enough; we must do.","What is not enough, per Goethe?","Knowing and willing.","Apply one idea today."],["L","w","Goethe","Wilhelm Meister","One ought every day to hear a song, read a poem, see a picture and speak a few sensible words.","What should we do daily, per Goethe?","Song, poem, picture, sensible words.","Hear a song and read a poem."],["L","w","Goethe","Maxims and Reflections","Treat people as if they were what they ought to be and you help them become it.","How should we treat people, per Goethe?","As what they ought to be.","Treat someone as their best self."],["L","w","Schiller","Letters on Aesthetic Education","Man is only fully human when he plays.","When is man fully human, per Schiller?","When he plays.","Play for 20 minutes."],["L","w","Novalis","Fragments","Character is a completely fashioned will.","What is character?","A fully fashioned will.","Exercise your will on one decision."],["L","w","Alexis de Tocqueville","Democracy in America","Americans form associations for everything; civic life strengthens freedom.","What strengthens freedom, per Tocqueville?","Associations and civic life.","Join a local group."],["L","w","Edmund Burke","Reflections","The only thing necessary for evil to triumph is for good men to do nothing (attributed).","What lets evil triumph?","Good people doing nothing.","Speak up about one wrong."],["L","w","Thomas Paine","The American Crisis","These are the times that try men's souls; the harder the conflict, the more glorious the triumph.","What did Paine say about conflict?","The harder, the more glorious the triumph.","Push through a difficult moment."],["L","w","Thomas Jefferson","Letters","I find that the harder I work, the more luck I seem to have (attributed).","What creates luck, per the saying?","Hard work.","Put in extra effort today."],["L","w","John Adams","Letters","Facts are stubborn things; wishes can't alter evidence.","What did Adams say facts are?","Stubborn things.","Check facts before forming an opinion."],["L","w","George Washington","Rules of Civility","Labour to keep alive in your breast that little spark of celestial fire called conscience.","What did Washington call conscience?","A spark of celestial fire.","Follow your conscience."],["L","w","George Washington","Rules of Civility","Associate with men of good quality; it's better to be alone than in bad company.","What is better than bad company?","Being alone.","Choose good company."],["L","d","Florence Nightingale","Notes on Nursing","I attribute my success to this: I never gave or took any excuse.","To what did Nightingale attribute success?","Never giving or taking excuses.","Drop one excuse."],["L","w","Charles Darwin","The Descent of Man","In the long history of humankind, those who learned to collaborate prevailed (paraphrase).","What prevailed, per the Darwin paraphrase?","Collaboration.","Collaborate on a task."],["L","w","Charles Darwin","Letters","A man who dares to waste one hour has not discovered the value of life.","What does wasting an hour reveal?","Not knowing life's value.","Use one hour wisely."],["L","w","Louis Pasteur","Lectures","Chance favours the prepared mind.","Whom does chance favour?","The prepared mind.","Prepare for a hoped-for opportunity."],["L","w","Thomas Edison","Interviews","I have not failed; I've found 10,000 ways that won't work.","How did Edison view failure?","As finding ways that don't work.","Treat a failure as data."],["L","w","Thomas Edison","Interviews","Opportunity is missed because it is dressed in overalls and looks like work.","Why is opportunity missed?","It looks like work.","Do the hard work in front of you."],["L","w","Nikola Tesla","My Inventions","Tesla visualised machines fully in his mind before building them.","What did Tesla do before building?","Visualised fully.","Visualise a task before starting."],["L","w","Richard Feynman","Surely You're Joking, Mr. Feynman!","The first principle is that you must not fool yourself — and you are the easiest person to fool.","Who is easiest to fool?","Yourself.","Test one belief honestly."],["L","w","Richard Feynman","The Pleasure of Finding Things Out","Knowing the name of something is different from knowing something.","What is the difference Feynman stressed?","Naming vs understanding.","Explain a concept simply."],["L","w","Carl Sagan","Pale Blue Dot","Earth seen from afar is a pale blue dot; be kinder to one another.","What does the pale blue dot teach?","Humility and kindness.","Be kind to someone today."],["L","w","Carl Jung","Memories, Dreams, Reflections","What you resist persists; integrate your shadow.","What is Jung's shadow?","Unacknowledged parts of ourselves.","Acknowledge one trait you dislike in yourself."],["L","w","Carl Jung","Modern Man in Search of a Soul","Who looks outside dreams; who looks inside awakes.","Who awakes, per Jung?","Who looks inside.","Reflect inwardly for 10 minutes."],["L","w","Sigmund Freud","Civilisation and Its Discontents","Love and work are the cornerstones of our humanness (attributed).","What are the cornerstones of humanness?","Love and work.","Invest in both love and work today."],["L","w","Abraham Maslow","Motivation and Personality","What a man can be, he must be — self-actualisation.","What is self-actualisation?","Becoming what one can be.","Do something toward your potential."],["L","w","Abraham Maslow","The Psychology of Science","If all you have is a hammer, everything looks like a nail.","What is Maslow's hammer?","Over-relying on one tool.","Try a different approach."],["L","w","Carl Rogers","On Becoming a Person","The curious paradox: when I accept myself as I am, then I can change.","What is Rogers' paradox?","Self-acceptance enables change.","Accept one flaw kindly."],["L","w","Erik Erikson","Childhood and Society","Each life stage has its task; in later life, generativity — caring for the next generation.","What is generativity?","Caring for the next generation.","Mentor a younger person."],["L","w","Daniel Goleman","Emotional Intelligence","Self-awareness — recognising your feelings as they happen — is the keystone of emotional intelligence.","What is the keystone of EQ?","Self-awareness.","Name your emotion three times today."],["L","w","Daniel Goleman","Emotional Intelligence","Empathy is built by listening and reading nonverbal cues.","How is empathy built?","Listening and reading nonverbal cues.","Watch body language in a conversation."],["L","w","Susan Cain","Quiet","Introverts recharge alone; quiet strength is real strength.","How do introverts recharge?","Alone.","Schedule quiet recharge time."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Yes to Life","Life retains meaning under all circumstances, even suffering.","What did Frankl affirm in Yes to Life?","Life always has meaning.","Say yes to life in a hard moment."],["L","w","Stephen Covey","Principle-Centered Leadership","Principles are like a compass: they always point true north.","What are principles like?","A compass.","Check a decision against your principles."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Practise in small things first: a spilled oil, a stolen wine. 'That is the price of tranquillity.'","What is the price of tranquillity?","Small losses accepted calmly.","Accept a small loss calmly."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","The soul becomes dyed with the colour of its thoughts.","What colours the soul?","Its thoughts.","Choose uplifting thoughts."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Even after a good meal, the Stoic keeps rehearsing simple living, so luck never owns him.","Why rehearse simplicity in good times?","So fortune never controls you.","Choose a simple option amid plenty."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Shared joy is doubled; teaching something to others is the best way to learn it.","Why share what you learn?","Teaching deepens learning.","Teach one card to someone today."],["L","d","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic","Old age is full of pleasure if you know how to use it.","What did Seneca say of old age?","It's full of pleasure if used well.","Plan a good habit for later life."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Take care not to be 'Caesarified' — dyed purple by power. Stay simple, good and pure.","What did Marcus warn against?","Being corrupted by power.","Stay humble in a position of authority."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Look beneath the surface; let neither the quality nor value of anything escape you.","What should not escape you?","The true quality of things.","Look beyond appearances once."],["L","d","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations","Remember: you are a small part of the whole, here a short time.","What should we remember about our place?","We're a small part of the whole.","Contribute to something larger."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","Every habit is strengthened by corresponding actions: walking by walking, running by running.","How is a habit strengthened?","By corresponding actions.","Repeat a desired action."],["L","d","Epictetus","Discourses","If you would cure anger, don't feed the habit; count the days you weren't angry.","How did Epictetus suggest curing anger?","Count days without anger.","Start counting anger-free days."],["L","d","Epictetus","Enchiridion","Keep death and exile before your eyes daily; you'll never have a base thought.","What should we keep in mind daily?","Death and exile.","Reflect briefly on impermanence."],["L","d","Viktor Frankl","Man's Search for Meaning","Frankl's idea: the 'tragic triad' of pain, guilt and death can become sources of growth.","What is the tragic triad?","Pain, guilt, death.","Turn one guilt into a lesson."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","War and Peace","Natasha's joy in dancing shows the beauty of spontaneous life.","What does Natasha's dance show?","Spontaneous joy.","Do something spontaneous and joyful."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","War and Peace","Platon Karataev lived simply and kindly, accepting all — a model of peace.","What did Karataev teach Pierre?","Simple acceptance and kindness.","Accept a situation with good grace."],["L","w","Leo Tolstoy","Anna Karenina","Anna's tragedy shows how isolation and passion without grounding destroy.","What destroyed Anna?","Isolation and ungrounded passion.","Stay connected to people who ground you."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","Shame is the fear of disrepute; it is fitting for the young, who make mistakes.","What is shame, per Aristotle?","Fear of disrepute.","Learn from a mistake without self-hatred."],["L","w","Aristotle","Nicomachean Ethics","We deliberate about means, not ends. Clarify the end, then choose means wisely.","What do we deliberate about?","Means, not ends.","Clarify the goal before choosing tactics."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","When it is obvious goals can't be reached, don't adjust the goals; adjust the action steps.","What should be adjusted when goals seem unreachable?","The action steps.","Revise your plan, not your goal."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","The cautious seldom err.","Who seldom errs?","The cautious.","Double-check one important thing."],["L","w","Confucius","Analects","Wherever you go, go with all your heart.","How should you go wherever you go?","With all your heart.","Put your heart into a task."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Be content with what you have; rejoice in the way things are.","What does Lao Tzu advise?","Contentment.","Rejoice in the present."],["L","w","Lao Tzu","Tao Te Ching","Kindness in words creates confidence; kindness in thinking creates profoundness.","What does kindness in thinking create?","Profoundness.","Think kindly about someone."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Speak only endearing speech, speech that is welcomed.","What speech should we use?","Endearing, welcomed speech.","Speak kindly all day."],["L","w","Buddha","Dhammapada","Even death is not to be feared by one who has lived wisely.","Who need not fear death?","One who has lived wisely.","Live one day wisely."],["L","w","Buddha","Anguttara Nikaya","Generosity, kind speech, helpful action and impartiality bind people together.","What binds people together?","Generosity, kind speech, help, impartiality.","Practise all four today."],["L","w","Thich Nhat Hanh","The Art of Living","Walk as if you are kissing the earth with your feet.","How should we walk?","Mindfully, kissing the earth.","Take a slow mindful walk."],["L","w","Pema Chödrön","When Things Fall Apart","Things falling apart is a kind of testing and also a kind of healing.","What is falling apart, per Chödrön?","Testing and healing.","Lean into a difficulty."],["L","w","Pema Chödrön","The Places That Scare You","Lean into the sharp points; discomfort is where growth happens.","What does leaning into sharp points mean?","Facing discomfort to grow.","Stay with a discomfort a little longer."],["L","w","Jon Kabat-Zinn","Wherever You Go, There You Are","You can't stop the waves, but you can learn to surf.","What can you learn instead of stopping waves?","To surf.","Ride out one stressful moment."],["L","w","Ram Dass","Be Here Now","Be here now: presence is the gateway to peace.","What is the gateway to peace?","Presence.","Put your phone away for an hour."],["L","w","Wayne Dyer","Your Erroneous Zones","Guilt and worry are useless emotions; they immobilise you in the present.","What two emotions did Dyer call useless?","Guilt and worry.","Replace worry with action."],["L","w","Norman Vincent Peale","The Power of Positive Thinking","Believe in yourself; humble, reasonable confidence leads to success.","What does Peale urge?","Belief in yourself.","Affirm a strength."],["L","w","Maxwell Maltz","Psycho-Cybernetics","Your self-image sets the limits of your achievement; change it and you change your results.","What sets the limits of achievement?","Self-image.","Visualise yourself succeeding."],["L","w","Dorothea Brande","Wake Up and Live!","Act as if it were impossible to fail.","What does Brande advise?","Act as if failure were impossible.","Take action boldly."],["L","w","Ralph Waldo Emerson","Self-Reliance","Do not go where the path may lead; go where there is no path and leave a trail.","What did Emerson say about paths?","Make your own trail.","Try something original."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","Things do not change; we change.","What changes, per Thoreau?","We do.","Notice how your perception shifted."],["L","w","Henry David Thoreau","Walden","Wealth is the ability to fully experience life.","What is wealth, per Thoreau?","Ability to fully experience life.","Experience something fully today."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","There is no knowledge so hard to acquire as the knowledge of how to live this life well.","What is the hardest knowledge?","How to live well.","Reflect on living well."],["L","w","Michel de Montaigne","Essays","A man who fears suffering already suffers what he fears.","What does fear of suffering cause?","Suffering itself.","Let go of anticipatory fear."],["L","w","Blaise Pascal","Pensées","Kind words do not cost much, yet they accomplish much.","What do kind words do?","Accomplish much at little cost.","Say three kind words."],["L","w","Søren Kierkegaard","Purity of Heart","Purity of heart is to will one thing.","What is purity of heart?","To will one thing.","Focus on one aim today."],["L","w","Fyodor Dostoevsky","The Brothers Karamazov","Love all creation, every leaf and ray of light, and you'll perceive the divine mystery.","What should we love, per Zosima?","All creation.","Notice beauty in nature."],["L","w","Hermann Hesse","Demian","Each person's life is a road toward himself.","What is each life, per Hesse?","A road toward oneself.","Take one step toward your true self."],["L","w","Hermann Hesse","Steppenwolf","Learn to laugh at yourself and your seriousness.","What did Steppenwolf learn?","To laugh at himself.","Laugh at your own mistake."],["L","w","Rainer Maria Rilke","Letters to a Young Poet","Perhaps everything terrible is something helpless that wants our love.","What might terrible things be?","Helpless things wanting love.","Show compassion to a difficult person."],["L","w","Rabindranath Tagore","Stray Birds","You can't cross the sea merely by standing and staring at the water.","How can you cross the sea?","Not by staring — by acting.","Take action on a goal."],["L","w","Rabindranath Tagore","Gitanjali","I slept and dreamt life was joy; I woke and saw life was service; I acted and service was joy.","What did Tagore find service to be?","Joy.","Serve someone today."],["L","w","Khalil Gibran","Sand and Foam","Generosity is giving more than you can; pride is taking less than you need.","What is generosity, per Gibran?","Giving more than you can.","Give generously."],["L","w","Lin Yutang","The Importance of Living","The wise man is not busy; leisure is an art of living.","What is the wise man not?","Busy.","Enjoy idle time."],["L","w","Okakura Kakuzō","The Book of Tea","Teaism is the adoration of the beautiful among sordid daily facts.","What is Teaism?","Adoring beauty in daily life.","Make a cup of tea mindfully."],["L","w","Yoshida Kenkō","Essays in Idleness","Beauty lies in impermanence; if things lasted forever they'd lose their poignancy.","Where does beauty lie, per Kenkō?","In impermanence.","Appreciate something fleeting."],["L","w","Kamo no Chōmei","Hōjōki","The flowing river never stops, and yet the water never stays the same.","What image opens the Hōjōki?","The flowing river.","Accept change today."],["L","w","Matsuo Bashō","The Narrow Road to the Deep North","Every day is a journey, and the journey itself home.","What is home, per Bashō?","The journey itself.","Treat today as a journey."],["L","w","Sen no Rikyū","Teachings","Wabi-sabi: find beauty in the imperfect and simple.","What is wabi-sabi?","Beauty in imperfection.","Appreciate something imperfect."],["L","d","Ikigai (García & Miralles)","Ikigai","Ikigai lies where what you love, what you're good at, what the world needs and what pays meet.","What four things meet in ikigai?","Love, skill, need, pay.","Map your ikigai."],["L","d","Ikigai (García & Miralles)","Ikigai","Okinawans eat until 80% full (hara hachi bu).","What is hara hachi bu?","Eating until 80% full.","Stop eating at 80% full."],["L","d","Robin Sharma","The Monk Who Sold His Ferrari","Rise early, reflect and set intentions — the 'heart of the rose' practice for focus.","What did the monk recommend in the morning?","Rising early and reflecting.","Wake 20 minutes earlier tomorrow."],["L","d","Robin Sharma","The 5AM Club","Own your morning, elevate your life: the 20/20/20 formula — move, reflect, grow.","What is the 20/20/20 formula?","Move, reflect, grow in the first hour.","Try 20 minutes each of move, reflect, grow."],["L","d","Hal Elrod","The Miracle Morning","SAVERS: silence, affirmations, visualisation, exercise, reading, scribing.","What does SAVERS stand for?","Silence, affirmations, visualisation, exercise, reading, scribing.","Do one SAVERS practice tomorrow morning."],["L","d","Arnold Bennett","How to Live on 24 Hours a Day","You get 24 fresh hours daily; spend the spare 90 minutes on improving your mind.","What did Bennett suggest doing with spare time?","Improving the mind.","Use 90 minutes to learn tonight."],["L","d","Arnold Bennett","How to Live on 24 Hours a Day","Don't try to do too much at first; start with a small, manageable plan.","How should you start using your time better?","Small and manageable.","Start a 15-minute study habit."],["L","w","Wallace Wattles","The Science of Getting Rich","Gratitude keeps you connected to the source of good; be grateful.","What does Wattles emphasise?","Gratitude.","Write three gratitudes."]];
const CARDS=RAW_CARDS.map((c,i)=>({id:'c'+(i+1),cat:c[0]==='B'?'Business':'Life',stat:{m:'wealth',d:'discipline',w:'wisdom'}[c[1]],author:c[2],book:c[3],text:c[4],q:c[5],a:c[6],action:c[7]}));
